// Generated by scripts/build-docs.mjs from docs/*.md. Do not edit by hand.
window.DAYHAND_DOCS_INDEX = [{"p":"Dayhand documentation","t":"Dayhand documentation","u":"index.html","x":"Dayhand is a task list and day plan for people who run a business on their own, and for the AI agents that work for them. The plan lives on your phone, your Mac and your Linux desktop and works offline there; a server you run yourself keeps the copies in step and lets your agents add, plan and finish tasks over MCP or the dayhand command line. Every change says who made it. All of it is free and open source under the Apache License 2.0: the apps, the command line, the server and these docs. There is nothing to buy and no account to create. These pages are published at dayhand.dev/docs and kept in the repository as docs/*.md. They describe what is in the main branch, not a published release. dayhand version shows the CLI and server versions; each app shows its version under About."},{"p":"Dayhand documentation","t":"What Dayhand is made of","u":"index.html#what-dayhand-is-made-of","x":"Android app ─┐ ┌─ /mcp MCP for agents and dayhand macOS app ───┤ each keeps its own copy and an │ Linux app ───┤ outbox of changes, works offline ──> │ /api/v1 REST API, webhooks, import │ │ dayhand ─────┤ command line and dayhand ui ───────> │ /auth phone pairing and app sign-in AI agents ───┘ MCP, or dayhand with --json ───────> │ /cal read-only calendar feed └─ your own Dayhand server (Convex) The plan. Tasks with a day and an optional time, repeats, checklists, labels, links, reminders and a note. One task can be the now task, with a note of where you stopped. Today shows what is overdue, the now task, today and what you finished today; the board has three columns (to do, in progress, done); deleted tasks wait 30 days in the trash. Every task has a number (#42) that is never given to another task. The apps. Android, macOS and Linux each keep the plan on the device, work offline and queue changes to send to the server in order when it can be reached. The command line (dayhand) and terminal UI (dayhand ui) need the server; they do not keep an offline plan or queue changes. The server. There is no hosted Dayhand service. You run the backend yourself: on your computer with one command, or on your own free Convex account (your own server). The Android, macOS and Linux apps also work with no server at all. The agents. Any MCP client, or any agent that runs shell commands, works on the same plan with its own key. Its changes are signed agent: <name>. Tasks can carry acceptance criteria and a plan, agents claim tasks for a while and finish them with proof (agents). You in charge. Agents ask before risky steps and you answer on the phone, one switch pauses every agent, a policy per key allows, asks for or denies each tool, work nobody asked for waits in an inbox, and a health report shows what needs a look."},{"p":"Dayhand documentation","t":"Where to start","u":"index.html#where-to-start","x":"You want to Read Try Dayhand on one computer in ten minutes Quick start Install an app or the command line Install Use the phone app Android Use the Mac or Linux app macOS, Linux and Omarchy Work in the terminal Terminal: dayhand ui, command reference Let an AI agent work on your plan Agents and you, MCP endpoint Connect the Claude apps Claude apps (OAuth) Run, update and back up your server Your own server Connect another tool REST API, webhooks, calendar feed Know what is stored where and who can read it Security and privacy Fix something that does not work FAQ and troubleshooting Change Dayhand itself Contributing, architecture"},{"p":"Dayhand documentation","t":"What exists and what is planned","u":"index.html#what-exists-and-what-is-planned","x":"Everything in these pages can be built and run from the source today. Nothing is published as a package or a signed download yet: Piece Today Planned dayhand CLI and dayhand ui build from source (macOS, Linux, Windows) npm and Homebrew, coming soon Android app build it yourself a signed APK on GitHub Releases and Google Play, coming soon Dayhand for macOS build from source, runs on the Mac that built it a signed, notarized download with updates, coming soon Dayhand for Linux Arch Linux and Omarchy package built from the repository; CMake elsewhere no AUR package yet iPhone and iPad in development; nothing to install yet an iOS app on the same backend Windows desktop app none; the CLI works on Windows with a server on Convex Backlog.md import and export not in main planned Pages that describe a planned piece say so where it matters, and give no command for it."},{"p":"Dayhand documentation","t":"Help and feedback","u":"index.html#help-and-feedback","x":"Dayhand is a personal open source project (Apache-2.0) with no hosted service and no support promise. Issues on GitHub are for bugs you can reproduce and for feature ideas. Security problems go privately, never in an issue: see security. The project was called Helm until backend 0.9.0 and CLI 0.10.0, and some old names keep working with a warning (renamed from Helm). The repository moved from maiarowsky/helm to maiarowsky/dayhand; the old addresses redirect."},{"p":"Quick start","t":"Quick start","u":"getting-started.html","x":"This page takes you from nothing to a running Dayhand on one computer: your own server, your first tasks from the command line, the plan full screen in the terminal, the phone connected, and an AI agent working on the plan with a key of its own. It takes about ten minutes, most of it the first download. You need Node.js 22.12 or newer and git. On macOS and Linux, steps 2–6 use the same local server and plan. On Windows, use dayhand server deploy in step 2 and keep that Convex server for the whole walkthrough; the local server is for macOS and Linux only. The shell examples use a POSIX shell (for example, Git Bash on Windows)."},{"p":"Quick start","t":"1. Build the command line","u":"getting-started.html#1-build-the-command-line","x":"No package is published yet, so build dayhand from the repository: git clone dayhand cd dayhand/cli && npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run build && npm link && cd .. npm link puts dayhand and its short name dh on your PATH; npm unlink -g dayhand removes them. dayhand --version prints the version. Other ways to install, and the state of each: Install."},{"p":"Quick start","t":"2. Start your own server","u":"getting-started.html#2-start-your-own-server","x":"dayhand server start The first run downloads the Convex local backend once (a pinned version whose checksum is checked), deploys Dayhand to it, creates a key for dayhand and signs this computer in with it, and prints a pairing code for the phone. Data and keys stay in a private folder on this computer; no account is needed. The server listens on 127.0.0.1 only and runs until you press Ctrl+C. The same command starts it again later, with its data. Leave it running and open a second terminal in the same folder for the next steps. In that second terminal, select the local server explicitly: export DAYHAND_URL= On Windows, run this instead of dayhand server start (you can also choose this from the start on macOS or Linux): dayhand server deploy export DAYHAND_URL= Setup opens your browser to sign in to your own Convex account, creates the server and signs dayhand in. Replace <deployment> with the name in the HTTPS address it prints. The command finishes; there is no local server process to leave running. Use this URL throughout the next steps. DAYHAND_URL selects the server in this terminal even if an existing profile points elsewhere; dayhand whoami checks the URL and sign-in. Set it again in any new terminal you use for this plan. To see every setup step first without changing anything, add --dry-run to server start (or server deploy on Windows). More about either backend: Your own server."},{"p":"Quick start","t":"3. Plan your day","u":"getting-started.html#3-plan-your-day","x":"dayhand add \"Call the accountant\" --due \"today 14:00\" -l admin dayhand add \"Send the invoice to Anna\" --due fri dayhand dayhand done 1 dayhand alone shows Today: the task you are working on now, what is overdue, what is due today and what you finished today. Tasks are referred to by their number (1 or #1). Dates are English phrases read in your time zone: fri 2pm, tomorrow 9am, in 3 days, 23 oct, 2026-11-02. Try one without changing anything: dayhand when \"next mon 9am\" The time zone comes from the server's settings, Europe/London until you change it: dayhand settings set timezone=Europe/Warsaw A few more things to try: dayhand list (every open task), dayhand board, dayhand now 2 --note \"waiting for the bank statement\" (the task you are on, and where you stopped), dayhand show 2 (a task with its history), dayhand rm 2 and dayhand restore 2. Every command and flag: command reference."},{"p":"Quick start","t":"4. See it full screen","u":"getting-started.html#4-see-it-full-screen","x":"dayhand ui Today, the board, the week and the trash in the terminal, with keys like vim (j/k, h/l), a to add a task with a date phrase, ? for every key and q to quit: Terminal."},{"p":"Quick start","t":"5. Connect your phone","u":"getting-started.html#5-connect-your-phone","x":"The Android app works without any server (On this phone only). To share the plan between the phone, this computer and your agents, connect it to your server. No APK is published yet, so for now you build the app yourself (Install). A phone cannot reach a server that listens on 127.0.0.1, and release builds of the app talk to their server over HTTPS only. For the local server from step 2, keep the same server and plan. First stop the running server with Ctrl+C in its terminal. Follow the Tailscale steps to give both server ports HTTPS addresses inside your own Tailscale network, then restart it in that terminal: dayhand server start --public-url This reuses the same local data and keys; it does not create a new plan. In the second terminal, select the HTTPS address for pairing and all the remaining commands: export DAYHAND_URL= dayhand whoami The phone syncs while the computer is on and in the tailnet. If you started on Convex in step 2 (including on Windows), skip Tailscale and keep your URL: it is already reachable from the phone. Optional: switch from local to Convex. A new Convex deployment is a separate server with its own empty plan, not a move of the local database. You can simply start there in step 2 instead. To carry your local tasks over now, export while DAYHAND_URL still points at the local server, then deploy, select the new address and import: dayhand export --format json -o dayhand.json dayhand server deploy export DAYHAND_URL= dayhand whoami dayhand import dayhand.json Replace <deployment> with the name setup prints. Deploy stores a key for that address but does not overwrite an existing profile; setting DAYHAND_URL above is what points dayhand at the new server for the import, pairing and agent setup below. The local plan is unchanged; no automatic migration occurs. The exported file contains private plan data, so keep it out of shared folders. More: Convex setup. Then, in the app: Settings → Server → Connect to my server (or Connect to my server on the first start), and scan the QR code that this prints, or type the address and the code: dayhand pair The code works once, for 10 minutes. Tasks you added on the phone before are sent to the server. dayhand devices lists paired phones and dayhand revoke <deviceId> signs one out. How pairing works: Phone pairing."},{"p":"Quick start","t":"6. Let an agent work on the plan","u":"getting-started.html#6-let-an-agent-work-on-the-plan","x":"Give every agent its own key. Its changes are then signed agent: <name>, and you can revoke it alone: AGENT_KEY=$(dayhand key create claude --scope write --json | jq -r .key) DAYHAND_API_KEY=$AGENT_KEY dayhand add \"Draft the VAT reply\" --due \"today 16:00\" --remind 30 dayhand log --by agent The key is printed once; keep it where the agent reads it. jq extracts it from the JSON here. The agent can use dayhand with that key, or the MCP endpoint of the same selected server. For Claude Code in this terminal: claude mcp add --transport http dayhand \"$DAYHAND_URL/mcp\" --header \"Authorization: Bearer $AGENT_KEY\" The MCP address is before Tailscale, or after it. On a Convex server (including the Windows route), it is Then ask it what is on your list today. Acceptance criteria, claims, approvals on the phone, the switch that pauses every agent and the rest: Agents and you."},{"p":"Quick start","t":"Next","u":"getting-started.html#next","x":"Keep the server: backups, updates and costs. Use the apps: Android, macOS, Linux. See your tasks in Google or Apple Calendar: calendar feed. Something did not work: FAQ and troubleshooting."},{"p":"Install","t":"Install","u":"install.html","x":"No Dayhand release is published yet: there is no package on npm or Homebrew, no APK on GitHub Releases and no signed Mac download. Everything below builds from the source today, and each section says what is coming. A piece marked coming soon has no command on this page, because none works yet. Piece Runs on Today Coming soon Command line (dayhand, dh, dayhand ui) macOS, Linux, Windows; Node.js 22.12+ from source npm, Homebrew Your own server macOS and Linux (on this computer), or your Convex account dayhand server start or dayhand server deploy Android Android 8.0 or newer build it yourself signed APK on GitHub Releases, Google Play macOS macOS 13 or newer; built with Xcode 26 from source signed, notarized download Linux Qt 6 desktops; a package for Arch Linux and Omarchy from source The iPhone and iPad app is in development and has nothing to install yet. There is no Windows desktop app; on Windows the command line works with a server on your own Convex account."},{"p":"Install","t":"Command line: dayhand","u":"install.html#command-line-dayhand","x":"You need Node.js 22.12 or newer and git, on macOS, Linux or Windows. git clone dayhand cd dayhand/cli && npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run build && npm link && cd .. npm link puts dayhand and dh (the same command, shorter) on your PATH. Where that needs administrator rights, run node cli/dist/bin/dayhand.js from the checkout instead. npm unlink -g dayhand removes the link. On Debian and Ubuntu, debhelper has a dh command of its own; type dayhand there. --ignore-scripts keeps the install scripts of dependencies from running. The packages come from the lockfile. Update: git pull, then cd cli && npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run build. dayhand version shows the CLI and server versions and whether they work together. Shell completion for bash, zsh, fish and PowerShell: dayhand help completion. Manual pages: man/dayhand.1 and man/dh.1 in the checkout (man ./man/dayhand.1). A package installed from npm will carry them, so that man dayhand works. Next, sign dayhand in to a server. dayhand server start and dayhand server deploy do it by themselves; for a server that already runs, dayhand login --url asks for a key (command reference). Coming soon: the dayhand package on npm and a Homebrew tap. The publishing workflow and the formula are prepared in the repository, but nothing is published."},{"p":"Install","t":"Your own server","u":"install.html#your-own-server","x":"The server is the backend/ folder of the repository, deployed to Convex. The command line sets it up; there is nothing else to install: On this computer (macOS or Linux, x64 or arm64): dayhand server start downloads the Convex local backend once and runs your server until Ctrl+C. No account needed. On your own free Convex account (from macOS, Linux or Windows): dayhand server deploy logs in to Convex in the browser and deploys. The server gets a public HTTPS address. Both are described step by step, with costs, backups and updates, in Your own server. The apps also work with no server at all, each on its own device."},{"p":"Install","t":"Android","u":"install.html#android","x":"Android 8.0 (API 26) or newer. Coming soon: a signed APK on GitHub Releases and Google Play. The release build is prepared and signed with the project's key, but no release is published yet. Build it yourself with JDK 17 or newer and the Android SDK (platform 36). With a phone connected over USB with debugging on, or an emulator running, in the checkout: cd android ./gradlew :app:installDebug ./gradlew :app:assembleDebug makes the APK (app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk) without installing it. A debug build can also talk to a server over plain HTTP, which helps with a server on your computer during development; release builds use HTTPS only. A release build needs a signing key of your own (DAYHAND_RELEASE_KEYSTORE): see android/README.md. Keep that key, because every update must be signed with the same one. Then open Dayhand and choose On this phone only or Connect to my server: Android."},{"p":"Install","t":"macOS","u":"install.html#macos","x":"macOS 13 or newer. Building needs Xcode 26 or later. (cd macos && scripts/build-app.sh) open macos/dist/Dayhand.app The app is built for the Mac that built it: it is not signed with a Developer ID or notarized, so other Macs would refuse it. Updates are built in (Sparkle) but stay off in local builds, which say so in Settings → Updates; update a local build by pulling and building again. Then connect it with a code from dayhand pair, or choose Not now: macOS. Coming soon: a signed, notarized download that updates itself."},{"p":"Install","t":"Linux and Omarchy","u":"install.html#linux-and-omarchy","x":"A Qt 6 app, dayhand-qt. On Arch Linux or Omarchy, build and install the package from the checkout (not from the AUR, where there is no package): cd packaging/arch makepkg -si It builds linux/, runs its tests (they need weston and dbus; --nocheck skips them) and installs dayhand-qt with its launcher entry and icon. It depends on qt6-base, qt6-declarative, qt6-svg, qt6-wayland and libsecret; the sign-in is kept by a Secret Service such as gnome-keyring. For Japanese and Chinese, install a CJK font such as noto-fonts-cjk. On other distributions, build it with CMake 3.21+ and Ninja against Qt 6 (Base, Declarative, SVG, Tools, Wayland) and libsecret, from your distribution's packages: cmake -S linux -B linux/build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo cmake --build linux/build ctest --test-dir linux/build --output-on-failure sudo cmake --install linux/build The install step puts dayhand-qt, its launcher entry, the AppStream metadata and the icon under /usr/local. Setting it up on the desktop (a key for quick add, the tray, themes): Linux and Omarchy. Only Arch Linux builds are tested in the project; the CMake path on other distributions is not. dayhand ui, the terminal app, comes with the command line, on Linux as everywhere else; dayhand ui on Omarchy makes it an app of its own."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Dayhand for Android","u":"android.html","x":"The Android app is the fullest Dayhand client: Today with quick add, the week and day calendar, the board, task details with repeats, checklists and reminders, the trash, a backup file, and, with a server, phone pairing, the calendar address, agent connections and the controls for your agents. It works offline, and reminders and the morning summary are worked out on the phone. It runs on Android 8.0 or newer and speaks English, Polish, German, Spanish, French, Brazilian Portuguese, Japanese and Simplified Chinese. No APK is published yet; Install says how to build it. How the app is built and tested: android/README.md."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"First start: where the plan lives","u":"android.html#first-start-where-the-plan-lives","x":"The first start asks where your plan lives, on two cards: On this phone only: no account and no server. Start with an empty plan, or Try with sample data to look around first: a few overdue, today's and done tasks, a repeating one, a checklist and labels. One tap removes the sample tasks (on Today, or Settings → Data), and they are never sent to a server. Connect to my server: the pairing form (below). Back returns to the two cards. Without a server, the plan lives only on this phone. Android's own backup is off for Dayhand, so keep a backup file (Backups)."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Today","u":"android.html#today","x":"From top to bottom: Overdue: not done and due before today in your time zone. Tick it off, move it To today, or pick another day or archive it from the menu. Now: the one task you are working on, with \"where I stopped / next step\" under its title. Tap the note to edit it. Choose the task with Work on it now in a task's menu. Today: tasks due today, timed ones first, by time. Done: what you finished today, folded. A line at the top says when the phone is offline and how many changes wait, while changes are sent, or when the server signed the phone out. With a server, a plan health card sits above the lists: one sentence with what needs a look, the most pressing first (agents paused, overdue tasks, claims that ran out, approval requests, blocked tasks, proposals, tasks done today without proof). It is red when agents are paused or something is late and amber when you are asked for something; Review opens the agents page."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Quick add","u":"android.html#quick-add","x":"The button at the bottom opens one title field. A date phrase at the end sets the day and time: \"Call the accountant fri 2pm\" is \"Call the accountant\" on the coming Friday at 14:00. Without a phrase, the task is for today. A grey line under the field says what will happen; Keep in title keeps the words as text. More options adds a day, a time and a note. The phrases are English in every language of the app, the same as dayhand add --due: today, tomorrow 9am, fri, next mon, in 3 days, 23 oct, 2026-10-23, 2pm. The full list: dates."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Calendar","u":"android.html#calendar","x":"The bar at the bottom switches between Today, the calendar and the board. Week shows Monday to Sunday on an hour grid, Day one day. Swipe or use the arrows to turn. Tasks with a time are blocks at their hour; tasks without a time sit in a band above the hours. On a phone held upright the week shows them as bars without titles; tap a day for its titles, or long-press a day for a list with Move on each task. Long-press a task and drag it to another day and time. It snaps to a quarter of an hour; drop it on the day headers or the band to remove its time. Undo in the message puts it back. Overdue tasks are marked !, later dates of a repeating task ↻ and done tasks ✓. Later dates of a series and done tasks do not move."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Board","u":"android.html#board","x":"Three columns: To do, In progress (with the now task first) and Done (the 50 most recently done). On a phone one column shows at a time under tabs; from 600 dp wide they sit side by side. Long-press a card and drag it to another column or tab, or use the card's menu. The bin icon opens the trash."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Task details","u":"android.html#task-details","x":"Tap a task to open it. You can change its title, status, the now flag and its note, the day with an optional time, the repeat, reminders, a checklist, labels and links (http and https only). Repeat: every N days, weeks (on chosen weekdays), months or years; a monthly task on a day of the month, the last day, or an nth weekday (\"the 2nd Tuesday\", \"the last Friday\"); counted from the due date or from the day you complete it; ending never, on a day or after a number of times. A day the month lacks (the 31st in April) falls on its last day. Checklist: items in order; ticking them does not complete the task, and the next occurrence of a repeating task starts with every item unticked. Reminders: the defaults from Settings, none, or the task's own: minutes before the due time, or a time on the due day or days before (at most five). Labels are also the projects: there is one list for both. Move to trash keeps the task for 30 days. A task in the trash is read only until you restore it. Change history lists who changed what, newest first: you in the app, you with dayhand, or an agent by name. When an agent works on a task, the details show it: \"agent: name works until time\" while its claim lasts, the acceptance criteria with the met ones ticked, and the proof the task was completed with (agent work). Your own changes always go through, claim or not."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Trash","u":"android.html#trash","x":"Tasks you or an agent deleted, newest first, with the day each one goes for good. Restore brings one back; it works offline too."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Reminders and the morning summary","u":"android.html#reminders-and-the-morning-summary","x":"Reminders are worked out on the phone from its copy of the plan, so they ring without a network and without a server. A task rings when it has a day and is not done or archived: for each of its own reminders, or the default ones from Settings when it has none. Minutes before the due time need a time; a time of day (\"18:00 the day before\") also works for tasks without one. Overdue tasks never ring; they wait at the top of Today. A reminder that would come more than 15 minutes late (phone off, battery saving) is dropped. The morning summary comes every day at the summary time (05:00 unless you change it): how many tasks today, how many overdue and where to start. It is quiet, so it waits on the lock screen. Times are read in the time zone that decides \"today\": the phone's own on this phone only, the server's when connected, so the phone and your agents agree. A reminder's Done button completes the task, offline too. Android 13 and newer ask for permission to show notifications when the first task with a time is added, or from Settings. For reminders on the minute, also allow exact alarms (off by default from Android 14); Settings → Notifications says which is missing and has a button for each. After a force stop, Android drops the alarms until you open Dayhand again."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Connect to your server","u":"android.html#connect-to-your-server","x":"Settings → Server → Connect to my server (also on the first start, and from a link): Server address: your server's address, such as A link or QR code fills it in. A .convex.cloud address is turned into its .convex.site one. Pairing code from dayhand pair on a computer signed in to that server. Case and dashes do not matter, and O is read as 0, I and L as 1. Connect. Errors are said in words: a wrong, used or expired code, too many wrong codes (with the wait), no network, a server that cannot be reached or is not a Dayhand server. Scanning the QR code that dayhand pair prints opens the form filled in. After connecting, every change made on the phone is sent in order, the tasks made before included (sample tasks stay behind), and the server's plan comes down. Release builds of the app connect over HTTPS only. A server on your computer needs tailscale serve in front of it (Phone and computer). A code for another server than the one whose plan is on the phone first asks Replace the plan on this phone?, with how many tasks and waiting changes would go. Cancel keeps everything. Disconnect (Settings → Server) forgets the sign-in on the phone and keeps the plan. The phone cannot sign itself out on the server; the dialog names dayhand revoke <deviceId> for that. When the server revokes the phone, Today and Settings say so, and Connect asks for a new code. Tasks and waiting changes stay on the phone. How the app signs in and stays signed in: Phone pairing."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Settings","u":"android.html#settings","x":"Appearance: theme (system, light or dark), a colour palette for each of dark and light, the font, and the language (the phone's, or one of the eight). Changes apply at once. Notifications: whether notifications and exact alarms are allowed, the morning summary time and the default reminders (at the due time, 5, 10, 15 or 30 minutes, 1 or 2 hours or a day before, any number of them). Data: the backup file, removing sample tasks and, with a server, Export (the whole plan as Markdown or JSON with its history) and Calendar, the read-only address for Google or Apple Calendar (calendar feed): turn it on, copy it, add it to a calendar app, Create a new address or Turn off. The address is a secret; anyone who has it can read your dated tasks. Export .ics shares a one-time calendar file. Connection: Server (status, waiting changes, the server's time zone with Use this phone's time zone when they differ, Disconnect), Agent connections and Agents (below). About: the licences and the version. The summary time and default reminders are server settings once connected, so your agents see them too."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Agents and connections","u":"android.html#agents-and-connections","x":"With a server, two pages under Settings → Connection keep you in charge of your agents: Agent connections lists the agents connected with OAuth, such as the Claude app, with where they come from, whether they may change tasks and when they were last used. Tap one to disconnect it. Approve a connection takes the code that an agent's sign-in page shows, then shows who asks, where the answer goes and how long ago it asked, with a switch per permission. Approve only a sign-in page you opened yourself. The steps for the Claude app: Claude apps. Agents has Pause agents, one switch that refuses every change from every agent while they can still read; the approval requests waiting for you, each with what the agent wants to do and the options it offers; the proposals agents and integrations made, to accept, merge into a task or reject; and the tool policies of your agent keys, read only here (dayhand policy set changes them). While the app is closed, the phone looks for new approval requests every 15 minutes and shows a notification for each. There is no push service in between, so a request can wait up to about 15 minutes, longer in battery saving, before the phone shows it. What these controls do and what they cannot do: Agents and you."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Backups","u":"android.html#backups","x":"Android's Auto Backup is off for Dayhand: the plan, the settings and the sign-in go neither to Google Drive nor to a new phone. On this phone only, a backup file is the only copy outside the phone. Settings → Data → Backup saves the plan as a file in Dayhand's JSON export format (Save a backup file or Share a backup) and restores one (Restore from a file). The same file can be imported into a server with dayhand import. Restoring adds the tasks from the file that the phone does not have yet, so the same file restores once."},{"p":"Dayhand for Android","t":"Privacy","u":"android.html#privacy","x":"The app asks for the network (to reach your server), notifications, exact alarms and starting after a reboot (to arm reminders again). It has no analytics, no crash reporting and no other outside services: the only address it talks to is your server. The sign-in to the server is encrypted with a key in the Android Keystore. More: Security and privacy."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"Dayhand for macOS","u":"macos-app.html","x":"Dayhand for macOS has Today, quick add from the menu bar or a global shortcut, task details with their change history, the trash, reminders and the morning summary as notifications, and works offline like the phone. It runs on macOS 13 or newer, in English, Polish, German, Spanish, French, Brazilian Portuguese, Japanese or Simplified Chinese. It is built from source and is not signed for other Macs yet: Install. How the app is built and tested: macos/README.md. Signing, notarization and the update feed for a release: macos.md."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"Connect, or keep the plan on this Mac","u":"macos-app.html#connect-or-keep-the-plan-on-this-mac","x":"On the first start, connect to your server with a code from dayhand pair: type the server's address and the code, or open the link that dayhand pair prints (the Mac hands it to Dayhand). Not now keeps everything on this Mac until you connect; what you add before is sent then. Disconnect forgets the sign-in on this Mac and keeps the plan; dayhand revoke <deviceId> ends it on the server. When the server revokes the Mac, Today says so, and the tasks and waiting changes stay."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"The window","u":"macos-app.html#the-window","x":"A sidebar on the left switches between Today and Trash and lists their tasks; the selected task's details are on the right. Today: overdue (with To today), Now with \"where I stopped\", today (timed tasks first, by time) and done today, folded. One click ticks a task off. The context menu makes a task the one you work on now, moves it to today or tomorrow, archives it or moves it to the trash. Task details: title, status, \"working on it now\" with the resume note, the day and time (a date phrase, Today, No day, Remove the time), labels, the checklist and the note. Repeat, reminders and links are shown but edited elsewhere for now (the phone, dayhand edit). History at the bottom of the details: who changed the task (you in the app, you with dayhand, or an agent by name) and when, each field from its old value to the new one. It comes from the server; offline, the last copy stays. Trash: deleted tasks, newest first, with the day each was deleted and the day it goes for good. Restore works offline. The server keeps deleted tasks for 30 days."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"Quick add","u":"macos-app.html#quick-add","x":"Quick add is at the top of Today, in the menu bar, and in a floating panel from any app with ⌃⌥N (change the shortcut in Settings). A date phrase at the end of the line sets the day and time: \"Call the accountant fri 2pm\", \"Pay rent the 1st\", \"Plan next week\". The line below the field says what was understood; Keep in title keeps the words as text. Without a phrase, the task is for today. The phrases are English in every language (dates)."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"Menu bar","u":"macos-app.html#menu-bar","x":"The menu bar icon shows how many tasks are open today, with a warning mark when some are overdue. Its menu has quick add and today's open tasks to tick off without opening the window. Closing the window keeps Dayhand in the menu bar, so reminders keep coming."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"Reminders and the morning summary","u":"macos-app.html#reminders-and-the-morning-summary","x":"Reminders and the morning summary are macOS notifications, worked out on the Mac from its copy of the plan with the same rules as the phone, in the time zone of the server's settings (this Mac's until a server has sent them). Overdue tasks never ring. The summary is quiet and waits in Notification Center. A reminder's Done completes the task. Dayhand asks for permission when the first reminder is planned, or from Settings."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"Language","u":"macos-app.html#language","x":"Dayhand follows the Mac's languages. Settings → General → Language picks one of the eight, and the windows change at once; the menus that come with macOS follow after Restart Dayhand."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"Updates","u":"macos-app.html#updates","x":"The app has an updater (Sparkle) with its own window and Settings → Updates. It works only in a build signed with the project's release key, and none is published yet: a build you made yourself says \"Updates aren't available in this build\" and never checks. Update it by pulling the repository and building again."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"How it stays in sync","u":"macos-app.html#how-it-stays-in-sync","x":"Every change goes into the Mac's database and an outbox at once, so the window never waits for the network. The app sends the outbox in order and reads the plan back every 15 seconds, and at once after a change of yours. Changes made elsewhere therefore appear within about 15 seconds. What Where Tasks and the outbox ~/Library/Application Support/Dayhand/dayhand.sqlite The sign-in to the server the login Keychain, item io.github.maiarowsky.dayhand Preferences (shortcut, window, updates, language) the defaults domain io.github.maiarowsky.dayhand DAYHAND_DATA_DIR=<folder> uses another database (for a second profile or a test), DAYHAND_CREDENTIALS=memory keeps the sign-in until the app quits, and DAYHAND_LANG=<code> starts it in a language without changing Settings."},{"p":"Dayhand for macOS","t":"Not on the Mac yet","u":"macos-app.html#not-on-the-mac-yet","x":"The board, the calendar, editors for repeats, reminders and links, the morning summary time and default reminders, export, the calendar address, and the agent pages (approving agent connections and approval requests, pausing agents, proposals) are on the phone and in dayhand, not in the Mac app yet. Quick add reads English date phrases only."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","u":"linux.html","x":"What the app does and how it is built and tested is in linux/README.md. This page is about putting it on a desktop, with Omarchy (Hyprland) as the worked example: install, a key for quick add, the tray, notifications, colours that follow the theme, the language, and where the data lives. Nothing here has been run on a real Omarchy or Hyprland session yet; see Not checked. The Omarchy details follow its own configuration files (omacom/omarchy, branch quattro at 99f479b, read on 2026-10-24)."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Install","u":"linux.html#install","x":"There is no AUR package. Build the Arch Linux package from a checkout of this repository: cd packaging/arch makepkg -si # builds linux/, runs its tests (needs weston and dbus; --nocheck skips them), installs It installs /usr/bin/dayhand-qt, io.github.maiarowsky.dayhand.desktop (also the handler of dayhand:// and helm:// links), the icon and the AppStream metadata. It needs qt6-base, qt6-declarative, qt6-svg, qt6-wayland and libsecret; the sign-in is kept by a Secret Service such as gnome-keyring, which Omarchy runs. For Japanese and Chinese, a CJK font such as noto-fonts-cjk must be installed."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"First start","u":"linux.html#first-start","x":"Open Dayhand from the launcher. It asks for a server: on a computer signed in with dayhand, run dayhand pair and type the address and code it shows, or open the link it prints (the browser hands it to Dayhand). Not Now, Use Dayhand Offline keeps the tasks on this computer; ⋯ → Connect… connects later and sends them. ⋯ → Disconnect forgets the sign-in here; dayhand revoke <device> ends it on the server. Closing the window keeps Dayhand in the tray, so reminders keep ringing. Quit Dayhand (tray or ⋯) stops it. Without a tray, closing the window quits."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Quick add from any app (Hyprland)","u":"linux.html#quick-add-from-any-app-hyprland","x":"dayhand-qt --quick-add opens the small quick add window. Only one Dayhand runs per session: when it is already running, the second dayhand-qt hands the request over a socket in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and exits, so the window appears at once. Return adds the task and closes the window, Escape closes it. On Omarchy, personal bindings go in ~/.config/hypr/bindings.lua (Super + Alt + N and Super + Alt + D are free in Omarchy's defaults; omarchy menu keybindings --print lists what is taken): o.bind(\"SUPER + ALT + N\", \"Dayhand quick add\", { launch = \"dayhand-qt --quick-add\" }) o.bind(\"SUPER + ALT + D\", \"Dayhand\", { launch = \"dayhand-qt\" }) { launch = … } runs the command through uwsm-app, as Omarchy does for its own apps. To have the quick add window float in the middle of the screen instead of tiling, add a window rule at the end of ~/.config/hypr/hyprland.lua (the window's class is the app id, its title is always the English Dayhand quick add): o.window({ class = \"io\\\\.github\\\\.maiarowsky\\\\.dayhand\", title = \"^Dayhand quick add$\" }, { float = true, center = true }) To start Dayhand in the tray when you log in, add to ~/.config/hypr/autostart.lua: o.launch_on_start(\"dayhand-qt --background\") On Hyprland without Omarchy, the same in hyprland.conf (the window rule syntax changes between Hyprland versions; check it against the wiki for yours): bind = SUPER ALT, N, exec, dayhand-qt --quick-add exec-once = dayhand-qt --background windowrulev2 = float, class:^(io\\.github\\.maiarowsky\\.dayhand)$, title:^(Dayhand quick add)$ windowrulev2 = center, class:^(io\\.github\\.maiarowsky\\.dayhand)$, title:^(Dayhand quick add)$"},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"The GlobalShortcuts portal (not used)","u":"linux.html#the-globalshortcuts-portal-not-used","x":"Hyprland's portal (xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland) also offers the GlobalShortcuts interface: an app registers a named shortcut (here it would be quick-add) and Hyprland binds a key to it with the global dispatcher (bind = SUPER ALT, N, global, io.github.maiarowsky.dayhand:quick-add, hl.dsp.global(…) in Lua). Dayhand does not register one in this version. The binding above does the same with nothing to set up in the app and works on any compositor that can run a command; the portal path would need a portal session kept for the life of the app and cannot be tested without a running Hyprland. It is a candidate for later, for desktops where users cannot add bindings of their own."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Tray","u":"linux.html#tray","x":"Dayhand's tray icon is a StatusNotifierItem with a dbusmenu menu, which Qt provides over D-Bus. Omarchy's shell shows such items in its tray, and so do Waybar's tray module and KDE. The tooltip says how many tasks are open today and how many are overdue, and the icon gets a red mark when something is overdue. The menu has Quick Add…, Open Dayhand, up to ten of today's open tasks (choosing one ticks it off), Sync Now and Quit Dayhand. A click on the icon opens the window; a middle click opens quick add."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Notifications","u":"linux.html#notifications","x":"Reminders and the morning summary go to the notification service on the session bus (org.freedesktop.Notifications: Omarchy's shell, mako, dunst, GNOME, KDE). A reminder has Done, which completes the task, and opening it shows Dayhand; it has normal urgency. The morning summary has low urgency, so it waits quietly. Dayhand rings only while it runs (in the tray); a reminder more than 15 minutes late, after the laptop slept, is dropped, as on Android."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Colours and Omarchy themes","u":"linux.html#colours-and-omarchy-themes","x":"Dayhand takes its colours from the system palette, which Qt gets from the platform theme. Omarchy sets QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=gtk3, so Qt apps follow the GTK theme's light or dark mode, not the colours of the Omarchy theme itself. For the theme's own colours, Dayhand reads a palette file, the first of: $DAYHAND_PALETTE ~/.config/dayhand/palette.conf ~/.local/state/omarchy/current/theme/dayhand-palette.conf with key = value lines (# and ; start comments). The keys are the names of Omarchy's colors.toml: background, lighter_background, foreground, accent, selection_foreground, muted, and red or overdue for overdue tasks. A key left out keeps the system's colour. Dayhand watches the file and recolours the running window when it changes. Omarchy renders every template in ~/.config/omarchy/themed/ into the current theme's folder on each theme switch, so one template keeps Dayhand in step with every theme: # ~/.config/omarchy/themed/dayhand-palette.conf.tpl background = {{ background }} lighter_background = {{ lighter_background }} foreground = {{ foreground }} accent = {{ accent }} selection_foreground = {{ selection_foreground }} muted = {{ muted }} red = {{ red }} Run omarchy theme set <theme> (or pick a theme in the menu) once after adding it. A theme without an accent colour leaves that line empty and Dayhand keeps the system's highlight."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Language","u":"linux.html#language","x":"Dayhand speaks English, Polish, German, Spanish, French, Brazilian Portuguese, Japanese and Simplified Chinese. It follows the system's language (LANGUAGE, LC_MESSAGES, LANG) and falls back to English; ⋯ → Settings… → Language picks another one at once. Quick add reads date phrases in English in every language (\"tomorrow 9am\", \"fri\", \"23 oct\"), the same grammar as dayhand."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Data and settings","u":"linux.html#data-and-settings","x":"What Where Tasks and the outbox (SQLite) ~/.local/share/dayhand/dayhand.sqlite Preferences (language, offline mode, last server) ~/.config/dayhand/dayhand-qt.conf The sign-in to the server the Secret Service keyring, item io.github.maiarowsky.dayhand, account=device Environment variables: DAYHAND_DATA_DIR=<folder> keeps the database and preferences there instead (for a second profile or a test), DAYHAND_CREDENTIALS=memory keeps the sign-in in memory only, and DAYHAND_PALETTE=<file> names a palette file."},{"p":"Dayhand for Linux: install and desktop setup","t":"Not checked","u":"linux.html#not-checked","x":"No run on Omarchy, Hyprland or another real desktop. The app was built and tested in an Arch Linux container: the window on Qt's offscreen platform, the tray and notifications as a client of a headless Weston against fake desktop services, and the real dayhand-qt started on that Weston. The bindings, the window rule, the autostart line and the theme template follow Omarchy's files, not a test on the system. Not tried with the Omarchy shell's tray or notification centre, Waybar, mako or dunst; only the D-Bus interfaces they implement were exercised. The Secret Service keyring path was not exercised (the tests keep the sign-in in memory), and no pairing with a real server was made from this app; pairing and sync were tested against a fake server. Japanese and Chinese were not looked at on screen (the test container has no CJK font); their texts and those of the other languages were translated by the agent that wrote the app and have not been reviewed by native speakers."},{"p":"Dayhand in the terminal","t":"Dayhand in the terminal","u":"terminal.html","x":"dayhand is two things in one command: plain commands for people, scripts and agents (dayhand add, dayhand done 3), and dayhand ui, the plan full screen in the terminal. Both use your server's MCP tools with the key that dayhand login stored, so every change shows up in the apps at once and is signed cli in the history. Installing it: Install."},{"p":"Dayhand in the terminal","t":"Everyday commands","u":"terminal.html#everyday-commands","x":"To Run See Today dayhand (or dayhand today) Add a task dayhand add Call the accountant --due \"fri 2pm\" -l admin See every open task dayhand list, dayhand list -l admin --due-before eow See the board dayhand board Finish a task dayhand done 3 (several at once: dayhand done 3 4 now) Move it to another day dayhand move 3 \"next mon 9am\" (none removes the day) Work on it now, and note where you stopped dayhand now 3 --note \"waiting for the bank\" Read or change its note, checklist or fields dayhand note 3, dayhand checklist 3, dayhand edit 3 See a task with who changed what dayhand show 3 Delete and restore dayhand rm 3, dayhand trash, dayhand restore 3 Check what a date phrase means dayhand when \"in 3 days at 8:15\" Try a change without saving it add --dry-run to the command Tasks are referred to by number (3 or #3), now for the now task, or their id. Dates are English phrases in your time zone from the server settings (dates). Changing three or more tasks at once asks first (or needs --yes in a script). Every command and option: command reference, also as dayhand help <command> and the manual page man/dayhand.1."},{"p":"Dayhand in the terminal","t":"dayhand ui","u":"terminal.html#dayhand-ui","x":"dayhand ui It opens full screen with four views: Today (overdue, the now task with its resume note, today, done today), the Board (to do, in progress, done), the Week (Monday to Sunday as bars, the selected day in full below) and the Trash. Enter opens a task with its history, which names who made each change: app, cli or agent: <name>, so an agent's edits stand out. The keys most people need: Key Does j k (or the arrows) Next and previous task h l Board column, week day Tab, 1 2 3 4 Next view; Today, Board, Week, Trash a Add a task: Pay rent fri 2pm #home Space or x Done or not done s Start (doing) or stop (to do) n, N Make it the now task with a resume note; clear the now task e, E Edit title, day, labels and resume note; title and note in $EDITOR m Move: tomorrow, fri 9am, none d, u, r To the trash (asks first), undo, restore / Filter by title, #label, note or #42 ?, q Every key; quit The full list is ? in the app, dayhand help ui and the reference. The layout and keys follow vim and lazygit. Quick add (a) reads a date phrase at the end or the start of the line and #labels anywhere. The line below shows how it was read before Enter adds it; no date adds no day, and quotes keep words as text. The mouse selects and scrolls. Mouse reporting stops the terminal's own text selection: hold Shift while dragging to select anyway, or start dayhand ui --no-mouse. Refresh: what is on screen is read again every 12 seconds (--interval 10 to 300), within the key's limits of 120 reads and 60 changes a minute; R refreshes now. Without a connection it keeps the last data on screen, says so in red, and tries again. Colours are the terminal's 16 ANSI colours, so your terminal theme applies. Language: the screens speak English, Polish, German, Spanish, French, Brazilian Portuguese, Japanese or Simplified Chinese, following --lang, then DAYHAND_LANG, then the locale (LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES, LANG). Date phrases, the other commands, --json and error codes stay English. --view week opens another view first. dayhand ui shows the plan while it is open; it has no reminders. For reminders on a computer, use the macOS or Linux app. On Omarchy, a launcher entry and a key make it an app of its own: dayhand ui on Omarchy."},{"p":"Dayhand in the terminal","t":"Several servers and profiles","u":"terminal.html#several-servers-and-profiles","x":"dayhand login --url <address> stores a key per server address. config.toml can name profiles and aliases: default_profile = \"personal\" [profiles.personal] url = \" [profiles.work] url = \" [aliases] week = \"list --due-before eow\" dayhand --profile work, or DAYHAND_PROFILE=work, picks one; dayhand week runs the alias. dayhand whoami shows which server, profile, key source and file are in use. Where the file lives and how the URL and key are chosen: config. The key itself never goes into config.toml: dayhand login keeps it in the system keychain (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, the Secret Service on Linux) or, without one, in a file only you can read (login)."},{"p":"Dayhand in the terminal","t":"For scripts","u":"terminal.html#for-scripts","x":"--json prints exactly what the server's tool returns, in a shape described by the schemas; --plain prints tab-separated rows without headers. Errors with --json are one JSON line on stderr, and the exit code says what to do next: 2 a usage error, 3 no or a wrong key, 4 not found, 5 the server is unavailable or the key made too many calls, 6 refused by the task's state, 7 refused by the owner (exit codes). After exit 5, run the same command again with the --client-id the error names: a change is never applied twice (retries). dayhand never asks anything without a terminal (--no-input or DAYHAND_NO_INPUT=1 make sure). DAYHAND_URL and DAYHAND_API_KEY win over the stored login, so a script or an agent can use its own key (agents)."},{"p":"dayhand ui on Omarchy","t":"dayhand ui on Omarchy","u":"omarchy.html","x":"Omarchy treats terminal programs as first-class apps: a launcher entry opens them in their own terminal window, a Hyprland key focuses that window when it is already open, and the terminal's 16 ANSI colours follow the Omarchy theme. dayhand ui uses only those 16 colours, so it changes with the theme like the rest of the desktop. For the desktop app with a tray, quick add and notifications, see linux.md. This page describes a hand-made setup. There is no AUR package yet, and nothing here has been run on an Omarchy machine (see Not checked)."},{"p":"dayhand ui on Omarchy","t":"Install dayhand","u":"omarchy.html#install-dayhand","x":"dayhand needs Node.js 22.12 or newer, which Omarchy does not install by default: omarchy pkg add nodejs npm # or: sudo pacman -S nodejs npm dayhand is not on npm yet (release.md), so build it from a checkout of this repository: cd cli npm ci --ignore-scripts npm run build npm link # puts dayhand on the PATH dayhand login --url dayhand login keeps the key in the Secret Service keyring (gnome-keyring and libsecret are in Omarchy's base packages). Check it with dayhand whoami, then try dayhand ui in any terminal."},{"p":"dayhand ui on Omarchy","t":"A launcher entry","u":"omarchy.html#a-launcher-entry","x":"Either go to Install > TUI in the Omarchy menu (Super + Space) and give it the name Dayhand, the command dayhand ui, a window style and an icon, or write the entry yourself. The hand-written one below focuses the Dayhand window when it is already open instead of starting a second one, because it launches through omarchy-launch-or-focus-tui with a fixed app id: # ~/.local/share/applications/Dayhand.desktop [Desktop Entry] Version=1.0 Name=Dayhand Comment=Today, board and week of your Dayhand tasks Exec=omarchy-launch-or-focus-tui --app-id=org.omarchy.dayhand dayhand ui Terminal=false Type=Application Icon=utilities-terminal StartupNotify=true omarchy-launch-or-focus-tui looks for a window whose class or title matches the app id and focuses it; otherwise it runs omarchy-launch-tui, which opens the default terminal with xdg-terminal-exec --app-id=org.omarchy.dayhand -e dayhand ui. Install > TUI writes a similar entry, but with Exec=xdg-terminal-exec --app-id=TUI.tile -e dayhand ui (or TUI.float), which always opens a new window. For your own icon, copy a PNG to ~/.local/share/icons/hicolor/256x256/apps/dayhand.png and set Icon=dayhand."},{"p":"dayhand ui on Omarchy","t":"A Hyprland key","u":"omarchy.html#a-hyprland-key","x":"Personal bindings go in ~/.config/hypr/bindings.lua. Pass the whole command as a string so that the app id stays org.omarchy.dayhand and the key focuses a running Dayhand: o.bind(\"SUPER + SHIFT + H\", \"Dayhand\", \"omarchy-launch-or-focus-tui --app-id=org.omarchy.dayhand dayhand ui\") Pick a free combination: omarchy menu keybindings --print lists the ones in use. The shorter { tui = \"dayhand ui\", focus = true } form does not suit a command with an argument: it quotes dayhand ui as one word, so the app id becomes org.omarchy.dayhand ui and never matches the window. The window tiles like any other; Super + T floats it. To open it floating every time, use the app id TUI.float instead (Omarchy floats that class), at the cost of the key also focusing other floating TUIs."},{"p":"dayhand ui on Omarchy","t":"In the app","u":"omarchy.html#in-the-app","x":"Keys: ? in the app, or dayhand help ui (cli.md). q quits. The mouse selects and scrolls. Mouse reporting stops the terminal's own text selection; hold Shift while dragging to select anyway, or start dayhand ui --no-mouse. E opens $VISUAL or $EDITOR for a task's title and note, in the same window, and comes back when it closes. The screen refreshes every 12 seconds (--interval). Without a connection it keeps the last data, says so in red, and retries."},{"p":"dayhand ui on Omarchy","t":"Not checked","u":"omarchy.html#not-checked","x":"No run on Omarchy or in a real Hyprland session: the entry, the binding and the theme colours above follow Omarchy's own scripts (bin/omarchy-launch-or-focus-tui, bin/omarchy-launch-tui, bin/omarchy-tui-install, default/hypr/helpers.lua, branch quattro at cbd44ba, read on 2026-10-23), not a test on the system. The keyring on Linux (Secret Service) is the same code path as for every dayhand command; it was not tried on Omarchy either. No reminders or notifications: dayhand ui shows the plan while it is open. Desktop notifications (notify-send from a systemd user timer) would be a separate addition."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand command reference","u":"cli.html","x":"dayhand is the Dayhand command line client. Every command is one or more MCP tool calls on the same /mcp endpoint the agent uses (see mcp.md); dayhand call reaches any tool directly. dh is a shorter name for the same command. On Debian and Ubuntu, debhelper has a dh command of its own; type dayhand there. hlm, the old name, still works until 1.0 and warns on every run. Task references: #42 or 42 (the task number), now (the now task) or the task id. Without a command, dayhand shows Today."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Commands","u":"cli.html#commands","x":"Command Calls --json schema Summary dayhand today list_today today The Today screen: now task, overdue, today and done today (also plain dayhand) dayhand add <title...> [options] add_task task Add a task (due today unless --due or --no-due) dayhand list [options] list_all task-list Open tasks, oldest due first (with --done also done ones); filters narrow the list dayhand board [options] board board Kanban board: todo, doing and done columns dayhand show <ref> get_task task-detail One task with its change history (who changed what, when) dayhand edit <ref> [options] update_task task Change a task's fields; --clear-* removes one; on a terminal without options, edit title and note in $EDITOR dayhand note <ref> [text...] [options] get_task, update_task task-detail, task Print a task's note (on a terminal: edit it in $VISUAL/$EDITOR), or append text to it dayhand checklist <ref> [options] get_task, update_task task-detail, task Print a task's checklist, or tick, untick, move, remove and add items dayhand move <ref> <date> [options] reschedule_task task Move a task to another day, optionally with a time (none removes the date) dayhand done <refs...> [options] complete_task complete Mark tasks done, optionally with proof; a repeating task gets its next occurrence dayhand start <refs...> set_status task Set tasks to doing dayhand stop <refs...> set_status task Set tasks back to todo (from doing) dayhand reopen <refs...> set_status task Set done tasks back to todo dayhand now [ref] [options] set_now, list_today set-now, today Show the now task, make a task the now task (--note: where you stopped), or --clear dayhand archive <refs...> archive_task task Archive tasks: off Today and the board, kept in exports dayhand rm <refs...> delete_task task Move tasks to the trash (purged after 30 days) dayhand restore <refs...> restore_task task Restore tasks from the trash (or the archive) dayhand trash list_trash task-list Tasks in the trash, newest first dayhand log [options] list_events event-list The activity log: task changes after a cursor or a time, oldest first (who changed what) dayhand claim <ref> [options] claim_task task Claim a task for a while (default 120 minutes) so other agents leave it alone dayhand renew <ref> [options] renew_claim task Extend your claim of a task by --minutes from now (default 120) dayhand release <ref> [options] release_task task End your claim of a task, optionally leaving a checkpoint as its resume note dayhand criteria <ref> [options] get_task, update_task task-detail, task Print a task's acceptance criteria, or mark them met, unmet, remove and add them dayhand ask <action...> [options] request_approval approval Ask the owner to decide before you do something; they answer on the phone dayhand approval <id> [options] get_approval approval One approval request and, once decided, the owner's choice dayhand approvals [options] list_approvals approval-list Approval requests, newest first dayhand approvals decide <id> <option> [options] decide_approval approval Pick an option of a pending approval request dayhand agents get_agent_controls agent-controls Whether agents are paused, and the tool policies of agent keys dayhand agents pause [options] pause_agents agent-controls Pause every agent: they can read, their changes are refused until you resume them dayhand agents resume pause_agents agent-controls Let agents change the plan again dayhand policy get_agent_controls agent-controls Tool policies of agent keys: allow, ask (the owner approves each call) or deny dayhand policy set <key> <tool> <rule> set_tool_policy agent-controls Set one agent key's rule for a tool that changes the plan, or \"*\" for every such tool dayhand proposals list_all task-list The proposal inbox: tasks agents and integrations proposed, waiting for you dayhand proposals accept <ref> accept_proposal task Accept a proposal: it joins the plan as it is dayhand proposals merge <ref> [options] merge_proposal merge Merge a proposal into a task alre"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Global options","u":"cli.html#global-options","x":"Option Meaning --json Print the tool's structuredContent as JSON; errors as JSON on stderr --plain Tab-separated text without headers (for scripts) --fields <names> Comma-separated task (or settings) fields for JSON and columns --client-id <id> Operation id for safe retries (default: generated, shown on errors) --approval <id> Run a change the owner's policy held back, with the approval request they allowed (exit 7 names it) -y, --yes Confirm changing many tasks at once --no-input Never prompt (also DAYHAND_NO_INPUT=1); prompts need a terminal anyway --dry-run Show the changes a command would make (dates resolved), checked by the backend, and save nothing --url <url> Deployment HTTP actions URL (default: DAYHAND_URL, then config.toml) --profile <name> Profile from config.toml (default: DAYHAND_PROFILE, then default_profile) --color <when> Colors in the human format (auto, always, never) --no-color Same as --color never (NO_COLOR=1 works too)"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"JSON output","u":"cli.html#json-output","x":"--json (or DAYHAND_FORMAT=json) prints the tool's structuredContent exactly as an MCP client receives it; schemas are in schemas/. Indented on a terminal, one line in a pipe. Fields may be added in a minor version; they are never removed or retyped within a major version. A list tool returns {\"result\": [...]}. A command given several tasks returns {\"results\": [...]}, one tool result per task. --fields a,b keeps only those fields of every task (or of the settings). dayhand now without a task prints list_today; dayhand note <ref> without text prints get_task. A Markdown export's JSON is {\"format\":\"markdown\",\"content\":\"…\"}. Errors go to stderr as one line: {\"error\":{\"code\",\"message\",\"exitCode\",\"retryable\",\"clientId\",…}} (schema); stdout stays empty. Warnings are {\"warning\":{…}} lines. --plain prints tab-separated rows without headers: ref status dueDate dueTime title labels (or the --fields); views with sections (Today, board) start each row with the section name. dayhand list filters (--status, --overdue, --due-before, --due-after, --search, --limit, --cursor) run on the backend and keep list_all's shape: {\"result\": [...], \"total\", \"nextCursor\"}. With --limit, pass nextCursor as --cursor for the next page. A backend older than 0.4.0 has no total and nextCursor; dayhand then filters its full list itself. --dry-run on a command that changes something prints {\"dryRun\":true,\"calls\":[{\"tool\",\"arguments\",\"result\"}]} (schema) with dates resolved. The backend checks each change and returns what it would give (result) without saving anything; reads still happen. A backend older than 0.4.0 and the device endpoints get nothing: their calls are only listed."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Human output","u":"cli.html#human-output","x":"Tables are aligned by display width; due days read as today, tomorrow, yesterday or Fri 23 Oct in your time zone. A terminal gets colors (--color auto|always|never, --no-color, [ui] color, NO_COLOR) and long titles cut to its width; a pipe gets the same text without colors or cutting. dayhand board shows its columns side by side on terminals 90 columns or wider. On a terminal, dayhand note <ref> and dayhand edit <ref> without options open $VISUAL/$EDITOR."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Exit codes","u":"cli.html#exit-codes","x":"Code Meaning 0 Success, including a change that was already applied (no-op) 1 Other error (internal) 2 Usage: unknown command or flag, bad value or date, --yes missing, broken config.toml 3 Authentication: no key (dayhand login or DAYHAND_API_KEY), or a wrong or revoked one 4 Not found: no such task (#n), device or now task 5 Server unavailable (network, timeout, HTTP 5xx): the change may have been applied; retry with the same --client-id. Also too many calls with the key (rate_limited): nothing was applied; run it again after the wait the message names 6 Refused by the task's state, nothing applied: another agent holds the task's claim (conflict), or its acceptance criteria are not met or the proof has no summary (precondition_failed) 7 Refused by the owner, nothing applied: agents are paused (agents_paused), only the owner may do this or a tool policy denies it (forbidden), or the policy asks first (approval_required: wait for dayhand approval <approvalId>, then rerun with --approval <approvalId>) 130 Interrupted (Ctrl-C) Backend error codes map to exit codes: invalid_argument → 2, unauthenticated → 3, not_found → 4, rate_limited → 5, conflict and precondition_failed → 6, agents_paused, forbidden and approval_required → 7, internal and unknown codes → 1."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Retries","u":"cli.html#retries","x":"Every change carries a clientId: --client-id <id>, or a generated one that is printed with any error. After exit code 5, run the same command line with that --client-id: the backend does not apply an operation twice. A command given several tasks uses <id>:1, <id>:2, … per task."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Prompts","u":"cli.html#prompts","x":"Changing 3 or more tasks at once needs --yes. On a terminal dayhand asks instead; without a terminal (or with --no-input / DAYHAND_NO_INPUT=1) it never waits for input and exits with 2."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Environment","u":"cli.html#environment","x":"Variable Meaning DAYHAND_URL Deployment HTTP actions URL, e.g. (wins over config.toml) DAYHAND_API_KEY API key; wins over the key stored by dayhand login (give agents and CI their own, kind agent) DAYHAND_PROFILE Profile from config.toml (--profile wins) DAYHAND_FORMAT json, plain or human: default output format (flags win) DAYHAND_NO_INPUT 1 to never prompt DAYHAND_CONFIG_DIR Folder of config.toml and credentials.json (default: the system's config folder) DAYHAND_CACHE_DIR Folder of the completion cache (default: the system's cache folder) DAYHAND_CREDENTIAL_STORE keychain or file: where dayhand login keeps keys (default: keychain, else file) NO_COLOR, FORCE_COLOR Turn colors off or on when neither --color nor config.toml decides VISUAL, EDITOR Editor for dayhand note <ref> and dayhand edit <ref> on a terminal (default: vi, notepad on Windows) HLM_*, HELM_URL, HELM_API_KEY, HELM_BACKEND_DIR, HELM_SERVER_CONFIG_DIR Old names (the hlm and helm commands), still read when the DAYHAND_* name is unset, with a warning"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Topic: dates","u":"cli.html#topic-dates","x":"dayhand help dates Dates (--due, dayhand move, dayhand list --due-before/--due-after, dayhand when) Dates are English phrases resolved in your time zone from the server settings (dayhand settings), not the computer's. The same phrase at the same moment always gives the same day. Try one with: dayhand when \"<phrase>\", or add --dry-run to a command. Days today (tod), tomorrow (tmr), yesterday, day after tomorrow Weekdays fri, friday, on mon: the first such day after today this fri: this week's (today included); next fri: next week's (weeks start on Monday) Periods next week (its Monday), next month (the 1st), next year, weekend, next weekend eow / end of week (Sunday), eoww / end of work week (Friday), eom / end of month, eoy / end of year Relative in 3 days, in a week, in 2 months, in 1 year, +3d, +2w, +1mo, +1y in 2 hours, in 30 min, +3h: a day and a time Calendar 23 oct, 23rd of october, oct 23, oct 23 2027, the 15th, 2026-10-23 (without a year: the next such day, today included) Times 14:00, 9:30, 2pm, 2:30 pm, at 9, noon, midnight alone: today, or tomorrow once that time has passed Together tomorrow at 9am, fri 14:30, 9am tomorrow, at 3pm on friday, in 3 days at 8:15 A time that does not exist because the clocks go forward (01:30 on the last Sunday of March in London) moves forward by the gap (to 02:30) with a warning. YYYY-MM-DD, today and tomorrow are sent to the server as typed; every other phrase is sent as YYYY-MM-DD (and HH:MM). Anything else is refused (exit 2), never guessed. Examples: today, tomorrow, fri, next mon, this weekend, in 3 days, +2w, in 2 hours, eow, eom, 23 oct, oct 23 2027, 2026-10-23, 14:00, 2pm, noon, tomorrow at 9am, fri 14:30"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Topic: config","u":"cli.html#topic-config","x":"dayhand help config Configuration (config.toml) The file lives in: Linux $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dayhand/config.toml (~/.config/dayhand/config.toml) macOS ~/Library/Application Support/dayhand/config.toml Windows %APPDATA%\\dayhand\\config.toml or in $DAYHAND_CONFIG_DIR. dayhand whoami prints the one in use. dayhand login creates it. url = \" # used when no profile is chosen default_profile = \"personal\" [profiles.personal] url = \" [profiles.work] url = \" [aliases] home = \"list -l home --status todo,doing\" # dayhand home --json week = \"list --due-before eow\" [ui] color = \"auto\" # auto, always or never Precedence. URL: --url, the profile named by --profile, DAYHAND_URL, the profile from DAYHAND_PROFILE or default_profile, then the top-level url. Key: DAYHAND_API_KEY, then the key dayhand login stored for that URL. Colors: --color/--no-color, [ui] color, NO_COLOR/FORCE_COLOR, then whether the output is a terminal. The API key never goes into this file: dayhand refuses keys named api_key, key, token, secret or password. An unknown key or a wrong value is an error that names the file and the key (exit 2). Aliases cannot hide a built-in command."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Topic: login","u":"cli.html#topic-login","x":"dayhand help login Signing in (dayhand login, dayhand logout, dayhand whoami) dayhand login --url # paste the key at the hidden prompt dayhand login --with-key < key.txt # or pipe it (scripts) dayhand whoami # URL, profile, where the key came from dayhand logout dayhand server deploy and dayhand server start make a key of kind cli and sign in by themselves. For another computer, make one where dayhand is signed in (dayhand key create laptop --kind cli --scope write) or with npx convex run apiKeys:create. dayhand login checks it against the server, then stores it for that URL in the OS keychain: macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager or the Secret Service on Linux (GNOME Keyring, KWallet, KeePassXC). Without one (a server, Omarchy without a keyring daemon) it says so and keeps the key in credentials.json next to config.toml, readable only by you (mode 0600). DAYHAND_CREDENTIAL_STORE=file or --store file chooses the file on purpose. DAYHAND_API_KEY, when set, wins over the stored key: give agents and CI their own key (kind agent) that way. The keychain keeps the key out of dotfiles, backups and shell history; it does not hide it from programs running as you."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Topic: completion","u":"cli.html#topic-completion","x":"dayhand help completion Shell completion (dayhand completion <shell>) bash echo 'source <(dayhand completion bash)' >> ~/.bashrc zsh dayhand completion zsh > \"${fpath[1]}/_dayhand\" (or source <(dayhand completion zsh) after compinit) fish dayhand completion fish | tee ~/.config/fish/completions/{dayhand,dh}.fish >/dev/null PowerShell dayhand completion powershell | Out-String | Invoke-Expression (add it to $PROFILE) Completes commands, aliases from config.toml, options and their values, task numbers with their titles, labels, profiles and date words. Task numbers and labels come from one list_all call, cached for 60 seconds in the cache folder (~/.cache/dayhand, ~/Library/Caches/dayhand or %LOCALAPPDATA%\\dayhand\\Cache). Completion never prompts and prints nothing when it cannot reach the server."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Topic: ui","u":"cli.html#topic-ui","x":"dayhand help ui The terminal app (dayhand ui) dayhand ui opens Dayhand full screen in the terminal: Today (overdue, the now task with its resume note, today, done today), the board, the week (Monday to Sunday as bars, the selected day in full below) and the trash. Enter opens a task with its history, which names who made each change: app, cli or agent: <name>. It uses the key from dayhand login and the same MCP tools as the other commands, refreshes what is on screen every 12 seconds (--interval) within the key's limits, and keeps the last data on screen with a red status line when the server cannot be reached. Colours are the terminal's 16 ANSI colours, so its theme applies. The mouse selects and scrolls (--no-mouse leaves it to the terminal). Quick add (a) reads a date phrase at the end or the start of the line, and #labels anywhere: \"Call the accountant fri 2pm #home\". The line below shows how it was read before Enter adds it; \"no date\" adds no day, quotes keep it literal. Move: j k ↓ ↑ Next / previous task h l ← → Board column, week day g G Home End First / last task C-d C-u PgDn PgUp Half a page down / up Enter Open a task (an empty day: add) Esc Back; clears the filter Views: Tab S-Tab Next / previous view 1 2 3 4 Today, Board, Week, Trash / Filter: title, #label, note, #42 R Refresh now (also every 12 s) ? This help q C-c Quit (q in details: back) Tasks: a Add: Pay rent fri 2pm #home Space x Done / not done (repeats go on) s Start (doing) / stop (todo) n Now task, with a resume note N Clear the now task e Edit title, due, labels, resume note E Title and note in $EDITOR m Move: tomorrow, fri 9am, none d To the trash (asks); u undoes r Restore (in the trash) Board and week: H L Board: column left / right H L Week: a day earlier / later [ ] Week: previous / next week t Week: this week Typing: Enter Esc Save / cancel Tab S-Tab Next / previous field (edit) ← → C-a C-e Move the cursor; start / end C-u C-k C-w Delete to start / to end / a word The layout and keys owe a lot to Backlog.md (github.com/MrLesk/Backlog.md, MIT) and lazygit; no code is taken from either."},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"Command details","u":"cli.html#command-details","x":""},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand today","u":"cli.html#dayhand-today","x":"The Today screen: now task, overdue, today and done today (also plain dayhand) dayhand today dayhand dayhand today --json dayhand today --plain --fields ref,title"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand add","u":"cli.html#dayhand-add","x":"Add a task (due today unless --due or --no-due) dayhand add <title...> [options] title: Task title Option Meaning --due <when> Due day, optionally with a time: tomorrow, fri 14:00, in 3 days, 2026-10-23 (dayhand help dates) --time <HH:MM> Due time, 24h, in your time zone --every <unit> Repeat every day, week, month or year (day, week, month, year) --interval <n> Repeat interval (2 = every other) --on <days> Weekly repeat days, e.g. mon,thu --month-day <day> Monthly repeat on day 1-31 (a shorter month: its last day) or last --month-weekday <day> Monthly repeat on a weekday: \"2 tue\", \"last fri\" --from-completion Count the next date from the day the task is done, not its due date --until <date> Last date the repeat may fall on (dayhand help dates) --count <n> How many times it repeats, this one included --rrule <rule> The rule as RFC 5545 RRULE instead, e.g. \"FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=-1FR\" --no-due No due day: the task shows only in list and board -l, --label <label> Label (repeatable) --note <text> Note --status <status> Initial status (todo, doing) --resume-note <text> Where you stopped / next step --link <url> Link, optionally followed by a title: \" Title\" (repeatable) --item <text> Checklist item (repeatable, in order) --remind <when> This task's reminder instead of the defaults: 0, 15m, 2h, 1d (before the due time), 09:00 or \"18:00 -1d\" (repeatable) --no-reminders No reminders for this task --accept <text> Acceptance criterion: what done means (repeatable, in order) --plan <text> How the work will be done --depends-on <ref> A task that must be done first (repeatable) --blocked <reason> Why the task cannot go on; a blocked task is not ready --propose A proposal: it waits in the owner's inbox, out of Today, until accepted --source <source> Where it comes from, \"<kind>:<ref>\" optionally followed by a URL; one source makes one task --requested-by <who> Who asked for it --return-to <webhookId> A webhook that hears, with the proof, when the task closes (dayhand webhook list) dayhand add Call the accountant --due \"fri 2pm\" -l home dayhand add Pay rent --due \"1 nov\" --dry-run dayhand add \"Review the plan\" --time 09:00 --every week --on mon,thu dayhand add Payroll --due 2026-10-30 --month-weekday \"last fri\" --remind \"09:00 -1d\" dayhand add Water plants --every day --interval 3 --from-completion dayhand add Pack --due fri --item passport --item charger dayhand add Buy a present --no-due --json dayhand add \"Ship the export\" --no-due --accept \"CSV opens in a spreadsheet\" --plan \"write, then test\" --depends-on 41 dayhand add \"Checkout times out\" --propose --source \"sentry:CHECKOUT-42"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand list","u":"cli.html#dayhand-list","x":"Open tasks, oldest due first (with --done also done ones); filters narrow the list Aliases: ls. dayhand list [options] Option Meaning -l, --label <label> Only tasks with this label (repeatable: all must match) --done Include done tasks --archived Include archived tasks --status <statuses> Only these statuses, comma-separated: todo, doing, done (done implies --done) --overdue Only overdue tasks --ready Only tasks ready to work on: not done, not blocked, every dependency done (backend 0.10.0) --due-before <when> Only tasks due before this day, e.g. eow, next mon, 2026-11-01 --due-after <when> Only tasks due after this day, e.g. today --search <text> Only tasks whose title, note, resume note or labels contain the text (any case) --limit <n> At most this many tasks; the JSON's nextCursor continues the list --cursor <cursor> Continue a list cut by --limit: the nextCursor of the previous page dayhand list -l home --status todo,doing dayhand list --overdue dayhand list --due-before eow --search accountant --limit 20 dayhand list --limit 50 --cursor <nextCursor> --json dayhand list --done --json --fields ref,title,status dayhand list --ready --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand board","u":"cli.html#dayhand-board","x":"Kanban board: todo, doing and done columns dayhand board [options] Option Meaning --done-limit <n> Maximum tasks in the done column (default 50) dayhand board dayhand board --done-limit 5 --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand show","u":"cli.html#dayhand-show","x":"One task with its change history (who changed what, when) dayhand show <ref> ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id dayhand show 42 dayhand show now --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand edit","u":"cli.html#dayhand-edit","x":"Change a task's fields; --clear-* removes one; on a terminal without options, edit title and note in $EDITOR dayhand edit <ref> [options] ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id Option Meaning --title <text> New title --note <text> Replace the note --clear-note Remove the note --due <when> Due day, optionally with a time: tomorrow, fri 14:00, in 3 days, 2026-10-23 (dayhand help dates) --time <HH:MM> Due time, 24h, in your time zone --every <unit> Repeat every day, week, month or year (day, week, month, year) --interval <n> Repeat interval (2 = every other) --on <days> Weekly repeat days, e.g. mon,thu --month-day <day> Monthly repeat on day 1-31 (a shorter month: its last day) or last --month-weekday <day> Monthly repeat on a weekday: \"2 tue\", \"last fri\" --from-completion Count the next date from the day the task is done, not its due date --until <date> Last date the repeat may fall on (dayhand help dates) --count <n> How many times it repeats, this one included --rrule <rule> The rule as RFC 5545 RRULE instead, e.g. \"FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=-1FR\" --clear-due Remove the due day (and time) --clear-time Remove the due time --clear-repeat Stop repeating --resume-note <text> Where you stopped / next step --clear-resume-note Remove the resume note -l, --label <label> Replace all labels (repeatable) --add-label <label> Add a label (repeatable) --rm-label <label> Remove a label (repeatable) --clear-labels Remove all labels --link <url> Replace all links: \" Title\" (repeatable) --clear-links Remove all links --item <text> Replace the checklist (repeatable; dayhand checklist edits single items) --clear-checklist Remove the checklist --remind <when> This task's reminder instead of the defaults: 0, 15m, 2h, 1d (before the due time), 09:00 or \"18:00 -1d\" (repeatable) --no-reminders No reminders for this task --default-reminders Back to the default reminders from settings --accept <text> Replace the acceptance criteria (repeatable; dayhand criteria edits single ones) --clear-acceptance Remove the acceptance criteria --plan <text> How the work will be done --clear-plan Remove the plan --depends-on <ref> Replace the dependencies: a task that must be done first (repeatable) --clear-depends Remove all dependencies --blocked <reason> Why the task cannot go on; a blocked task is not ready --unblock Remove the blocked reason dayhand edit 42 --title \"Call the bank\" --due \"tomorrow 10:00\" dayhand edit 42 --add-label work --rm-label home dayhand edit 42 --every month --month-day last --until 2027-06-30 dayhand edit 42 --rrule \"FREQ=WEEKLY;INTERVAL=2;BYDAY=MO\" dayhand edit 42 --remind 15m --remind 1d dayhand edit 42 --clear-repeat dayhand edit 42 --blocked \"waiting for the client's brief\" dayhand edit 42 --depends-on 40 --depends-on 41 dayhand edit 42"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand note","u":"cli.html#dayhand-note","x":"Print a task's note (on a terminal: edit it in $VISUAL/$EDITOR), or append text to it dayhand note <ref> [text...] [options] ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id text: Text to append as a new line Option Meaning --clear Remove the note dayhand note 42 dayhand note 42 \"Account number is in the email\" dayhand note 42 --clear"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand checklist","u":"cli.html#dayhand-checklist","x":"Print a task's checklist, or tick, untick, move, remove and add items Items are numbered from 1 as printed. Changes apply in this order: --check, --uncheck, --move, --rm, then --add. dayhand checklist <ref> [options] ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id Option Meaning --add <text> Add an item at the end (repeatable) --check <n> Tick items: 2, 1,3 or all (repeatable) --uncheck <n> Untick items (repeatable) --move <from:to> Move an item, e.g. 3:1 (repeatable) --rm <n> Remove items (repeatable) --clear Remove the whole checklist dayhand checklist 42 dayhand checklist 42 --add \"Book the hotel\" --add \"Pack\" dayhand checklist 42 --check 1,2 dayhand checklist 42 --move 3:1 --rm 4"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand move","u":"cli.html#dayhand-move","x":"Move a task to another day, optionally with a time (none removes the date) dayhand move <ref> <date> [options] ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id date: When: tomorrow, fri 14:00, next mon, 2026-10-23 … or none (dayhand help dates) Option Meaning --time <HH:MM> New due time --clear-time Remove the due time dayhand move 42 tomorrow --time 08:00 dayhand move 42 \"next mon 9am\" dayhand move 42 none"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand done","u":"cli.html#dayhand-done","x":"Mark tasks done, optionally with proof; a repeating task gets its next occurrence An agent completing a task with acceptance criteria needs every criterion met (dayhand criteria) and --summary; otherwise it fails with precondition_failed (exit 6). dayhand done <refs...> [options] refs: Tasks: #42 or 42, now, or ids Option Meaning --summary <text> Proof: what was done (an agent needs it for a task with acceptance criteria) --check <check> Proof: a check and its result, \"<name>=pass\" or \"<name>=fail\", optionally followed by a URL (repeatable) --proof-link <url> Proof: where to see the result, \" Title\" (repeatable) dayhand done 42 dayhand done 42 43 now dayhand done 1 2 3 --yes --client-id 7f3c dayhand done 42 --summary \"Export shipped\" --check \"unit tests=pass --proof-link \" PR\""},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand start","u":"cli.html#dayhand-start","x":"Set tasks to doing dayhand start <refs...> refs: Tasks: #42 or 42, now, or ids dayhand start 42"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand stop","u":"cli.html#dayhand-stop","x":"Set tasks back to todo (from doing) dayhand stop <refs...> refs: Tasks: #42 or 42, now, or ids dayhand stop 42"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand reopen","u":"cli.html#dayhand-reopen","x":"Set done tasks back to todo dayhand reopen <refs...> refs: Tasks: #42 or 42, now, or ids dayhand reopen 42"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand now","u":"cli.html#dayhand-now","x":"Show the now task, make a task the now task (--note: where you stopped), or --clear dayhand now [ref] [options] ref: Task to make the now task Option Meaning --note <text> Resume note shown on Today --clear Clear the now task dayhand now dayhand now 42 --note \"have the account number\" dayhand now --clear"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand archive","u":"cli.html#dayhand-archive","x":"Archive tasks: off Today and the board, kept in exports dayhand archive <refs...> refs: Tasks: #42 or 42, now, or ids dayhand archive 42"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand rm","u":"cli.html#dayhand-rm","x":"Move tasks to the trash (purged after 30 days) Aliases: delete. dayhand rm <refs...> refs: Tasks: #42 or 42, now, or ids dayhand rm 42 dayhand rm 4 5 6 --yes"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand restore","u":"cli.html#dayhand-restore","x":"Restore tasks from the trash (or the archive) dayhand restore <refs...> refs: Tasks: #42 or 42, now, or ids dayhand restore 42"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand trash","u":"cli.html#dayhand-trash","x":"Tasks in the trash, newest first dayhand trash dayhand trash dayhand trash --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand log","u":"cli.html#dayhand-log","x":"The activity log: task changes after a cursor or a time, oldest first (who changed what) Without --since: the newest changes. Keep nextCursor and pass it as --since next time to see only what changed meanwhile; hasMore says there are more already. dayhand log [options] Option Meaning --since <from> nextCursor of an earlier log (c…), a time ago (30m, 2h, 3d), a date phrase (yesterday, mon 09:00) or an ISO time --by <actor> Only changes by app, cli, agent (any agent) or \"agent: <name>\" --task <ref> Only changes of this task --limit <n> At most this many changes, 1 to 500 (default 100) dayhand log dayhand log --since 2h --by cli dayhand log --since c1793011200000123 --json dayhand log --task 42 --by \"agent: claude-code\""},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand claim","u":"cli.html#dayhand-claim","x":"Claim a task for a while (default 120 minutes) so other agents leave it alone While the claim lasts another agent's changes and claims of the task fail with conflict (exit 6). Claiming it again extends your claim. dayhand claim <ref> [options] ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id Option Meaning --minutes <n> Length of the claim in minutes from now, 1 to 1440 (default 120) dayhand claim 42 dayhand claim 42 --minutes 30 --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand renew","u":"cli.html#dayhand-renew","x":"Extend your claim of a task by --minutes from now (default 120) dayhand renew <ref> [options] ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id Option Meaning --minutes <n> Length of the claim in minutes from now, 1 to 1440 (default 120) dayhand renew 42 dayhand renew 42 --minutes 60"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand release","u":"cli.html#dayhand-release","x":"End your claim of a task, optionally leaving a checkpoint as its resume note dayhand release <ref> [options] ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id Option Meaning --note <text> Checkpoint: where you stopped and the next step (saved as the resume note) dayhand release 42 dayhand release 42 --note \"tables migrated; indexes next\""},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand criteria","u":"cli.html#dayhand-criteria","x":"Print a task's acceptance criteria, or mark them met, unmet, remove and add them Criteria are numbered from 1 as printed. Changes apply in this order: --met, --unmet, --rm, then --add. An agent can complete a task only when every criterion is met. dayhand criteria <ref> [options] ref: Task: #42 or 42 (its number), now, or its id Option Meaning --add <text> Add a criterion at the end (repeatable) --met <n> Mark criteria met: 2, 1,3 or all (repeatable) --unmet <n> Mark criteria not met (repeatable) --rm <n> Remove criteria (repeatable) --clear Remove all criteria dayhand criteria 42 dayhand criteria 42 --add \"Tests pass\" --add \"Docs updated\" dayhand criteria 42 --met 1,2"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand ask","u":"cli.html#dayhand-ask","x":"Ask the owner to decide before you do something; they answer on the phone Give 2 to 4 --option, each \"label: consequence\". Read the answer with dayhand approval <id> (--wait to wait for it). Dayhand records the decision; acting on it is up to you. Needs backend 0.11.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand ask <action...> [options] action: What you want to do, as one short sentence Option Meaning --option <text> A choice, \"label: what happens then\" (repeatable, 2 to 4) --detail <text> Why, and what the owner needs to know --task <ref> The task it is about --reversible What you want to do can be undone (default: it cannot) --expires <span> How long the owner has: minutes, 30m, 2h, 1d (default 1d, at most 7d) dayhand ask Deploy 2.4 to production --option \"Deploy now: live in 10 minutes\" --option \"Wait: nothing changes until Monday\" --expires 2h dayhand ask Delete old backups --task 42 --option Yes --option No --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand approval","u":"cli.html#dayhand-approval","x":"One approval request and, once decided, the owner's choice --wait <seconds> checks every 5 s until the request is decided or expired, or the time is up. Needs backend 0.11.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand approval <id> [options] id: Id of the approval request Option Meaning --wait <seconds> Wait up to this many seconds for a decision dayhand approval <id> dayhand approval <id> --wait 600 --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand approvals","u":"cli.html#dayhand-approvals","x":"Approval requests, newest first Needs backend 0.11.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand approvals [options] Option Meaning --status <status> Only pending, decided or expired requests (pending, decided, expired) --task <ref> Only requests about this task --limit <n> At most this many, 1 to 200 (default 50) dayhand approvals --status pending dayhand approvals --task 42 --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand approvals decide","u":"cli.html#dayhand-approvals-decide","x":"Pick an option of a pending approval request Only the owner can do this: a cli key (an agent key gets exit 7, forbidden). dayhand approvals decide <id> <option> [options] id: Id of the approval request option: Id of the chosen option (o1, allow, …) Option Meaning --note <text> A word for the agent with the decision dayhand approvals decide <id> o2 --note \"after the weekend\" dayhand approvals decide <id> allow"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand agents","u":"cli.html#dayhand-agents","x":"Whether agents are paused, and the tool policies of agent keys Needs backend 0.11.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand agents dayhand agents dayhand agents pause --reason holiday dayhand agents resume"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand agents pause","u":"cli.html#dayhand-agents-pause","x":"Pause every agent: they can read, their changes are refused until you resume them Only the owner can do this: a cli key (an agent key gets exit 7, forbidden). dayhand agents pause [options] Option Meaning --reason <text> Why; agents see it in the error dayhand agents pause dayhand agents pause --reason \"going on holiday\""},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand agents resume","u":"cli.html#dayhand-agents-resume","x":"Let agents change the plan again Only the owner can do this: a cli key (an agent key gets exit 7, forbidden). dayhand agents resume dayhand agents resume"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand policy","u":"cli.html#dayhand-policy","x":"Tool policies of agent keys: allow, ask (the owner approves each call) or deny Needs backend 0.11.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand policy dayhand policy dayhand policy set <keyId> delete_task deny dayhand policy set <keyId> \"*\" ask"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand policy set","u":"cli.html#dayhand-policy-set","x":"Set one agent key's rule for a tool that changes the plan, or \"*\" for every such tool The key's id comes from dayhand key list. allow removes the rule (unless \"*\" says otherwise). Only the owner can do this: a cli key (an agent key gets exit 7, forbidden). dayhand policy set <key> <tool> <rule> key: Id of an agent API key tool: A tool that changes the plan (dayhand tools), or \"*\" rule: allow, ask or deny dayhand policy set <keyId> complete_task ask"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand proposals","u":"cli.html#dayhand-proposals","x":"The proposal inbox: tasks agents and integrations proposed, waiting for you Accept, merge or reject them with the subcommands; proposals stay out of Today, list and the board until accepted. Needs backend 0.11.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand proposals dayhand proposals dayhand proposals accept 42 dayhand proposals merge 43 --into 12 dayhand proposals reject 44 --reason \"not now\""},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand proposals accept","u":"cli.html#dayhand-proposals-accept","x":"Accept a proposal: it joins the plan as it is Only the owner can do this: a cli key (an agent key gets exit 7, forbidden). dayhand proposals accept <ref> ref: The proposal: #42 or 42 dayhand proposals accept 42"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand proposals merge","u":"cli.html#dayhand-proposals-merge","x":"Merge a proposal into a task already in the plan; the proposal goes to the trash The task gets the proposal's note, links, labels, source and return webhooks. Only the owner can do this: a cli key (an agent key gets exit 7, forbidden). dayhand proposals merge <ref> [options] ref: The proposal: #42 or 42 Option Meaning --into <ref> The task to merge it into (required) dayhand proposals merge 43 --into 12"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand proposals reject","u":"cli.html#dayhand-proposals-reject","x":"Reject a proposal: it goes to the trash (restorable for 30 days) Only the owner can do this: a cli key (an agent key gets exit 7, forbidden). dayhand proposals reject <ref> [options] ref: The proposal: #42 or 42 Option Meaning --reason <text> Why; the return webhook gets it dayhand proposals reject 44 --reason \"not this quarter\""},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand health","u":"cli.html#dayhand-health","x":"What needs a look: overdue, blocked, stale claims, approvals, proposals, done without proof Each section counts every task and lists the first 20. Needs backend 0.11.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand health dayhand health dayhand health --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand settings","u":"cli.html#dayhand-settings","x":"Show settings (time zone, morning summary time, default reminders) dayhand settings dayhand settings dayhand settings --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand settings get","u":"cli.html#dayhand-settings-get","x":"Show settings dayhand settings get dayhand settings get --fields timezone"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand settings set","u":"cli.html#dayhand-settings-set","x":"Change settings: timezone, summaryTime (summary-time), defaultReminderMinutes (reminders) dayhand settings set <pairs...> pairs: key=value pairs dayhand settings set timezone=Europe/London summary-time=05:00 dayhand settings set reminders=0,15"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand export","u":"cli.html#dayhand-export","x":"Export the whole plan as Markdown, JSON (with history) or iCalendar (ics) dayhand export [options] Option Meaning --format <format> md, json or ics (default: md, or json with --json) (md, markdown, json, ics) -o, --output <file> Write to a file instead of stdout dayhand export > plan.md dayhand export --format json -o dayhand.json dayhand export --format ics -o dayhand.ics dayhand export --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand tools","u":"cli.html#dayhand-tools","x":"MCP tools and their input schemas (introspection) dayhand tools [name] name: One tool, to print its schema dayhand tools dayhand tools update_task --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand call","u":"cli.html#dayhand-call","x":"Call an MCP tool with JSON arguments, exactly as an MCP client would Prints the tool's structuredContent (or, for a Markdown export, its text). Errors and exit codes are the same as for the other commands. dayhand call <tool> [json] tool: Tool name (see dayhand tools) json: Arguments as a JSON object, or - to read them from stdin (default {}) dayhand call get_task '{\"taskId\":\"#42\"}' echo '{\"format\":\"json\"}' | dayhand call export_plan -"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand version","u":"cli.html#dayhand-version","x":"CLI and server versions, and whether they are compatible dayhand version dayhand version --json dayhand --version"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand pair","u":"cli.html#dayhand-pair","x":"One-time code (10 minutes) that signs the phone app in as you dayhand pair [options] Option Meaning --no-qr Do not draw the QR code dayhand pair dayhand pair --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand device","u":"cli.html#dayhand-device","x":"Paired phones (same as dayhand device list); revoke one with dayhand device revoke dayhand device dayhand device dayhand device revoke <deviceId>"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand device list","u":"cli.html#dayhand-device-list","x":"Paired phones Aliases: ls. dayhand device list dayhand device list"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand device revoke","u":"cli.html#dayhand-device-revoke","x":"Sign a phone out: it gets no new app tokens dayhand device revoke <deviceId> deviceId: Device id from dayhand device list dayhand device revoke <deviceId>"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand devices","u":"cli.html#dayhand-devices","x":"Same as dayhand device list dayhand devices dayhand devices"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand connection","u":"cli.html#dayhand-connection","x":"Agent connections approved on the phone (OAuth, e.g. the Claude app); revoke one with dayhand connection revoke An app such as Claude connects through OAuth: its sign-in page shows a code, and you approve it in the Dayhand app (Settings → Agent connections). API keys are not connections; see dayhand whoami. Needs backend 0.6.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand connection dayhand connection dayhand connection revoke <connectionId>"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand connection list","u":"cli.html#dayhand-connection-list","x":"Agent connections Aliases: ls. dayhand connection list dayhand connection list --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand connection revoke","u":"cli.html#dayhand-connection-revoke","x":"Disconnect an agent: its tokens stop working at once dayhand connection revoke <connectionId> connectionId: Connection id from dayhand connection list dayhand connection revoke <connectionId>"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand connections","u":"cli.html#dayhand-connections","x":"Same as dayhand connection list dayhand connections dayhand connections"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand revoke","u":"cli.html#dayhand-revoke","x":"Same as dayhand device revoke dayhand revoke <deviceId> deviceId: Device id from dayhand device list dayhand revoke <deviceId>"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand calendar","u":"cli.html#dayhand-calendar","x":"Your tasks in Google or Apple Calendar: the secret address of the read-only feed (same as dayhand calendar url) Dated tasks become calendar events (with a time) or all-day events, repeating ones with their rule, reminders as alerts. The address works without a key, so treat it like a password. Needs backend 0.7.0 or later (dayhand version). dayhand calendar dayhand calendar dayhand calendar on dayhand calendar rotate --yes dayhand calendar export -o dayhand.ics"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand calendar url","u":"cli.html#dayhand-calendar-url","x":"Show the feed's addresses (null when it is off) dayhand calendar url dayhand calendar url --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand calendar on","u":"cli.html#dayhand-calendar-on","x":"Turn the feed on (keeps an existing address) and show it dayhand calendar on dayhand calendar on"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand calendar rotate","u":"cli.html#dayhand-calendar-rotate","x":"A new secret address; the old one stops working at once dayhand calendar rotate dayhand calendar rotate --yes"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand calendar off","u":"cli.html#dayhand-calendar-off","x":"Turn the feed off; its address stops working dayhand calendar off dayhand calendar off --yes"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand calendar export","u":"cli.html#dayhand-calendar-export","x":"The plan as a one-time .ics file to import (does not need the feed) dayhand calendar export [options] Option Meaning -o, --output <file> Write to a file instead of stdout (e.g. dayhand.ics) dayhand calendar export -o dayhand.ics dayhand calendar export > dayhand.ics"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand key","u":"cli.html#dayhand-key","x":"API keys for integrations: list, create with a scope and an expiry, revoke (same as dayhand key list) A key made here reads (scope read) or also changes tasks and settings (scope write), never more; it expires no later than the key that makes it and stops working when that key is revoked. Needs backend 0.8.0 or later and a key with scope full (dayhand version, dayhand key list). dayhand key dayhand key list dayhand key create n8n --scope read --expires 90d dayhand key revoke <keyId> --yes"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand key list","u":"cli.html#dayhand-key-list","x":"Every API key with its kind, scope and expiry (never the key itself) dayhand key list dayhand key list --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand key create","u":"cli.html#dayhand-key-create","x":"Create a key (printed once) that reads or writes, for an integration dayhand key create <name> [options] name: What the key is for, shown in history (e.g. n8n) Option Meaning --scope <scope> read (default) or write (read, write) --kind <kind> agent (default) or cli: the actor of its changes (agent, cli) --expires <when> 90d (default), 12w, 1y, a date YYYY-MM-DD or never dayhand key create n8n dayhand key create shortcuts --scope write --expires 1y dayhand key create backup --expires never --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand key revoke","u":"cli.html#dayhand-key-revoke","x":"Revoke a key; the keys it created stop working too dayhand key revoke <keyId> keyId: Key id from dayhand key list dayhand key revoke <keyId> --yes"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand webhook","u":"cli.html#dayhand-webhook","x":"Webhooks: HTTPS addresses that get a signed message for every task change (same as dayhand webhook list) Messages follow Standard Webhooks (webhook-id, webhook-timestamp, webhook-signature); see docs/webhooks.md. Needs backend 0.8.0 or later and a key with scope full (dayhand version, dayhand key list). dayhand webhook dayhand webhook list dayhand webhook add --event task.completed dayhand webhook deliveries <webhookId>"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand webhook list","u":"cli.html#dayhand-webhook-list","x":"Every webhook, whether it is on, and why it was turned off dayhand webhook list dayhand webhook list --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand webhook add","u":"cli.html#dayhand-webhook-add","x":"Add a webhook; prints its signing secret once dayhand webhook add <url> [options] url: HTTPS address on a public host Option Meaning -e, --event <type> Event to send (repeatable; default all): task.created, task.updated, task.completed, task.deleted, task.restored, now.changed (task.created, task.updated, task.completed, task.deleted, task.restored, now.changed) -d, --description <text> What it is for dayhand webhook add dayhand webhook add -e task.completed -e task.created -d n8n"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand webhook rm","u":"cli.html#dayhand-webhook-rm","x":"Remove a webhook and its delivery log dayhand webhook rm <webhookId> webhookId: Webhook id from dayhand webhook list dayhand webhook rm <webhookId> --yes"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand webhook test","u":"cli.html#dayhand-webhook-test","x":"Send a webhook.test message now (also to a webhook that is off) dayhand webhook test <webhookId> webhookId: Webhook id from dayhand webhook list dayhand webhook test <webhookId> dayhand webhook deliveries <webhookId>"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand webhook enable","u":"cli.html#dayhand-webhook-enable","x":"Turn a webhook back on after it was turned off for failing dayhand webhook enable <webhookId> webhookId: Webhook id from dayhand webhook list dayhand webhook enable <webhookId>"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand webhook deliveries","u":"cli.html#dayhand-webhook-deliveries","x":"The delivery log: newest first, each attempt's HTTP status or error dayhand webhook deliveries <webhookId> [options] webhookId: Webhook id from dayhand webhook list Option Meaning -n, --limit <n> How many (1 to 200, default 50) dayhand webhook deliveries <webhookId> dayhand webhook deliveries <webhookId> -n 5 --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand import","u":"cli.html#dayhand-import","x":"Import tasks from Dayhand's JSON export, an iCalendar file or a Todoist CSV export Each task is checked like dayhand add; one that fails is listed and the rest are imported. Importing the same file again imports nothing twice. --dry-run asks the server what would happen without storing anything. Needs backend 0.8.0 or later and a key with scope full (dayhand version, dayhand key list). dayhand import <file> [options] file: The file, or - for stdin Option Meaning --format <format> auto (default), dayhand-json, ics or todoist-csv (auto, dayhand-json, helm-json, ics, todoist-csv) dayhand export --format json -o dayhand.json && dayhand import dayhand.json --dry-run dayhand import todoist.csv dayhand import calendar.ics --format ics --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand login","u":"cli.html#dayhand-login","x":"Store an API key for the deployment in the OS keychain (or a private file) dayhand server deploy and dayhand server start sign in by themselves. For another computer, create a key with kind cli where dayhand is signed in (dayhand key create laptop --kind cli --scope write) or with npx convex run apiKeys:create. The key is checked against the server first; DAYHAND_API_KEY, when set, still wins over the stored key. dayhand login [options] Option Meaning --with-key Read the key from stdin instead of a hidden prompt --store <store> Where to keep the key (default: keychain, else file) (keychain, file) dayhand login --url dayhand login --with-key < ~/dayhand-key.txt dayhand --profile work login"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand logout","u":"cli.html#dayhand-logout","x":"Remove the stored API key for the deployment (keychain and file) dayhand logout dayhand logout dayhand --profile work logout --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand whoami","u":"cli.html#dayhand-whoami","x":"Which deployment, profile, key source and config file dayhand uses, checked against the server dayhand whoami dayhand whoami dayhand whoami --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand server deploy","u":"cli.html#dayhand-server-deploy","x":"Set up or update your server on your own Convex account, sign dayhand in and make a pairing code Logs in to Convex in the browser, creates a project with a production deployment (or uses --deployment), sets the app-signing key and DAYHAND_* variables, deploys, checks /api/v1/openapi.json, /auth/token and /mcp, and makes a CLI key and a pairing code. Run it again to update: keys, the CLI key and the data are kept; a different signing key is replaced only after a question, the issuer never. --dry-run shows the steps without network. dayhand server deploy [options] Option Meaning --team <slug> Convex team (default: your only team; asked when you have several) --project <name> Name of the new Convex project (default: dayhand) --deployment <name> Use this existing production deployment instead of a new project --issuer <url> DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER for a new server (default: the server's address) --backend <folder> The Dayhand repository's backend/ folder (default: found from here) dayhand server deploy dayhand server deploy --team my-team --project dayhand dayhand server deploy --dry-run"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand server start","u":"cli.html#dayhand-server-start","x":"Run your server on this computer (Convex local backend, no account) until Ctrl+C, sign dayhand in macOS and Linux. Data and keys stay in DAYHAND_SERVER_CONFIG_DIR/local, so the same command starts the server again. A phone with a release build needs HTTPS: put the ports behind tailscale serve and pass --public-url (docs/deploy.md). With --json the result is printed when the server is ready. dayhand server start [options] Option Meaning --port <n> Convex API port (default: 3210) --site-port <n> HTTP actions port (default: 3211) --public-url <url> The HTTPS address phones use, e.g. --public-api-url <url> The Convex API's HTTPS address (default: --public-url at port 8443) --backend <folder> The Dayhand repository's backend/ folder (default: found from here) dayhand server start dayhand server start --public-url dayhand server start --json --yes"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand prime","u":"cli.html#dayhand-prime","x":"Context for an AI agent: Today and how to use dayhand (for a SessionStart hook) Prints plain text without colors in every format except --json, which prints list_today like dayhand today --json. In a Claude Code hook: {\"hooks\":{\"SessionStart\":[{\"hooks\":[{\"type\":\"command\",\"command\":\"dayhand prime\"}]}]}}. dayhand prime dayhand prime dayhand prime --json"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand when","u":"cli.html#dayhand-when","x":"Show the day (and time) a date phrase means in your time zone, without changing anything dayhand when <phrase...> [options] phrase: A date phrase, e.g. fri 14:00 (quotes optional) Option Meaning --timezone <zone> IANA time zone to resolve in (default: yours from the server settings) --now <moment> Resolve as if it were this moment (ISO 8601) instead of now dayhand when \"fri 14:00\" dayhand when next mon --json dayhand when \"in 2 hours\" --timezone America/New_York"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand completion","u":"cli.html#dayhand-completion","x":"Print the shell completion script for bash, zsh, fish or powershell (dayhand help completion) dayhand completion <shell> shell: bash, zsh, fish, powershell source <(dayhand completion bash) dayhand completion zsh > \"${fpath[1]}/_dayhand\" dayhand completion powershell | Out-String | Invoke-Expression"},{"p":"dayhand command reference","t":"dayhand ui","u":"cli.html#dayhand-ui","x":"Dayhand full screen in the terminal: Today, board, week and trash, with keys like vim and lazygit (dayhand help ui) Needs a terminal. Refreshes every 12 s by default, within the key's limits of 120 reads and 60 changes a minute. q quits, ? lists the keys; dayhand help ui prints them. The screens speak the terminal's language (LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES or LANG, overridden by DAYHAND_LANG or --lang) when it is one of en, pl, de, es, fr, pt-BR, ja, zh-Hans, else English; date phrases stay English. dayhand ui [options] Option Meaning --view <view> The view to open with (today, board, week, trash) --interval <seconds> Seconds between refreshes, 10 to 300 (default 12) --no-mouse Leave the mouse to the terminal (for selecting text) --lang <language> The screens' language: en, pl, de, es, fr, pt-BR, ja, zh-Hans (default: DAYHAND_LANG, then the locale) dayhand ui dayhand ui --view week dayhand ui --interval 30 --no-mouse dayhand ui --lang pl"},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"Agents and you","u":"agents.html","x":"Dayhand lets AI agents work on your plan next to you: read Today, add and plan tasks, claim one, finish it with proof. You stay in charge: agents ask before risky steps, one switch stops all of them, a policy per agent says which tools it may use, and work nobody asked for waits in an inbox. This page shows how to set that up and use it. The reasons behind each feature are in how agent work is designed, the exact tools in MCP endpoint. What Dayhand can and cannot do. Dayhand records every change an agent makes in your plan and enforces your controls on its own tools. It cannot see or stop what an agent does outside Dayhand: an approval you give is a record the agent is expected to respect, not a lock on its other tools."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"Give each agent its own key","u":"agents.html#give-each-agent-its-own-key","x":"Every change is signed with who made it: app (you in an app), cli (you with dayhand) or agent: <name>. An agent gets a key of its own, so its changes show its name and you can revoke it alone: AGENT_KEY=$(dayhand key create claude --scope write --json | jq -er .key) dayhand key list dayhand key revoke <keyId> Dayhand returns the key only this once; save the captured $AGENT_KEY in a password manager so you can set it again in a later shell. The examples use jq to read JSON responses. Scope The key may read (the default of dayhand key create) read the plan: Today, lists, tasks, history, the activity log write also change tasks and settings full also manage keys, webhooks, devices, connections and the calendar feed; dayhand key create never makes one A key made with dayhand key create expires after 90 days unless --expires says otherwise (12w, 1y, a date, never), never outlives the key that made it, and stops when that key is revoked. Each key may make 120 reads and 60 changes a minute. More: keys and actors."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"Connect the agent","u":"agents.html#connect-the-agent","x":"Claude Code and other MCP clients that can send a header connect to your server's /mcp address with the key: claude mcp add --transport http dayhand --header \"Authorization: Bearer $AGENT_KEY\" On a server on your Convex account, the address is Any other client works the same way: the address and an Authorization: Bearer <key> header. The Claude apps (claude.ai, Claude Desktop, Claude for Android and iOS) add Dayhand as a custom connector and sign in with OAuth instead of a key. You approve each connection on your phone, by the code the sign-in page shows. This needs a server with a public HTTPS address (dayhand server deploy): Claude apps (OAuth). Agents that run shell commands can use dayhand with the key in DAYHAND_API_KEY (and the server in DAYHAND_URL). With --json it prints exactly what the MCP tools return, and its exit codes say what to do next (for scripts). Two helpers: A ready Agent Skill teaches an agent the commands and the rules: mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills && cp -R docs/skills/dayhand ~/.claude/skills/ from the checkout. dayhand prime prints Today and a short guide as plain text, for a session-start hook. In Claude Code's settings: {\"hooks\":{\"SessionStart\":[{\"hooks\":[{\"type\":\"command\",\"command\":\"dayhand prime\"}]}]}}."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"What the agent sees","u":"agents.html#what-the-agent-sees","x":"Over MCP your server offers 32 tools, three resources with two resource templates, and four prompts (the full list): Tools for everything the apps do: add, update, complete, reschedule, archive, delete and restore tasks; Today, search, the board and the trash; the activity log, claims, approval requests, proposals and the health report. Tools that only you may use (deciding approvals, pausing agents, policies, accepting proposals) are left out of an agent's list. Resources: and a short text on how an agent should work with Dayhand; the templates and read the log from a cursor and one task with its history. Prompts: plan_day, weekly_review, triage_overdue and plan_health, each of which asks the agent to go through the plan with you and ask before changing anything. Every change takes dryRun: true to check it without saving, and a clientId so that a retry is never applied twice (dry runs)."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"One task, start to finish","u":"agents.html#one-task-start-to-finish","x":"Tasks can say what done means (acceptance criteria) and how the work will go (a plan). An agent claims a task so other agents leave it alone, ticks the criteria and finishes with proof: TASK_ID=$(dayhand add \"Send the Q4 invoices\" --due \"today 17:00\" --accept \"One PDF per client\" --plan \"Export, then check the totals\" --json | jq -er .id) DAYHAND_API_KEY=$AGENT_KEY dayhand claim \"$TASK_ID\" --minutes 30 DAYHAND_API_KEY=$AGENT_KEY dayhand criteria \"$TASK_ID\" --met all DAYHAND_API_KEY=$AGENT_KEY dayhand done \"$TASK_ID\" --summary \"3 PDFs sent\" --check totals=pass dayhand log --by agent Acceptance criteria and proof. When a task has criteria, an agent can complete it only when every criterion is met and the proof has a summary; otherwise the call fails (precondition_failed, exit 6) and nothing changes. The proof (a summary, links, checks that passed or failed) stays on the task and shows in the phone's task details. You complete tasks as always: the rule binds agents, not you. Claims are leases. dayhand claim holds a task for a while (120 minutes unless --minutes says 1 to 1440). While it lasts, another agent's changes to the task fail with conflict (exit 6), so it picks another. The holder extends it with dayhand renew and ends it with dayhand release --note \"<where I stopped>\", which becomes the task's resume note. A claim that runs out ends by itself. Your own changes always go through, and the phone can take a task over at once. Order. --depends-on names tasks that must be done first and --blocked says why a task cannot go on. dayhand list --ready lists what can be worked on now. Catching up. dayhand log lists every change in order with who made it. An agent keeps the nextCursor and passes it as --since next time to read only what changed meanwhile."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"Approvals: agents ask, you decide on the phone","u":"agents.html#approvals-agents-ask-you-decide-on-the-phone","x":"Before a risky or irreversible step, an agent asks with two to four options and what each one leads to: dayhand ask Deploy 2.4 to production --option \"Deploy now: live in 10 minutes\" --option \"Wait: nothing changes until Monday\" --expires 2h dayhand approval <id> --wait 600 --json The request shows on your phone (Settings → Agents, the health card above Today, and a notification) and in dayhand approvals. You pick an option, optionally with a note; from a terminal with a cli key: dayhand approvals decide <id> o2 --note \"after the weekend\". The agent reads the outcome with dayhand approval <id> (or the MCP tool get_approval), or hears it through the webhook approval.decided. A request expires after a day unless it says otherwise (a week at most); an expired one cannot be decided, and the agent has to ask again. With the app closed, the phone looks for new requests every 15 minutes, so give a realistic expiry. Dayhand records who asked, what was offered and what you chose, in the task's history and the activity log. Whether the agent then does what you chose is up to the agent."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"Pausing every agent","u":"agents.html#pausing-every-agent","x":"One switch stops every agent's changes at once, for every key and every OAuth connection, while they can still read and tell you what they see: dayhand agents pause --reason \"on holiday\" dayhand agents dayhand agents resume The same switch is Pause agents under Settings → Agents on the phone. While it is on, every change by an agent fails with agents_paused (exit 7), dry runs and approval requests included. Your own changes from the apps and cli keys are never refused, and no key needs to be replaced afterwards. An agent cannot lift the pause itself."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"Tool policies per agent key","u":"agents.html#tool-policies-per-agent-key","x":"A policy says, for one agent key, whether a tool that changes the plan is allowed (the default), needs your approval each time, or is refused. \"*\" covers every such tool without a rule of its own: dayhand policy set <keyId> delete_task deny dayhand policy set <keyId> complete_task ask dayhand policy set <keyId> \"*\" ask dayhand policy deny refuses the call (forbidden, exit 7). Reading tools have no policy. ask refuses the call with approval_required and the id of an approval request Dayhand made for exactly that call (an hour to decide). Once you choose Allow once, the agent repeats the call with the approval (--approval <id> in dayhand, approvalId over MCP) and it runs, once. The key's id comes from dayhand key list. The phone shows the policies but does not change them yet. Policies apply to API keys. An OAuth connection, such as the Claude app, follows the pause and its scopes (read, or read and change), but has no per-tool rules: give an agent that needs them a key."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"The proposal inbox","u":"agents.html#the-proposal-inbox","x":"Work an agent or an integration came up with, rather than work you asked for, can be added as a proposal. It waits in your inbox, out of Today, the board, lists, the calendar and reminders, until you decide: dayhand add \"Checkout times out\" --propose --source \"sentry:CHECKOUT-42 dayhand proposals dayhand proposals accept 42 dayhand proposals merge 43 --into 12 dayhand proposals reject 44 --reason \"not now\" Accept: it joins the plan as it is. Merge: an open task gets its note, links and labels, and the proposal goes to the trash. Reject: it goes to the trash, restorable for 30 days. One source, one task. A task with a --source (a kind and a reference, such as an alert id) is made once: adding it again returns the same task, also after it was rejected or merged. A repeating alert does not fill your inbox. --requested-by says who asked for it; --return-to <webhookId> names a webhook that hears, with the task's proof, when it is completed, rejected or merged (webhooks). The phone shows proposals under Settings → Agents and on the health card."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"The health report","u":"agents.html#the-health-report","x":"dayhand health One read that says what needs a look: whether agents are paused, overdue tasks, tasks due today, tasks in progress and who works on them, claims that ran out, blocked tasks with every reason, tasks waiting for an approval, proposals, tasks done today with and without proof, and the oldest open task. The phone shows the same in one line above Today; agents get it as the tool and prompt plan_health."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"What a well-behaved agent does","u":"agents.html#what-a-well-behaved-agent-does","x":"Back from a pause: read the activity log from its cursor, and the health report. Pick work with dayhand list --ready (or list_all with ready: true). Claim the task; on conflict, pick another. Write criteria and a plan if the task has none; renew the claim while working. Before a risky step: ask, and wait for the answer. Tick the criteria, then complete with proof. Stopping early: release with a note; stuck: set a blocked reason. Work nobody asked for: add it as a proposal with a source. On agents_paused stop changing things and wait; on forbidden do not retry; on approval_required wait for that request, then repeat the call with it. The same steps are in the resource, in dayhand prime and in the Agent Skill."},{"p":"Agents and you","t":"Planned","u":"agents.html#planned","x":"Backlog.md import and export are planned and not in main yet. Dayhand's criteria, plan and dependencies follow the same ideas as Backlog.md, a Markdown task board for people and agents, so a plan can later move between the two. Phase guides as MCP resources. Changing tool policies on the phone."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"Agent work","u":"agent-work.html","x":"Since backend 0.10.0 (CLI 0.11.0, Android 0.9.1), Dayhand has four features for AI agents that work on the plan next to the person who owns it: an activity log, acceptance criteria with proof of completion, dependencies with a blocked reason, and time-boxed claims. Backend 0.11.0 (CLI 0.12.0, Android 0.10.0) adds four that keep the owner in charge of those agents: approval requests decided on the phone, a switch that pauses every agent with a policy per tool, an inbox for proposed work, and a plan health report. This page explains the problem each one solves, the decisions behind it and the public designs it follows. The tool reference is in mcp.md, the REST routes in api.md and the commands in cli.md."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"The problem","u":"agent-work.html#the-problem","x":"Dayhand started as one person's day plan with an agent as a helper: the agent read Today, moved a task, left a resume note. Once several agents work on the same plan, for hours and with pauses, four things go wrong: Catching up is expensive. An agent that comes back after a pause has to read the whole plan again to see what changed, which costs time and context, and it still cannot tell who changed what. \"Done\" is a feeling. An agent marks a task done when it believes it is done. The person cannot see what done meant or what shows that it happened. Order is invisible. Some tasks can only start after others, and some are stuck on something outside the plan. Without that in the data, an agent picks work it cannot finish. Two agents take the same task. Nothing says \"someone is on this\", so two agents start the same work, or one overwrites the other's changes. One rule shaped every answer: no new statuses. The board has three columns, todo, doing and done, and the Android app, the CLI, the calendar feed, the webhooks and integrations rely on exactly these three. Everything below is fields next to the status, so a client that does not know them keeps working, and every addition is a minor version of the API (new tools, new fields, new error codes; see mcp.md)."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"1. The activity log","u":"agent-work.html#1-the-activity-log","x":"list_events (and the resource dayhand log in the CLI, GET /api/v1/events) returns the user's task changes in the order they happened, each with who made it (by: app, cli or agent: <name>) and the changed fields before and after. Every result carries a nextCursor. An agent keeps it and passes it back as since next time: it then gets only the changes made in between, without reading the plan. by and taskId narrow the log; hasMore says another page is already waiting. Decisions: The data was already there. Every change has written a history row since 0.1.0 (taskEvents, signed with the actor). The log is a read of that table, not a second copy. A position, not a time. Times are not enough as a cursor: two changes can share a millisecond, and a change whose transaction started earlier can commit after a later one, so a reader that resumes \"after 12:00:00.123\" can miss it. Each task change therefore gets a position (seq) from a per-user counter that every task change reads and writes in its own transaction. Convex runs such conflicting mutations one after the other, so positions follow the commit order and a cursor never skips a change. The position starts from the change's time in microseconds, so a time can still be turned into a starting point (since also takes Unix milliseconds or an ISO 8601 time) and history from before 0.10.0 can be numbered into the same order by a migration (migrations:backfillEventSeqs, deploy.md). Without since, the newest changes. A new agent wants to start following the log from now, not from the first change ever made; since: \"0\" reads from the start. Only task changes. History also records account changes (API keys, webhooks, devices); they stay out of the log, which a key with scope read may read. Bounded work per call. A call looks at no more than a few thousand changes; when filters skip most of them, the cursor continues from where it stopped. Public counterparts: an MCP resource with a URI template ( for clients that read context without a tool call; the cursor-and-hasMore shape of MCP's own pagination; the same events as the webhooks (Standard Webhooks) for clients that would rather be told than ask."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"2. Acceptance criteria, plan and proof","u":"agent-work.html#2-acceptance-criteria-plan-and-proof","x":"A task may carry acceptance, a list of criteria { id, text, met }, and a plan, free text on how the work will be done. complete_task takes an optional proof: { summary, links, checks }, where each check is { name, result: \"pass\" | \"fail\", url } (a test run, a CI job, a review). The proof is kept on the task. The rule: when a task has acceptance criteria and an agent completes it, every criterion must be met and the proof must have a summary; otherwise the call fails with precondition_failed and nothing changes. A person completing a task in the app or with a CLI key does it as before: the rule binds agents, not the owner. Decisions: Criteria are data, with ids. Like the checklist, criteria keep their ids (a1, a2, …) across edits, so an agent ticks a2 without rewriting the others' text. They are separate from the checklist because they mean something else: the checklist is steps, the criteria are the test. Agents tick criteria themselves, with update_task. Dayhand does not judge whether a criterion is really met; it makes the claim explicit and leaves a record of who made it and with what proof, in the history and on the task. A failing check does not block completion. The rule asks for met criteria and a summary; a fail check is recorded as it is, so the person sees it. precondition_failed, not invalid_argument. The arguments are fine; the task's state does not allow the change yet. The same code already meant \"the state is not what this change needs\" for If-Match (RFC 9110 412). A repeating task's next occurrence keeps the criteria (unticked) and the plan, but not the proof. Public counterpart: Backlog.md (MIT), a Markdown task board for people and AI agents, gives its tasks acceptance criteria and an implementation plan in the same spirit. Dayhand takes the idea, not the format or code: its criteria are structured fields behind its own tools."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"3. Dependencies and a blocked reason","u":"agent-work.html#3-dependencies-and-a-blocked-reason","x":"dependsOn lists the tasks (ids, or numbers as \"#42\") that must be done first; blockedReason says why a task cannot go on. list_all takes ready: true: tasks that are not done, not blocked, and whose every dependency is done (ready: false gives the rest). Decisions: Blocked is a reason, not a status. A blocked task stays in todo or doing; the reason is what a person needs to read anyway. No cycles. A task cannot depend on itself, directly or through others; such a change is an invalid_argument. A dependency in the trash, or purged, no longer holds a task back: it is not part of the plan any more. Restoring it makes it count again. Ready is worked out when asked, never stored. Ready does not look at claims. An agent picking work calls list_all with ready: true and then claim_task; a conflict tells it to take the next one. Public counterpart: Backlog.md's dependencies field, a list of task ids a task waits for. A plan moved between the two keeps the same meaning: wait until those tasks are done."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"4. Time-boxed claims","u":"agent-work.html#4-time-boxed-claims","x":"claim_task claims a task for a while (120 minutes unless minutes says 1 to 1440). The task then shows claimedBy (the API key or agent connection, and its name) and claimExpiresAt. While the claim lasts, another agent's claim and changes of that task fail with the new error code conflict (HTTP 409 on the REST API, exit code 6 in the CLI). renew_claim extends the claim from now; release_task ends it, optionally with a checkpoint note saved as the resume note (where the work stopped, the next step). Completing the task ends the claim too. After claimExpiresAt anyone may claim the task again, without waiting for the holder. The owner wins. The person's own changes from the app and the CLI always go through, claim or not, and the app can take a task over at once (app.release, the same operation with the owner as the actor). The phone shows \"agent: <name> works until <time>\" in the task's details while a claim lasts. Decisions: A lease, not a lock. A lock held by a crashed agent blocks the task until someone notices. A lease ends by itself; the holder has to renew it while it works. The expired claim stays on the task until it is released or claimed again, so the history shows who last worked on it. The holder is the credential. A claim belongs to one API key or one agent connection, not to a name: two keys with the same name are two holders. Give each agent its own key. Other agents are refused, not only told. A claim that other agents could ignore would protect nothing; their changes of the task fail with conflict and the message says who holds it until when. Changes that only touch the task on the side (another task becoming the now task) are not refused. Renewing a claim you no longer hold is precondition_failed; renewing after it expired works as long as nobody else claimed the task meanwhile. Claims do not change the status. Claiming does not move a task to doing; an agent that starts the work says so with set_status, as a person would. Renewals are not webhook events. Claiming and releasing are task.updated; a renewal only moves the expiry and would flood the receivers of an agent that renews every few minutes. Public counterparts: a Kubernetes Lease names its holder (holderIdentity), its length (leaseDurationSeconds) and when it was renewed, and anyone may take it over once it has run out; Amazon SQS's visibility timeout hides a message from other consumers while one works on it, can be extended (ChangeMessageVisibility) and gives the message back when it runs out. Dayhand's claim is the same contract for a task, with the owner allowed to step in. The status code follows RFC 9110 409 Conflict."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"The second problem: who is in charge","u":"agent-work.html#the-second-problem-who-is-in-charge","x":"The first four features let agents work together. Once they do, for hours and while the owner is away from the desk, the owner needs four more things: A say before something risky. An agent about to deploy, delete or pay should be able to ask, and the owner should be able to answer from the phone, without a terminal. A brake. When an agent misbehaves, or the owner just wants quiet, one control has to stop every agent at once, and finer rules have to say which agent may use which tool. A door for work nobody asked for. Alerts, issues and agents' own ideas should not land in Today as if the owner had planned them, and the same alert must not make ten tasks. One look at the state of things. What is late, what is stuck, who is working on what, and what was finished without anything to show for it. The same rule holds: no new statuses. Everything below is new tables, new fields of a task and new tools; a client that knows none of them keeps working."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"5. Approval requests","u":"agent-work.html#5-approval-requests","x":"request_approval stores a request: what it is about (optionally a task), what the agent wants to do (action, with detail), 2 to 4 options, each with the consequence of picking it, whether the step is reversible, and how long the owner has (expiresInMinutes, a day unless set, a week at most). The phone shows it under Settings → Agents (waiting for you), on the health card above Today, and as a notification; the owner picks an option and may add a note. The agent reads the outcome with get_approval (pending, decided or expired), or is told by the webhook approval.decided. dayhand ask and dayhand approval do the same from a terminal, and a cli key can decide with dayhand approvals decide. Dayhand records the decision; it does not carry it out and cannot stop an agent from acting without one. The agent, or the tooling around it, has to ask and then respect the answer. What Dayhand guarantees is the record: who asked, what they offered, what the owner chose, when, in the task's history and the activity log. The one place Dayhand does enforce a decision is its own tools, through the tool policy ask (next section). Decisions: Options, not yes or no. Real decisions often have a third way (\"wait until Monday\"). The agent offers the choices it can actually carry out and says what follows from each, so the owner decides without asking back. Option ids default to o1, o2, …, so an agent can branch on them. Requests expire. A question that waits forever is a decision by default. After expiresAt the request is expired and can no longer be decided; the agent has to ask again. Expiry sends no webhook: the agent knows the time. The owner decides, nobody else. decide_approval is one of the owner's tools: agents do not see it, and an agent that calls it gets forbidden. Deciding again the same way changes nothing; deciding differently is precondition_failed. A request about a task is part of that task's story: asking and deciding enter its history and the activity log (approvalRequest, approvalDecide). Closed app. The phone looks for new requests in the background every 15 minutes (Android's shortest periodic work) and shows a notification for each. A request that needs an answer in less than that will often be seen only when the owner opens the app; the agent should give a realistic expiresInMinutes. There is no push service in between, on purpose: Dayhand has no server of its own that could reach the phone. Public counterpart: MCP elicitation lets a server ask the user something through the client, during one request. Dayhand's requests are the asynchronous form of the same idea: the agent asks, goes on with something else or waits, and the answer comes from another device, maybe hours later. The webhook follows Standard Webhooks, like every other Dayhand event."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"6. Pausing agents and tool policies","u":"agent-work.html#6-pausing-agents-and-tool-policies","x":"Pause agents (Settings → Agents on the phone, dayhand agents pause, pause_agents) is one switch. While it is on, every change by an agent key or an agent connection (OAuth) is refused with agents_paused, including dry runs, approval requests and the account routes of the REST API (keys, webhooks, import); reads keep working, so an agent can still see what is going on and tell the user. Switching it off lets them change things again. The owner's own changes, from the app and from cli keys, are never refused. Each agent key can also have a policy: for a tool that changes the plan, or for \"*\" (every such tool without its own rule), allow (the default), ask or deny (dayhand policy set <key> <tool> <rule>, set_tool_policy). deny refuses the call with forbidden. ask refuses it with approval_required and the id of an approval request Dayhand made for exactly that call; once the owner picks \"Allow once\", the agent repeats the call with approvalId and it runs, once. Reading tools have no policy. Decisions: Checked where the change happens. The pause and the policies are checked inside the same transaction as the change (gateAgentWrite in convex/lib/agentControls.ts), for MCP and the REST API alike. There is no way around them through another surface, and no window between the check and the write. The pause is not a revocation. Revoking keys would stop agents too, but they would need new keys afterwards and their history would split. A pause keeps every key and connection and only holds their changes back. An agent cannot lift its own brake. Pausing, resuming and setting policies are the owner's tools: hidden from agents, and forbidden (or, while paused, agents_paused) if called. One approval, one call. A policy request is bound to the key, the tool and the arguments (their SHA-256, without clientId, dryRun and approvalId), and is used up in the transaction that applies the change; if the change fails, the approval stays unused. Calling again while the request waits returns the same request, so a retrying agent does not flood the phone. A policy request waits an hour. A dry run needs no approval under ask: it changes nothing, and it is how an agent shows the owner what it is about to do. Under deny and while paused it is refused like the call. Policies are per key. An agent connection (OAuth) has its scopes (tasks:read, tasks:write) and obeys the pause, but has no per-tool rules; give an agent that needs finer rules its own API key. Public counterparts: Claude Code's permission rules sort tools into allow, ask and deny; Dayhand uses the same three words with the same meaning, per agent key and per Dayhand tool, with the \"ask\" answered on the phone. The pause is the kill switch of operations: one long-lived toggle that turns a whole capability off without a deploy."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"7. The proposal inbox","u":"agent-work.html#7-the-proposal-inbox","x":"A task added with proposal: true is a proposal: it has proposedAt and waits in the owner's inbox (Settings → Agents → Proposals, the health card, dayhand proposals). It is not in Today, the board, list_all (unless asked for with proposed: true), the ready list, the calendar feed or the phone's reminders until the owner decides: Accept: it joins the plan as it is. Merge into a task already in the plan: that task gets the proposal's note (after a line naming it), links, labels, source and return webhooks; the proposal goes to the trash with mergedInto. Reject: it goes to the trash, restorable for 30 days. Work waits for the decision: a proposal cannot be claimed, made the now task, moved to doing or completed. Only the owner decides. Any new task can carry three more fields: source { kind, ref, url? }: where it came from, e.g. { kind: \"sentry\", ref: \"CHECKOUT-42\" }. One source makes one task: adding a task with a source that already made one returns that task instead, also when it was rejected (it is in the trash) or merged (the source then leads to the task it was merged into). Once the trash is purged, the source is free again. requestedBy: who asked for it, in words (a customer, a colleague). An agent's proposal without it says agent: <name>. returnTo: the id of one of the user's webhooks. When the task is completed, rejected or merged, that webhook gets task.returned with the outcome and the task's proof (section 2), whatever events it subscribes to. An integration that opened a task from an alert can close the alert with the proof the work left. Decisions: A field, not a status. A proposal is a todo task with proposedAt; accepting removes the field. Clients that do not know proposals never see them, because every list they read leaves proposals out. The creator chooses. An agent or integration says proposal: true for work nobody asked for; work the owner asked for goes straight in as before. Uniqueness by source, kept in its own table (taskSources), so it holds across merges and the trash, and two calls with the same source at the same moment still make one task (Convex runs them one after the other). Rejecting is not purging. A rejected proposal sits in the trash for 30 days, so a mistaken rejection can be undone and the same alert does not come back as a new task the next minute. The return target is a registered webhook, not a free URL: its address was checked when it was added (public HTTPS only) and its messages are signed with its own secret. Public counterpart: the Idempotency-Key draft for HTTP makes a repeated request create one resource. A task's source is the same promise keyed by the outside world's own id, so it also holds when the sender is not the one retrying, e.g. an alert that fires again."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"8. The plan health report","u":"agent-work.html#8-the-plan-health-report","x":"plan_health (also GET /api/v1/health, dayhand health, the MCP prompt plan_health) answers \"what needs a look?\" in one read: overdue tasks, tasks due today, tasks in progress and who works on them (agent: <name> while a claim lasts), claims that ran out, blocked tasks with every reason (the blocked reason and each dependency not done yet), tasks waiting for an approval and the pending requests in all, proposals in the inbox, tasks completed today with and without proof, and the oldest open task with its age in days. Each section has its count and lists the first 20 tasks. Whether agents are paused comes first. On the phone, a card above Today says the same counts in one line, the most pressing first, and its Review opens Settings → Agents when a request or a proposal waits. The phone reads the report from the server (app:planHealth) when Today opens, when the app comes back to the front and when the tasks change; offline it keeps the last report, and a phone without a server has no card. Decisions: Counts and names, not judgements. The report says what is there; the agent or the owner decides what to do about it. The plan_health prompt asks the agent to go through it with the owner, most urgent first, and to ask before changing anything. Open means in the plan: not done, not archived, not in the trash and not a proposal. \"Done without proof\" counts tasks completed today with no summary, link or check. It is a prompt to look, not an error: people complete tasks without proof all the time."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"What an agent does","u":"agent-work.html#what-an-agent-does","x":"Back from a pause: list_events with the nextCursor it kept; plan_health for the overview. Pick work: list_all with ready: true. claim_task; on conflict, pick another task. Write acceptance and plan if the task has none; renew_claim while working. Before a risky step: request_approval, then act on what get_approval says. Tick the criteria with update_task, then complete_task with proof. Stopping early: release_task with a checkpoint note; stuck: blockedReason. Work nobody asked for: add_task with proposal: true and a source. agents_paused: stop changing things and wait. forbidden: do not retry. approval_required: wait for that request, then repeat the call with approvalId. The same steps are in the resource and in dayhand prime for agents."},{"p":"Agent work","t":"Not in this release","u":"agent-work.html#not-in-this-release","x":"Phase guides as MCP resources and Backlog.md import or export are planned separately. The phone does not edit tool policies yet (it shows them; dayhand policy sets them), and it notifies about approval requests at most every 15 minutes while the app is closed."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"MCP endpoint","u":"mcp.html","x":"URL: <deployment HTTP actions URL>/mcp, e.g. locally or on Convex Cloud. Transport: Streamable HTTP, stateless, JSON responses (application/json); no SSE stream. Every message is one POST; notifications get 202; GET and DELETE get 405. Protocol: 2026-07-28 and the 2025 revisions (2025-11-25, 2025-06-18, 2025-03-26, 2024-11-05) on the same URL; see Protocol versions. Authentication: Authorization: Bearer <personal API key>, or an OAuth access token of an agent connection the owner approved on the phone, which is how the Claude apps connect (oauth.md). Missing, wrong, expired or revoked credentials get HTTP 401 with a WWW-Authenticate header that points OAuth clients at the authorization server. A request with a browser Origin header gets HTTP 403 unless the origin is listed in DAYHAND_MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS (deploy.md). Every change is recorded with the key's kind as the actor (cli for the user at a terminal, agent for an AI agent) and the key's name, or as agent with the OAuth client's name; see Keys and actors. Pass a clientId (e.g. a UUID) with changes you might retry; a repeated clientId is not applied twice. Pass dryRun: true to check a change without saving it (Dry runs). Each key or connection may make 120 reads and 60 changes a minute (Limits). Several agents can share the plan: an activity log, acceptance criteria and proof, dependencies and time-boxed claims; agents can ask the owner first, the owner can pause them or set a policy per tool, agents propose work into an inbox, and a health report shows what needs a look (agent-work.md). The server identifies itself as { \"name\": \"dayhand\", \"version\": \"0.11.0\" } (serverInfo of initialize, _meta of 2026-07-28 results); see Versions."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Tools","u":"mcp.html#tools","x":"The input schemas are generated from the same validators the operations enforce; the output schemas come from schemas/, the contract dayhand --json follows too. Every task has an id and a ref: the user's task number (1, 2, 3, …). It is given once, kept through the trash, the archive and a restore, and never given to another task, even after the task is purged. Every tool that takes a taskId accepts either the id or the number written as \"#42\". tools/list returns the tools in this order. Tools marked O are the owner's: only the app and cli keys may call them, and tools/list leaves them out for agents (agent keys and agent connections; since 0.11.0). Read-only tools are marked R, destructive ones D (destructiveHint); every change except add_task is idempotent (idempotentHint: repeating it with the same arguments changes nothing more), and no tool reaches outside Dayhand (openWorldHint: false). Each tool also has a title. Tool Title Hints Result (schema) Operation add_task Add task task Add a task (title; optional note, dueDate, dueTime, status, resumeNote, repeat, labels, links, checklist, reminders, acceptance, plan, dependsOn, blockedReason, proposal, source, requestedBy, returnTo) update_task Update task task Change fields; null clears an optional field set_status Set task status task todo / doing / done set_now Set the now task set-now Make one task the \"now\" task, optionally with a resume note; taskId: null clears it complete_task Complete task complete Mark done, optionally with proof; ends the claim; repeating tasks return their next occurrence (next: null when the series has ended). An agent needs every acceptance criterion met and a proof summary (below) reschedule_task Reschedule task task Move to another day/time; dueDate: null removes the date archive_task Archive task task Archive delete_task Move task to trash D task Move to the trash (purged after 30 days) restore_task Restore task task Restore from the trash, or from the archive list_today Today R today Overdue, today, done today and the now task list_all Find tasks R task-list Tasks not in the trash: search, filters and pages (below) list_trash Trash R task-list Tasks in the trash board Board R board Three columns: todo, doing, done get_task Task with history R task-detail One task with its change history export_plan Export plan R export Markdown, JSON or iCalendar (ics, calendar.md) export of the whole plan get_settings Settings R settings Time zone, summary time, default reminders update_settings Update settings D settings Change them list_events Activity log R event-list Task changes after a cursor (below) claim_task Claim task task Claim a task for minutes (default 120); other agents get conflict (below) renew_claim Renew claim task Extend your claim by minutes from now release_task Release task task End your claim, optionally with a checkpoint note (saved as the resume note) request_approval Ask the owner approval Ask the owner to decide before you act: action, 2 to 4 options, reversible, expiresInMinutes, optional taskId (below) get_approval Approval request R approval One request: pending, decided (with the chosen option and note) or expired list_approvals Approval requests R approval-list Requests, newest first, by status and taskId decide_approval Decide approval request O approval Pick an option of a pending request get_agent_controls Agent controls R agent-controls Whether agents are paused, and the tool policies pause_agents Pause agents O agent-controls paused: true refuses every agent's changes until paused: false set_tool_policy Set tool policy O agent-controls apiKeyId, toolName (or \"*\"), rule: allow, ask or deny accept_proposal Accept proposal O task A proposal joins the plan (below) merge_proposal Merge proposal O merge Fold a proposal into a task already in the plan (intoTaskId) reject_proposal Reject proposal O, D task Move a proposal to the trash, optionally with a reason plan_health Plan health R plan-health What needs a look, in one read ("},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Results","u":"mcp.html#results","x":"A successful call returns the result as structuredContent (a list as { \"result\": [...] }, null as { \"result\": null }) and as JSON in its text block. A Markdown export's text block is the Markdown itself and its structuredContent is { \"format\": \"markdown\", \"content\": \"…\" }; an iCalendar export (format: \"ics\") likewise has the calendar text in both. Each tool's outputSchema is the result's schema from schemas/ or the tool error (anyOf), with every referenced schema inlined under $defs: clients never fetch a URL. The top level is an object, as 2025 clients require. backend/convex/lib/outputSchemas.ts is generated from docs/schemas by npm run schemas in backend/; a test fails when it is out of date."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Finding tasks (list_all)","u":"mcp.html#finding-tasks-list_all","x":"Argument Meaning query Tasks whose title, note, resume note or labels contain this text, in any case status Only these statuses, e.g. [\"todo\", \"doing\"]; done tasks are included only when listed labels Only tasks having all of these labels ready true: only tasks ready to work on (not done, no blockedReason, not a proposal, every task in dependsOn done); false: only the others proposed true: only proposals waiting in the inbox; without it, or false, proposals are left out (since 0.11.0) dueBefore, dueAfter Only tasks due before / after this day (YYYY-MM-DD, \"today\", \"tomorrow\"); tasks without a day never match overdue true: only overdue tasks; false: only the others includeDone, includeArchived Done tasks (default true; ignored when status is given), archived ones (default false) limit Page size, 1 to 200 cursor nextCursor of the previous page Tasks come oldest due first (tasks without a day first, timed before untimed on one day). The result is { \"result\": [...], \"total\": <all matches>, \"nextCursor\": <string or null> }; pass nextCursor back as cursor with the same filters for the next page. The cursor is opaque and stays valid when tasks are added or removed in the meantime. Without limit, every match comes back at once and the text block holds the plain task array, as before 0.4.0, so clients that read the text keep working; with limit or cursor the text block holds the whole object."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Agent work","u":"mcp.html#agent-work","x":"Since 0.10.0 a task may carry fields for agents working on it (agent-work.md has the reasons). None of them is a new status: the board stays todo, doing, done. Field Set with Meaning acceptance add_task, update_task Criteria { id, text, met } in order; new ones get a1, a2, …; the list is replaced as a whole, null or [] removes it (at most 50, up to 500 characters each) plan add_task, update_task How the work will be done (up to 20000 characters) proof complete_task { summary?, links, checks: [{ name, result: \"pass\" | \"fail\", url? }] }, kept on the task (schema) dependsOn add_task, update_task Ids of the tasks that must be done first (given as ids or \"#42\"); [] removes them; no cycles (at most 50) blockedReason add_task, update_task Why the task cannot go on; null clears it claimedBy, claimExpiresAt claim_task, renew_claim, release_task Who holds the claim (keyId or connectionId, and name) and until when (Unix ms); kept after expiry until released or claimed again When a task has acceptance criteria, an agent (an agent key or an agent connection) can complete it only when every criterion is met and the proof has a summary; otherwise complete_task (and set_status to done) fails with precondition_failed. The app and cli keys complete tasks as before. A repeating task's next occurrence keeps the criteria, unticked, and the plan. Claims claim_task gives the caller's key or connection the task for minutes (1 to 1440, default 120). While the claim lasts, another agent's claim_task, renew_claim, release_task and every change of that task (update_task, set_status, set_now, complete_task, reschedule_task, archive_task, delete_task, restore_task) fail with conflict, whose message names the holder and the expiry. Claiming again extends your own claim. renew_claim works while nobody else has claimed the task, also after your claim ran out; without a claim of yours it is precondition_failed. release_task ends the claim and saves note as the resume note. Completing the task ends its claim. Done, archived and trashed tasks cannot be claimed (invalid_argument). The owner wins: changes from the app and from cli keys are never refused, and the app can end any claim (app.release). Activity log (list_events) Argument Meaning since nextCursor of an earlier call (\"c…\"), or a time as Unix milliseconds or ISO 8601 with a zone (the changes from then on); without it, the newest changes by Only changes by app, cli, agent (any agent) or \"agent: <name>\" taskId Only changes of this task limit 1 to 500, default 100 The result is { \"events\": [...], \"nextCursor\": \"c…\", \"hasMore\": <bool> } (schema). Each event has id, at, taskId, ref and title (the task's now), action, actor, actorName, by, before and after, like a history entry. Events come oldest first, in the order the changes were committed. Pass nextCursor as since next time to get only what changed in between; hasMore: true means another page is already there. Only task changes are listed, not changes of API keys, webhooks or devices. Changes made before 0.10.0 appear once the deployment ran migrations:backfillEventSeqs (deploy.md)."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Approvals, pausing and policies","u":"mcp.html#approvals-pausing-and-policies","x":"Since 0.11.0 (agent-work.md has the reasons): request_approval stores a request { action, detail?, options: [{ label, consequence?, id? }], reversible, expiresInMinutes? (1 to 10080, default 1440), taskId?, clientId? }; options get o1, o2, … unless they carry an id. The owner decides on the phone (or with a cli key, decide_approval); the agent reads the outcome with get_approval or gets the webhook approval.decided (webhooks.md). A request about a task enters that task's history and the activity log (approvalRequest, approvalDecide). Dayhand records the decision; it does not stop an agent from acting without one. The agent's own tooling has to check it. pause_agents with paused: true refuses every change by an agent key or agent connection with agents_paused, dry runs included, until paused: false; reads keep working, and the owner's own changes (app, cli keys) are never refused. set_tool_policy sets, for one agent key, allow, ask or deny on a tool that changes the plan, or on \"*\" (every such tool without its own rule); no rule means allow. deny fails the call with forbidden. ask fails it with approval_required and an approvalId: Dayhand has made a request with the options allow (\"Allow once\") and deny, bound to this key, this tool and these arguments (without clientId, dryRun and approvalId); calling again while it waits returns the same request. Once the owner allows it, the same call with approvalId runs once; another call, a second use, a denied or an expired request is refused. A dry run needs no approval. Policies are per API key; agent connections (OAuth) follow only the pause and their scopes."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Proposals","u":"mcp.html#proposals","x":"add_task with proposal: true adds a task with proposedAt: it waits in the owner's inbox and stays out of list_today, board, list_all (unless proposed: true), ready, the calendar feed and the phone's Today until the owner accepts it (accept_proposal), merges it into another task (merge_proposal: that task gets the proposal's note after a line naming it, its links, labels, source and return webhooks; the proposal goes to the trash with mergedInto) or rejects it (reject_proposal: to the trash). A proposal cannot be claimed, made the now task, moved to doing or completed (invalid_argument). Three fields work on any new task: Field Meaning source { kind, ref, url? }, e.g. { \"kind\": \"sentry\", \"ref\": \"CHECKOUT-42\" }. One source makes one task: add_task with a source that already made a task returns that task, also when it is in the trash; once the trash is purged the source is free again. A merged proposal's source leads to the task it was merged into requestedBy Who asked for it (up to 200 characters); an agent's proposal without it says agent: <name> returnTo Id of one of the user's webhooks: it gets task.returned with the outcome (completed, rejected or merged) and the proof, whatever events it subscribes to (webhooks.md)"},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Plan health (plan_health)","u":"mcp.html#plan-health-plan_health","x":"plan_health (also GET /api/v1/health, dayhand health, the prompt plan_health) returns, as of at, in the user's time zone: agentsPaused; overdue, dueToday, inProgress (each with workingBy: \"agent: <name>\" while a claim lasts), expiredClaims, blocked (each with reasons: the blocked reason and every dependency not done yet), awaitingApproval (tasks with a pending request, and pendingApprovals in all), completedToday (each with proof, and withProof, withoutProof): each { count, tasks } with the first 20 tasks; proposals.count; and oldestOpen, the open task created longest ago with ageDays (schema). Proposals and archived tasks are not counted as open."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Dry runs","u":"mcp.html#dry-runs","x":"Every changing tool takes dryRun: true. The change runs with every check, then Dayhand rolls it back: nothing is saved and nothing enters the history. The result is what the real call would return, marked \"dryRun\": true (for set_now with taskId: null, { \"result\": null, \"dryRun\": true }). A dry run of add_task shows the number the task would get, but does not reserve it. A change that would fail fails its dry run with the same error. Dry runs count as changes for the limits."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Resources","u":"mcp.html#resources","x":"URI Type Content application/json list_today application/json list_events without since: the newest changes and the cursor to follow from (template) application/json list_events with since, e.g. text/markdown How an agent should work with Dayhand: numbers, the now task, retries, dry runs, limits (template) application/json get_task of task #ref, e.g. resources/list returns the first three, resources/templates/list the two templates. The helm:// URIs of the same resources, from before the rename, can still be read. Reading a resource that does not exist (also a task number that does not) is a JSON-RPC error with data: { \"uri\" }: -32602 on 2026-07-28, -32002 on 2025 revisions."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Prompts","u":"mcp.html#prompts","x":"Prompt Arguments What it starts plan_day focus (optional) Go through overdue and today's tasks, agree on an order and set the now task; the Today screen is attached as the resource weekly_review Look back at the last 7 days (board's done column) and ahead at the next 7 (list_all with dates), then tidy the plan triage_overdue Decide, task by task, what happens to each overdue task; the overdue tasks are attached plan_health Go through what needs a look, most urgent first; the plan_health report is attached (since 0.11.0) Each prompt tells the agent to ask before changing anything, to pass a clientId and to preview larger changes with dryRun. An unknown prompt or argument is a JSON-RPC error -32602."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Limits","u":"mcp.html#limits","x":"Each API key, and each agent connection (OAuth), may make 120 reads and 60 changes a minute (convex/callLimits.ts). The minute starts with the first call; reads and changes are counted apart. Reads are tool calls of read-only tools, resources/read of and and prompts/get; listing tools, resources and prompts, server/discover and are not counted. A call over the limit is refused before it runs, so nothing is applied: a tool call returns the tool error rate_limited with retryAfterMs, HTTP 200 and a Retry-After header (seconds); resources/read and prompts/get get HTTP 429 with Retry-After and the JSON-RPC error { \"code\": 429, \"message\": \"Too many reads with this API key: …\", \"data\": { \"retryAfterMs\" } }. npx convex run callLimits:reset '{\"keyId\":\"<key id>\"}' clears a key's counters ('{\"connectionId\":\"<connection id>\"}' a connection's)."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Repeat rules","u":"mcp.html#repeat-rules","x":"repeat is an object, or an RFC 5545 RRULE string (with or without RRULE:) that the server reads into the object. Every repeating task also comes back with its rule as an RRULE in rrule, e.g. \"FREQ=WEEKLY;BYDAY=MO,TH\". Field Meaning RRULE every, interval every day, week, month or year; interval 2 = every other (1 to 366) FREQ, INTERVAL weekdays weekly rules: days 1 (Monday) to 7 (Sunday) BYDAY=MO,TH monthDay monthly rules: day 1 to 31, or -1 for the last day BYMONTHDAY=15, BYMONTHDAY=-1 weekdayOfMonth monthly rules: { nth, weekday }, nth 1 to 5 or -1 (last) to -5 BYDAY=2TU, BYDAY=-1FR (or BYDAY=TU;BYSETPOS=2) from \"due\" (default): the next date follows the due date; \"completion\": it is counted from the day the task is done none until last date the series may fall on, YYYY-MM-DD UNTIL=20261231 (a UTC date-time is read in the user's time zone) count occurrences left, this one included; 1 = the last one COUNT until and count cannot be combined (as in RFC 5545). Completing a task counted from the due date creates the first date of the series after today, so a late completion skips the dates it missed, and those count against count. When the series has ended, complete_task returns next: null. The next occurrence keeps the title, note, labels, links and reminders, with the checklist unticked. Dayhand reads FREQ (DAILY, WEEKLY, MONTHLY, YEARLY), INTERVAL, BYDAY, BYMONTHDAY, BYSETPOS, UNTIL, COUNT and WKST; anything else (BYHOUR, BYMONTH, …) is an invalid_argument naming the part. Where Dayhand differs from RFC 5545: A day the month does not have (31 in April) falls on the month's last day; RFC 5545 skips the month. rrule writes such days as BYMONTHDAY=28,29,30,31;BYSETPOS=-1, which means the same in both, and reads that form back as monthDay: 31. A plain BYMONTHDAY=31 is read as monthDay: 31 too, with Dayhand's meaning. A plain monthly rule takes its day from the due date; after a shorter month the next occurrence keeps that day as monthDay (31 Jan, 28 Feb, 31 Mar). A yearly rule from 29 February falls on 28 February in other years and stays there. from: \"completion\" has no RRULE form; rrule describes the dates only. Weeks start on Monday; another WKST is refused only where it would change the dates (several days every two or more weeks). The rule logic is in backend/convex/lib/repeat.ts (advance, and occurrences to list a series ahead, e.g. for a calendar) and lib/rrule.ts (toRRule, parseRRule)."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Checklists and reminders","u":"mcp.html#checklists-and-reminders","x":"checklist: items { id, title, done } in the order shown. update_task replaces the whole list: send the items you keep with their ids, new items without one (the server gives c1, c2, …). null or [] removes it. At most 100 items of up to 500 characters. History records the list before and after each change. reminders: the task's own reminders instead of the default ones from settings. Each is { minutesBefore } (before the due time; rings only for timed tasks) or { time: \"HH:MM\", daysBefore? } (that time on the due day, or up to 30 days before; works for untimed tasks too). A task without reminders uses the defaults; [] means no reminders for this task; null in update_task goes back to the defaults. At most 5. Reminders ring on the phone (architecture.md)."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Errors","u":"mcp.html#errors","x":"A failed tool call is a normal result with isError: true. The text block holds a message for people; structuredContent.error holds the same message and a code for programs: { \"content\": [{ \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Task #404 not found\" }], \"structuredContent\": { \"error\": { \"code\": \"not_found\", \"message\": \"Task #404 not found\" } }, \"isError\": true } Code Meaning Example message invalid_argument Wrong or missing arguments, or an operation the task's state does not allow dueDate must be YYYY-MM-DD, \"today\" or \"tomorrow\", got \"someday\"; Invalid arguments: taskId must be a task id or \"#<number>\", got \"nope\"; Task is in the trash; restore it first not_found No such task (or API key, device) for this user Task #404 not found rate_limited Too many calls with this key or connection; retryAfterMs says when to try again Too many changes with this API key: at most 60 a minute. Try again in 12 s. unauthenticated Reserved for identity errors inside a tool; a missing, wrong or revoked key or token gets HTTP 401 before any tool runs precondition_failed The task's state does not allow the change yet: an agent completing a task with unmet acceptance criteria or without a proof summary, renew_claim without a claim of yours; through the REST API also If-Match naming a version the task is no longer at (api.md) Acceptance criteria not met: a2; mark them met with update_task first; The task has changed since that version (it is now \"1793011200000\"); read it again conflict Another agent holds the task's claim (Claims); pick another task or wait for the time named Task #42 is claimed by claude-code until 2026-10-24T14:30:00.000Z agents_paused The owner paused every agent; reading works, changes wait until they resume (since 0.11.0) The owner has paused all agents (holiday): reading works, changes are refused until they resume agents forbidden Only the owner may do this, or the owner's policy denies the tool to this key, or the owner denied the approval (since 0.11.0) Only the owner can accept proposals (in the app or with a cli key); The owner's policy does not let this key call delete_task approval_required The owner's policy asks first; approvalId names the request to wait for, then repeat the same call with it (since 0.11.0) The owner's policy asks before this key calls complete_task. Approval request … waits for the owner until … internal Anything unexpected; the message says only Internal error Internal error New codes may be added in a minor version; treat an unknown code like internal. Errors outside tools are JSON-RPC errors: Code When HTTP -32602 Unknown tool or prompt, bad prompt arguments, a resource that does not exist (2026-07-28), a missing _meta field (2026-07-28) 200; 400 for the _meta field -32002 A resource that does not exist (2025 revisions) 200 -32601 Unknown method 200; 404 on 2026-07-28 -32020 2026-07-28 header mismatch: a standard header missing or not matching the body 400 -32022 Unsupported protocol version; data: { supported, requested } 400 429 Too many reads outside tools (Limits) 429 -32600, -32700 Not a JSON-RPC request, a batch, a body over 256 KB, a browser origin not allowed 400, 413, 403 An OAuth access token whose scopes do not cover the request gets HTTP 403 with WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error=\"insufficient_scope\", scope=\"…\" and no JSON-RPC body: a tool of the other scope, or, without tasks:read, resources/read of the user's data and prompts/get (oauth.md). tools/list shows only the tools the token's scopes allow."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Protocol versions","u":"mcp.html#protocol-versions","x":"One endpoint serves both eras of MCP, the way the specification describes a dual-era server: 2025 revisions (2025-11-25 and earlier): the client sends initialize, and the server answers with the requested version when it knows it, otherwise 2025-11-25. ping works. Results have no resultType. Dayhand keeps no session, so every request stands alone anyway. 2026-07-28: no initialize; a request is served as 2026-07-28 when its params._meta carries io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion. It must also carry io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities (else -32602, HTTP 400) and the headers MCP-Protocol-Version (equal to that version), Mcp-Method and, for tools/call, prompts/get and resources/read, Mcp-Name (plain or =?base64?…?=), all matching the body (else -32020, HTTP 400). server/discover returns supportedVersions (both eras), the capabilities and the instructions. Every result has resultType: \"complete\" and _meta[\"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo\"]; server/discover, tools/list, prompts/list, resources/list and resources/templates/list carry ttlMs: 300000 and cacheScope: \"public\" (tools/list of an agent, or of a token with fewer scopes: private, since it leaves tools out), resources/read carries ttlMs: 0 and cacheScope: \"private\" ( public). ping no longer exists and unknown methods get HTTP 404. Not offered in either era: subscriptions/listen (and listChanged notifications), logging, completion, sampling, elicitation and the tasks extension. Dayhand never asks the client for input, so no request returns input_required. The official TypeScript SDK v2 reaches the 2026-07-28 era with versionNegotiation: { mode: \"auto\" } (or a pin); without it, it speaks 2025 like SDK v1. Both are tested (backend/tests/mcp-modern.test.ts)."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Keys and actors","u":"mcp.html#keys-and-actors","x":"Every API key has a name and a kind: cli: the user at a terminal. History shows changes as cli. agent: an AI agent. History shows changes as agent: <key name>, e.g. agent: claude-code. Changes made in the app are app. Changes made through an agent connection (OAuth, e.g. the Claude app) are agent with the client's name, e.g. agent: Claude (oauth.md). get_task returns each history entry with actor (app, cli or agent), actorName (the key's or client's name, null for the app) and by, the label above. Give each agent its own key so it can be told apart and revoked on its own. Keys without a kind (created before 0.3.0) act as agent keys; the 0.3.0 migration records that (deploy.md). A new key is an agent key unless created with \"kind\": \"cli\": npx convex run apiKeys:create '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\",\"name\":\"macbook\",\"kind\":\"cli\"}' npx convex run apiKeys:create '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\",\"name\":\"claude-code\"}' The app does the same with app.createApiKey({ name, kind }); app.listApiKeys returns each key's kind. Since 0.8.0 a key also has a scope and may expire: Scope On /mcp REST /api/v1 (api.md) Account: keys, webhooks, devices, connections, calendar feed read only the reading tools (tools/list shows only them; a change gets 403 insufficient_scope) GET routes no (403) write every tool every task and settings route no (403) full every tool everything yes Keys without a scope (every key made before 0.8.0) are full keys; nothing changes for them. expiresAt (Unix milliseconds) ends a key: after it the key gets 401 like a revoked one. npx convex run apiKeys:create '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\",\"name\":\"n8n\",\"scope\":\"read\",\"expiresAt\":1798761600000}' Creating and revoking API keys is not an MCP tool on purpose: a key that can mint keys could outlive its own revocation. Keys are managed by the app (app.createApiKey, app.revokeApiKey), by the deployment admin (npx convex run apiKeys:create), or with a full key through POST /api/v1/keys (dayhand key create). A key made that way can only have scope read or write, expires no later than the key that made it, and works only while that key works: revoking the parent stops it too (api.md). Phone pairing is not an MCP tool either. The CLI pairs phones through separate endpoints (/devices/*, see pairing.md) that accept the same API key."},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Versions","u":"mcp.html#versions","x":"serverInfo.version is the backend version (backend/package.json) and the one clients use to check compatibility. It follows semver: a minor version only adds tools, fields, error codes and accepted values; a major version may change or remove them. A client written for 0.3 works with any 0.x from 0.3 on. Version Changes 0.11.0 Agents and the owner (agent-work.md): request_approval, get_approval, list_approvals, decide_approval; get_agent_controls, pause_agents, set_tool_policy, and approvalId on every change an agent makes; proposals (add_task takes proposal, source, requestedBy, returnTo; task fields proposedAt, source, requestedBy, returnTo, mergedInto; list_all takes proposed; accept_proposal, merge_proposal, reject_proposal); plan_health and the prompt plan_health; error codes agents_paused, forbidden, approval_required (with approvalId); webhook events approval.decided and task.returned; the owner's tools (O) are left out of an agent's tools/list 0.10.0 Agent work (agent-work.md): list_events and the resource (the activity log); task fields acceptance, plan, proof, dependsOn, blockedReason, claimedBy, claimExpiresAt; complete_task takes proof; list_all takes ready; claim_task, renew_claim, release_task; error code conflict, and precondition_failed outside If-Match too 0.9.0 The rename to Dayhand: serverInfo.name dayhand; resources and (the helm:// URIs are still read); new API keys start with dayhand_ (helm_ keys keep working); import format dayhand-json (helm-json still accepted); WWW-Authenticate realm dayhand; x-dayhand-scope in the OpenAPI document; problem types under deployment variables DAYHAND_* (HELM_* still read, deploy.md) 0.8.0 API keys with a scope (read, write, full) and expiresAt (a read key sees and calls only reading tools); the error code precondition_failed (REST If-Match only); the REST API /api/v1 with OpenAPI 3.1 (api.md), webhooks (webhooks.md) and import. Devices, connections and the calendar feed need a full key 0.7.0 export_plan format ics; the read-only calendar feed GET /cal/<token>.ics and its endpoints /calendar/feed* (calendar.md) 0.6.0 OAuth 2.1 for the Claude apps and other MCP clients: access tokens next to API keys, WWW-Authenticate with resource_metadata, scopes tasks:read and tasks:write (tools/list filtered, 403 insufficient_scope), limits per connection (oauth.md) 0.5.0 Repeat rules: from, monthDay, weekdayOfMonth, until, count, RRULE strings in repeat and rrule on repeating tasks; checklist and reminders on tasks; complete_task returns next: null when a series has ended 0.4.0 MCP 2026-07-28 next to the 2025 revisions; tool titles, all behaviour hints and output schemas; list_all search, filters and pages (total, nextCursor); dryRun on every change; resources prompts plan_day, weekly_review, triage_overdue; limits per key and error rate_limited; structured content for a Markdown export; Origin check 0.3.0 Task numbers (ref, taskId: \"#42\"); error codes in structuredContent.error; key kinds and named actors in history (actor: \"cli\", actorName, by) 0.2.0 Phone pairing endpoints (/devices/*, /auth/*) 0.1.0 MCP endpoint with 17 tools"},{"p":"MCP endpoint","t":"Connecting a client","u":"mcp.html#connecting-a-client","x":"The Claude apps (claude.ai, Desktop, Android, iOS) add Dayhand as a custom connector with the URL <deployment HTTP actions URL>/mcp and sign in with OAuth; the owner approves the connection on the phone. The steps are in oauth.md. Any MCP client with Streamable HTTP and custom headers works with an API key, for example: claude mcp add --transport http dayhand \\ --header \"Authorization: Bearer $DAYHAND_API_KEY\""},{"p":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","t":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","u":"oauth.html","x":"The hosted Claude apps (claude.ai on the web, Claude Desktop, Claude for Android and iOS) add a remote MCP server as a custom connector and sign in to it with OAuth; a fixed API key header is only a beta for some organizations (claude.com/docs, add-unlisted). Dayhand therefore runs a small OAuth 2.1 authorization server next to its MCP endpoint. Personal API keys keep working unchanged for the CLI and for agents that can send a header (mcp.md). The owner approves every connection on the paired phone. Dayhand has no web sign-in: the authorization page in the browser shows a code, and the owner types that code into the Dayhand app (Settings → Agent connections → Approve a connection), sees who asks for what, and approves or refuses. The threat model is in oauth-threat-model.md. Claude ──POST /mcp (no token)──────────────────────> 401, WWW-Authenticate: resource_metadata=… Claude ──GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp, /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server Claude ──(POST /oauth/register, or its client metadata URL as client_id) browser ─GET /oauth/authorize?…&code_challenge=…(S256)&resource=…/mcp <── page: \"Approve on your phone\", code K7QF-M2XA phone ───app.lookupConnectionRequest {code} ──> who, which scopes, where it returns phone ───app.decideConnectionRequest {code, approve, scopes} browser ─(page polls POST /oauth/authorize/status, then POST /oauth/authorize/continue) <── 303 Claude ──POST /oauth/token (code + code_verifier) ──> access token (1 h) + refresh token (60 days) Claude ──POST /mcp, Authorization: Bearer helmat_… ──> tools allowed by the scopes"},{"p":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","t":"Endpoints","u":"oauth.html#endpoints","x":"All on the deployment's HTTP actions URL ( locally That URL is the issuer; <issuer>/mcp is the protected resource. Endpoint Purpose GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource and …/oauth-protected-resource/mcp RFC 9728 metadata: resource (<issuer>/mcp), authorization_servers ([<issuer>]), scopes_supported GET /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server RFC 8414 metadata: endpoints, code_challenge_methods_supported: [\"S256\"], token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported: [\"none\"], client_id_metadata_document_supported: true, authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported: true GET /oauth/authorize Checks the request and shows the page with the code (HTML, English or Polish by Accept-Language) POST /oauth/authorize/status The page polls whether the phone decided (form field request) POST /oauth/authorize/continue Sends the browser back to the client with a code, or with error=access_denied POST /oauth/token authorization_code and refresh_token grants, application/x-www-form-urlencoded POST /oauth/register Dynamic Client Registration (RFC 7591), JSON, public clients only POST /oauth/revoke RFC 7009 revocation (form: token, client_id) GET /connections, POST /connections/revoke The owner's list and revocation with a personal API key (dayhand connection) /mcp answers a request without a valid credential with 401 and WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm=\"dayhand\", scope=\"tasks:read tasks:write\", resource_metadata=\"<issuer>/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp\" (plus error=\"invalid_token\" when a token was sent but is not valid)."},{"p":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","t":"Clients and redirect URIs","u":"oauth.html#clients-and-redirect-uris","x":"A client is identified in one of two ways: Client ID Metadata Document (Claude's \"Use Claude's published identity\"): client_id is an HTTPS URL that Dayhand fetches. The Claude apps use Claude Code Only URLs on the allowed origins are fetched, by default only no redirects are followed, the answer is limited to 16 KB and 5 seconds. The document's client_id must equal its URL, token_endpoint_auth_method must be none (or absent), grant_types and response_types (when given) must include authorization_code and code, and its redirect_uris must all be Claude's (below). Other grant types the document lists, such as the JWT bearer grant in Claude's, are ignored: the token endpoint offers only authorization_code and refresh_token. Claude uses this method only because the metadata advertises client_id_metadata_document_supported and the auth method none. Dynamic Client Registration (Claude's \"Register automatically\"): POST /oauth/register with redirect_uris (Claude's only), optional client_name; no client secret is ever issued. Registrations nobody used are deleted after 7 days. Redirect URIs are limited to the callbacks Claude documents (authentication for connectors): the hosted Claude apps (web, desktop, mobile, Cowork), matched exactly; and on any port: Claude Code's loopback redirect, as its own metadata document ( declares. The page and the phone show a warning for these. The requested redirect_uri must also be one the client registered. A request with an unknown client or a wrong redirect URI gets an error page and is never redirected."},{"p":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","t":"Requests, scopes and tokens","u":"oauth.html#requests-scopes-and-tokens","x":"PKCE is required: code_challenge_method=S256 and a 43-character challenge; plain and requests without PKCE are refused with error=invalid_request. Scopes: tasks:read (list_today, list_all, list_trash, board, get_task, export_plan, get_settings) and tasks:write (every other tool). A request without scope asks for tasks:read only; unknown scopes are refused (invalid_scope); offline_access is ignored. The phone may grant a non-empty part of what was asked, never more. tools/list shows only the tools the token's scopes allow; calling another tool gets 403 with WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error=\"insufficient_scope\", scope=\"…\". Reading the user's data through resources/read (all but and prompts/get needs tasks:read the same way. Resource indicators (RFC 8707): resource, when sent to /oauth/authorize or /oauth/token, must be <issuer>/mcp; every token is bound to it and /mcp refuses a token bound to anything else. The request waits 10 minutes for the phone; the code shown in the browser is 8 Crockford base32 characters, stored only as SHA-256. The authorization code is valid 2 minutes and dies on its first presentation, right or wrong. Presenting a used code again revokes the connection it created. Access tokens (helmat_…) last one hour; refresh tokens (helmrt_…) 60 days and are rotated on every refresh. Using a refresh token a second time revokes the whole connection, unless it comes back within 30 seconds of its first use: that is a client retrying after a lost answer, so it gets a new pair and the pair it never saw stops refreshing. A refresh may ask for fewer scopes (scope), and the narrower scopes stay with the tokens that follow; it can never ask for more (invalid_scope). Both tokens are a selector plus a 256-bit secret stored as SHA-256 only, like device tokens. The token answer carries Cache-Control: no-store. Errors follow RFC 6749 (invalid_grant, invalid_request, invalid_client for a client secret, unsupported_grant_type, invalid_scope, invalid_target)."},{"p":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","t":"Connections, history and revocation","u":"oauth.html#connections-history-and-revocation","x":"An approved request becomes a connection when the client exchanges its code. Changes made through it are recorded as actor: \"agent\" with the client's name as actorName, so history shows e.g. agent: Claude (mcp.md); the connection's id is stored with each change. Approvals, refusals and revocations are recorded in history too. Phone: Settings → Agent connections lists the connected agents (where they come from, read only or read and change, last use); tapping one disconnects it. CLI: dayhand connection list (or dayhand connections) and dayhand connection revoke <id> (cli.md). Admin: npx convex run oauthOwner:list '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\"}' and npx convex run oauthOwner:revoke '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\",\"id\":\"<connectionId>\"}'. Revocation takes effect on the next request: access and refresh tokens of a revoked connection are refused at once (unlike a revoked phone's app JWT, which lives up to an hour)."},{"p":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","t":"Limits","u":"oauth.html#limits","x":"What Limit Over the limit Waiting requests per client (client_id) 5 at a time, 20 started per 10 minutes 429 page Waiting requests, whole deployment 300 started per 10 minutes (a flood guard) 429 page Registrations 100 per hour, whole deployment 429 (rate_limited) Wrong codes typed on the phone 10 per 10 minutes per user the app says so MCP calls per connection 120 reads and 60 changes a minute, as for an API key (mcp.md) tool error rate_limited or 429 npx convex run oauth:resetLimits clears all OAuth counters. The token endpoint has no lockout: codes and tokens carry 256 bits, so guessing is hopeless, and a lockout would only let anyone block real clients. Someone who floods the authorization endpoint with Claude's own client_id can delay new Claude connections for up to ten minutes, never get one; registering automatically (Dynamic Client Registration) is the way around it. See oauth-threat-model.md."},{"p":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","t":"Configuration","u":"oauth.html#configuration","x":"Variable Default Meaning DAYHAND_PUBLIC_URL the Convex site URL (CONVEX_SITE_URL) The issuer and the base of /mcp, for a custom domain. It must be exactly the URL users add in Claude, without /mcp. DAYHAND_OAUTH_CIMD_ORIGINS Comma-separated origins whose client metadata documents are fetched. Tests add a local origin; production should not change it. No secret is involved: tokens are random and stored hashed, and the app-signing key (DAYHAND_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK) is not used by OAuth."},{"p":"OAuth: connecting the Claude apps and other MCP clients","t":"Connecting the Claude app (manual procedure)","u":"oauth.html#connecting-the-claude-app-manual-procedure","x":"Done once by the owner after the backend with OAuth is deployed to production. It cannot be tested against the real Claude apps before that, because Claude reaches the server from Anthropic's network and needs public HTTPS. Check the deployment from any terminal (no key needed): curl -si -X POST | grep -i www-authenticate # WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm=\"dayhand\", scope=\"tasks:read tasks:write\", resource_metadata=\" curl -s curl -s Phone ready: the Dayhand app is paired with this deployment (Settings → Server shows Synced) and updated to the version with Settings → Agent connections. Add the connector in the Claude app: Settings (or Customize) → Connectors → Add custom connector. Name: Dayhand. URL: Under the advanced or authentication settings choose Sign in now (or leave the default) and Use Claude's published identity for the OAuth client; leave client ID and secret empty. Add. Connect: Claude opens Dayhand's page in a browser view. It shows a code like K7QF-M2XA. Approve on the phone: switch to the Dayhand app → Settings → Agent connections → Approve a connection → type the code → check that it says Identified by claude.ai and Answer goes to claude.ai, that Asked is a moment ago and that there is no red warning → leave both permissions on (or switch off Change tasks and settings for read only) → Approve. Back to the browser view: within two seconds it continues by itself (or tap Continue) and Claude shows Dayhand as connected. Try it: ask Claude \"What is on my Dayhand list today?\" (it calls list_today), then \"Add test from Claude to Dayhand for today\" and check the task in the app; its history shows agent: <the client's name>. Delete the test task afterwards. If step 4 shows \"Unknown client\": Claude's published identity is served from an origin other than Remove the connector and add it again with Register automatically (Dynamic Client Registration), which needs no allowlist. Note the origin from the Convex logs (Client metadata refused: …) so it can be added to DAYHAND_OAUTH_CIMD_ORIGINS deliberately. To disconnect: Settings → Agent connections → tap the connection → Disconnect, or dayhand connection revoke <id>. Removing the connector in Claude alone does not revoke it on Dayhand's side; unused connections end 60 days after their last refresh. Claude refreshes the access token by itself (on 401 and shortly before it expires), so the phone is needed only for the first approval and after a disconnection."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Deploying Dayhand","u":"deploy.html","x":"Dayhand deploys the backend/ folder to a Convex deployment (Convex Cloud or self-hosted). The repository holds no secrets: everything below goes into the deployment's environment."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Run your own server in 5 minutes","u":"deploy.html#run-your-own-server-in-5-minutes","x":"One command sets up a complete Dayhand server of your own on your own free Convex account: sync for the phone app, the MCP endpoint for AI agents, the REST API and dayhand. Nobody else runs or maintains anything for you, and nobody else can see your tasks. You need Node.js 22.12 or newer and git, on macOS, Linux or Windows. The Convex account is made (or signed in to) in your browser during the setup. git clone cd dayhand/cli && npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run build && npm link && cd .. dayhand server deploy Without dayhand, the same setup is cd dayhand/backend && npm run setup (it then prints the dayhand login command to use later). npm link puts dayhand on the PATH; where it needs administrator rights, run node cli/dist/bin/dayhand.js server deploy instead. dayhand server deploy (which runs backend/scripts/setup.mjs): installs the backend's dependencies from the lockfile when they are missing (npm ci --ignore-scripts); logs you in to Convex with the browser (npx convex login; skipped when this computer is already signed in); creates a project, dayhand by default, in your team and its production deployment (or uses the one you name with --deployment); generates an ES256 key pair for the app's sign-in and sets DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER (the server's address, DAYHAND_JWKS and DAYHAND_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK in the deployment's environment, passing the values on stdin; deploys backend/ and checks that /api/v1/openapi.json, /auth/token and /mcp answer; creates a CLI key (kind cli) and signs dayhand in with it (OS keychain, or a private file), and makes a pairing code for the phone, valid for 10 minutes. If you already used another server, setup stores the new server's key but does not overwrite an existing profile. Select the printed address explicitly for subsequent commands, for example in a POSIX shell: export DAYHAND_URL= dayhand whoami Replace <deployment> with the name setup prints. Set this again in a new terminal, or pass --url to each command, including pair and key creation. At the end it prints the server address, the pairing code with a QR code, and the next steps: Phone: open Dayhand, type the server address on the Connect screen and the code (or scan the QR code). Another phone later: dayhand pair. dayhand: already signed in; dayhand add \"First task\" and dayhand show it works. Claude: in the Claude app, Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector with in Claude Code, claude mcp add --transport http dayhand then /mcp to sign in. Either way you approve the connection on the paired phone (oauth.md). Options: --team <slug> (asked when you belong to several teams), --project <name>, --deployment <name> (an existing production deployment, e.g. made in the dashboard), --issuer <url> (only for a new server). --dry-run lists every step and command without changing anything or using the network. Without a terminal, add --yes: every question then gets its safe answer (keep what is there)."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Where the keys are","u":"deploy.html#where-the-keys-are","x":"Secrets never go into the repository or the output. They are kept in a private folder (mode 700, files mode 600; on Windows the profile folder's permissions): DAYHAND_SERVER_CONFIG_DIR, or ~/Library/Application Support/dayhand-server (macOS), $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dayhand-server or ~/.config/dayhand-server (Linux), %APPDATA%\\dayhand-server (Windows). cloud/ holds the Convex server, local/ the one on this computer: File What it is server.json The deployment, its address, the issuer and the user id (<issuer>|owner); no secrets signing-key.json A copy of the app-signing key. The deployment's environment holds the one in use; keep the copy in a password manager or delete it cli-key The CLI key dayhand was signed in with; revoke it with dayhand key revoke if it leaks convex.env Which deployment the Convex CLI acts on (local/: the local backend's admin key) local/instance-secret.json, local/data/ The local backend's secret and database The Convex CLI keeps its own login in ~/.convex. Anyone with admin access to the deployment (your Convex account) can read its environment variables."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Updating","u":"deploy.html#updating","x":"git pull dayhand server deploy # or: cd backend && npm run setup A second run deploys the new code and keeps everything else: the deployment, the keys, the CLI key (while it still works) and the data. It never replaces DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER (it is part of every user id, so a new one would hide your tasks) and asks before replacing a signing key the deployment already has. Read the \"Upgrading to\" section of the new version below first: some versions need a step after the deploy."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"What it costs","u":"deploy.html#what-it-costs","x":"Nothing, on Convex's Free plan. Its limits per team, as published on convex.dev/pricing and docs.convex.dev/production/state/limits (checked 2026-10-23; check them again, they change): Resource Free plan Function calls (app sync, MCP and API requests, scheduled jobs) 1,000,000 a month Database storage 0.5 GB Database I/O 1 GB a month File storage (Dayhand stores no files; backups count) 1 GB Data egress 1 GB a month Action compute 20 GB-hours a month Deployments 40 per team Developers 1 to 6 On Free the limits are hard caps: once one is reached, new writes may fail; nothing is billed. The paid Starter plan instead charges for usage above the same amounts. One person's tasks are small next to these numbers; the Convex dashboard shows what you use."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Backups","u":"deploy.html#backups","x":"Export the data (tasks, history, keys' hashes, devices) to a zip file outside the repository: cd backend npx convex export --env-file \"<folder>/cloud/convex.env\" --path ~/dayhand-backup-$(date +%F).zip <folder> is the private folder above. To restore into the same deployment: npx convex import --env-file \"<folder>/cloud/convex.env\" --replace-all <file>.zip (it drops changes made since). Into a new deployment, keep the same DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER (from server.json), or the restored tasks belong to a user nobody signs in as: with a new DAYHAND_SERVER_CONFIG_DIR, run dayhand server deploy --deployment <new> --issuer <old issuer>, import the zip with that folder's convex.env, run dayhand server deploy once more (the import replaced the keys table, so it makes a new CLI key) and pair the phone again. The environment variables are not in the export; the signing key can be new."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"On this computer, without an account","u":"deploy.html#on-this-computer-without-an-account","x":"dayhand server start # or: cd backend && npm run setup -- --local The same setup with the Convex local backend binary on this computer (macOS or Linux, x64 or arm64; not Windows), downloaded once from Convex's GitHub releases, version pinned and checksum verified. The binary is source-available under the FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0 licence (each release becomes Apache 2.0 two years later), not open source. The server runs until Ctrl+C; the same command starts it again with its data and keys (local/ in the private folder). It listens on 127.0.0.1 only (ports 3210 and 3211, --port and --site-port to change them), so the phone cannot reach it as it is. The Claude apps need the public HTTPS server above; Claude Code on the same computer can use with an agent key (mcp.md)."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Phone and computer (Tailscale)","u":"deploy.html#phone-and-computer-tailscale","x":"A release build of the Android app talks to its server over HTTPS only. To use a phone with the server on your computer, put both in a Tailscale network (tailnet): tailscale serve gives the computer an HTTPS address with a certificate inside the tailnet. Install Tailscale on the computer and the phone and sign both in to the same tailnet. In the admin console turn on MagicDNS and HTTPS certificates. The computer's name is then <machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net. On the computer, publish the HTTP actions (pairing, MCP) on port 443 and the Convex API (the app's sync) on port 8443, inside the tailnet only: tailscale serve --bg --https=443 tailscale serve --bg --https=8443 If the server is already running, stop it with Ctrl+C first. Start it again with that address, keeping the same data and keys, so it gives the phone the HTTPS addresses instead of 127.0.0.1: dayhand server start --public-url (--public-api-url when the Convex API is not on port 8443.) Then pair the phone with the printed address and code. In a second terminal, select that address for later CLI commands (POSIX shell), since an existing profile can still point at 127.0.0.1: export DAYHAND_URL= dayhand whoami tailscale serve status shows what is published; tailscale serve reset stops it. The phone syncs only while the computer is on and in the tailnet. This setup has not been tested by the project yet; if the app does not sync, check that the phone opens"},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Environment variables","u":"deploy.html#environment-variables","x":"Variable Value Read by DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER Issuer of app tokens, e.g. auth.config.ts, token signing DAYHAND_JWKS Public key(s) as a JWKS data URI (data:application/json;base64,…) auth.config.ts (Convex verifies app JWTs) DAYHAND_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK Private ES256 key as a JWK with kid; its public half must be in DAYHAND_JWKS under the same kid POST /auth/token only; never returned DAYHAND_MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS Optional. Comma-separated browser origins allowed to call /mcp, e.g. Unset: any request with an Origin header gets 403 POST /mcp DAYHAND_PUBLIC_URL Optional. The URL users add in Claude, without /mcp, when it is not the Convex site URL (a custom domain). Unset: CONVEX_SITE_URL OAuth issuer and resource (oauth.md) DAYHAND_OAUTH_CIMD_ORIGINS Optional. Comma-separated origins whose OAuth client metadata documents are fetched. Unset: production should leave it unset GET /oauth/authorize DAYHAND_WEBHOOK_ALLOWED_ORIGINS Optional. Comma-separated origins exempt from the webhook address rules (HTTPS, public hosts), e.g. a receiver on a self-hosted deployment's own network. Unset: none; production should leave it unset Webhooks (webhooks.md) DAYHAND_WEBHOOK_RETRY_DELAYS Optional. Comma-separated seconds before each webhook retry. Unset: 5,300,1800,7200,18000,36000,36000 Webhook delivery App users are <DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER>|<subject> (single-user deployments use owner as subject). Personal API keys for the CLI and agent should belong to that same user, so the phone, the CLI and the agent see one task list."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Renamed variables","u":"deploy.html#renamed-variables","x":"Before 0.9.0 these variables were called HELM_* (HELM_JWT_ISSUER, HELM_JWKS, HELM_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK and so on). The backend still reads HELM_<name> while DAYHAND_<name> is unset, and logs once per variable that it is deprecated (npx convex logs). dayhand server deploy and npm run setup leave the old names in place and say which ones the deployment still uses. Moving to the new names is optional; to do it, copy each value under its new name, deploy, check, then remove the old one: cd backend npx convex env list # which HELM_* names the deployment has for name in JWT_ISSUER JWKS JWT_PRIVATE_JWK; do npx convex env get HELM_$name | npx convex env set DAYHAND_$name done npx convex deploy # Convex reads auth.config.ts at deploy time Do the same for the optional variables the list shows. The issuer must stay exactly the same value (it is part of every user id). Check that the phone still syncs and dayhand whoami works, then remove the old names (npx convex env remove HELM_JWT_ISSUER and so on) and deploy once more. Before rolling back to 0.8.x, put the HELM_* names back: 0.8.x reads only those."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"First deployment by hand","u":"deploy.html#first-deployment-by-hand","x":"What npm run setup does, step by step, for a deployment you manage yourself: cd backend node scripts/signing-key.mjs new ~/dayhand-signing-key.json # mode 600, keep it out of the repo npx convex env set DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER npx convex env set DAYHAND_JWKS \"$(node scripts/signing-key.mjs jwks ~/dayhand-signing-key.json)\" node scripts/signing-key.mjs private ~/dayhand-signing-key.json | npx convex env set DAYHAND_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK npx convex deploy npx convex run apiKeys:create '{\"userId\":\" signing-key.mjs private refuses to print to a terminal; piping into npx convex env set NAME (no value argument) keeps the key out of shell history and process lists."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.11.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-0110","x":"0.11.0 lets the owner stay in charge of agents (agent-work.md): approval requests decided on the phone, a switch that pauses every agent, a tool policy per agent key, a proposal inbox, and the plan health report. Everything is new tools, routes, optional task fields (proposedAt, source, requestedBy, returnTo, mergedInto), error codes (agents_paused, forbidden, approval_required), webhook events (approval.decided, task.returned) and four tables (approvals, agentControls, toolPolicies, taskSources). There is no migration, no new status and no new secret; nothing changes until the owner uses a control or an agent proposes a task. Older clients keep working: they do not see proposals (which stay out of every list they read), and an old agent that gets agents_paused treats it like any unknown error. Take a snapshot to roll back to: npx convex export --path dayhand-before-0.11.0.zip. Deploy: dayhand server deploy (or npx convex deploy). Check: dayhand version shows server 0.11.0, dayhand agents prints Agents run, and dayhand health prints the report. Two things behave differently: tools/list of an agent (an agent key or an agent connection) leaves out the owner's tools (deciding approvals, pausing agents, policies, accepting proposals), and is marked cacheScope: \"private\" on 2026-07-28. A webhook added without events gets the same task events as before; approval.decided has to be asked for. Rollback: 0.10.x's schema does not know the new tables and fields, so Convex refuses to deploy it while any of them hold data. Restore the snapshot from step 1 first, npx convex import --replace-all dayhand-before-0.11.0.zip (this also drops every change made since), then deploy 0.10.x. Agents paused at that moment run again after the rollback."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.10.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-0100","x":"0.10.0 adds agent work (agent-work.md): the activity log (list_events, GET /api/v1/events), acceptance criteria, a plan and proof on tasks, dependencies with a blocked reason and list_all ready, and time-boxed claims with the new error code conflict. Everything is new, optional fields, tools, routes and one table (eventCounters); no field changes its meaning, there is no new status and no new secret. Older clients keep working; they just do not see the new fields. One migration numbers the history from before 0.10.0 into the activity log. Until it has run, list_events shows only changes made after the deploy; nothing else depends on it. Take a snapshot to roll back to: npx convex export --path dayhand-before-0.10.0.zip. Deploy: dayhand server deploy (or npx convex deploy). Number the old history: npx convex run migrations:backfillEventSeqs. It works in batches and schedules itself; npx convex run migrations:status shows taskEventsWithoutSeq: 0 when it is done. Running it again changes nothing. Check: dayhand version shows server 0.10.0 and dayhand log --since 0 --limit 5 lists the oldest changes. Agents get the new behaviour only when they use it: an agent completing a task that has acceptance criteria needs them met and a proof summary, and another agent's claim refuses its changes with conflict. The app and cli keys are never refused. The Android app 0.9.1 shows a claim, the criteria and the proof in a task's details; older apps ignore the new fields. Rollback: 0.9.x's schema does not know the new fields, so Convex refuses to deploy it while any task has them or any history row has seq (which the migration gives every old row). Restore the snapshot from step 1 first, npx convex import --replace-all dayhand-before-0.10.0.zip (this also drops every change made since), then deploy 0.9.x. To keep those changes, roll forward with a fix instead."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.9.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-090","x":"0.9.0 renames Helm to Dayhand. There is no schema change, no migration and no new secret: tasks, API keys, paired phones, agent connections and calendar addresses keep working, and every old name below is still accepted: the variables are DAYHAND_*; the HELM_* ones are read while the new ones are unset (Renamed variables); MCP serverInfo.name is dayhand and the resources are and (helm:// URIs are still read); new API keys start with dayhand_ (helm_ keys keep working: keys are found by their hash); dayhand pair prints links. The Android app from 0.9.0 and the macOS app open both forms; an older Android app opens only so type the code there; the import format is dayhand-json (helm-json still accepted), the calendar export file dayhand.ics, and the feed's events say Dayhand #<number> (their UIDs stay …@helm, so calendars do not show them twice); WWW-Authenticate says realm=\"dayhand\", the OpenAPI document x-dayhand-scope, and problem types are under a client that compares these strings exactly needs the new ones; the old helm command of backend/ (npx helm) is gone; use dayhand from cli/. Take a snapshot to roll back to: npx convex export --path dayhand-before-0.9.0.zip. Deploy: dayhand server deploy (or npx convex deploy). Check: dayhand version shows server 0.9.0, the phone syncs, and npx convex logs shows one deprecation line for each HELM_* variable still in use. Rollback: deploy 0.8.x again. Nothing in the data changed, and dayhand_ keys work there too; only the HELM_* variables must be in place (see Renamed variables). The sections below were written before the rename: hlm is now dayhand, the helm command of backend/ is gone, and their HELM_* and HLM_* variables are now DAYHAND_*."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.8.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-080","x":"0.8.0 adds the REST API for integrations (api.md), webhooks (webhooks.md), import, and API keys with a scope and an expiry date: new optional fields in apiKeys: scope, expiresAt, parentKeyId; new tables webhooks and webhookDeliveries, and a daily cron (webhooks.purgeDeliveries, 04:15 UTC) that drops delivery log entries older than 30 days; new routes under /api/v1/ (/api/v1/openapi.json is public); the optional variables HELM_WEBHOOK_ALLOWED_ORIGINS and HELM_WEBHOOK_RETRY_DELAYS (leave both unset in production). Every new field is optional and every key without a scope is a full key, so there is no migration and no new secret: existing keys, the app, agents and hlm 0.7 keep working. Nothing sends webhooks until a user adds one. One behaviour tightens: /devices*, /connections* and /calendar/feed* now need a full key; since every existing key is full, this only affects the new read and write keys. Take a snapshot to roll back to: npx convex export --path helm-before-0.8.0.zip (it holds the user's tasks; keep it out of the repository). Deploy: npx convex deploy. Check, with the key hlm uses (hlm whoami shows the URL): curl -s | head -c 200 # public, \"openapi\": \"3.1.0\" curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' \\ -H \"Authorization: Bearer $HLM_API_KEY\" # 200 curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' # 401 npx convex logs shows any error. The MCP endpoint reports serverInfo.version 0.8.0. Optional: give each integration its own key with the smallest scope and an expiry, from a full key (hlm key create n8n --scope read --expires 90d, hlm 0.8.0) or as the admin (npx convex run apiKeys:create '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\",\"name\":\"n8n\",\"scope\":\"read\",\"expiresAt\":<ms>}'). Rollback: first remove what 0.7.0 does not know, then deploy 0.7.0 again. Remove the webhooks (hlm webhook rm <id> --yes for each, which also deletes their delivery logs) and revoke the keys that have a scope or an expiry date, or were created with a key (hlm key list, hlm key revoke <id>). Deploy 0.7.0. Its schema has no webhooks, webhookDeliveries, scope, expiresAt or parentKeyId, so Convex refuses the push while any row has them. Empty a table the same way as callLimits for 0.4.0 below (npx convex import --table webhookDeliveries --replace --yes empty.jsonl, likewise webhooks). A revoked key still carries its scope: delete those rows by hand (npx convex data apiKeys shows them) or restore the snapshot from step 1 of the upgrade (npx convex import --replace-all helm-before-0.8.0.zip, which drops changes made since). Tasks created by an import and history entries with the actions import, webhook.* and apiKey.* are ordinary rows that 0.7.0 reads as they are; nothing else needs to change. Integrations that use /api/v1 then get 404 until 0.8.0 is back."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.7.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-070","x":"0.7.0 adds the read-only calendar feed (calendar.md): the table calendarFeeds, the routes GET /cal/<token>.ics and /calendar/feed*, and the format ics of export_plan. Nothing existing changes, so there is no migration and no new environment variable: npx convex deploy is enough. The feed URL is built from CONVEX_SITE_URL, which Convex sets itself. The feed is off for every user until they turn it on (Android 0.8.0 Settings → Calendar, or hlm calendar on with hlm 0.7.0); older apps and hlm keep working without it. Rollback: turn the feeds off (hlm calendar off --yes), then deploy 0.6.0 again. Its schema has no calendarFeeds; if Convex refuses the push because the table holds rows, empty it the same way as callLimits for 0.4.0 below. Subscribed calendars then get 404 and keep their last copy."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.6.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-060","x":"0.6.0 adds OAuth for the Claude apps (oauth.md): the tables oauthClients, oauthRequests, oauthGrants and oauthTokens, the optional field connectionId in taskEvents and callLimits (where keyId becomes optional), and a daily cron (oauth.purgeExpired, 04:00 UTC). Existing rows stay valid, so there is no migration and no new secret: npx convex deploy is enough. API keys, the app and hlm 0.5 keep working; the only visible change for them is a longer WWW-Authenticate header on 401 from /mcp. Connecting the Claude app afterwards is a manual step done once on the phone (oauth.md). The Android app needs 0.7.0 for Settings → Agent connections; older apps keep working without it. Rollback: revoke the connections first (hlm connection revoke <id>), then deploy 0.5.0 again. Its schema has no OAuth tables and no connectionId; if Convex refuses the push because of rows in them, empty the four oauth* tables and callLimits the same way as for 0.4.0 below. History entries written through a connection keep their connectionId and would block the push too; they are rare enough to delete by hand (npx convex data taskEvents), or stay on 0.6.0."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.5.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-050","x":"0.5.0 adds checklist and reminders to tasks and new optional fields to repeat (from, monthDay, weekdayOfMonth, until, count). All of them are optional in the schema, so the deploy accepts existing data and needs no migration: a task without them repeats, rings and exports as before. Deploy with npx convex deploy. Clients: the CLI and the agent keep working unchanged. An app older than 0.6.0 shows repeat rules with the new fields as their plain unit and does not show checklists or per-task reminders; changing such a task's repeat rule in that app replaces the rule with what it shows. Rollback: deploying 0.4.0 again fails schema validation once any task uses a new field. Roll forward with a fix, or remove the new fields from those tasks first (or restore a snapshot taken before the deploy with npx convex export)."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.4.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-040","x":"0.4.0 adds the table callLimits (MCP calls per key and minute) and changes nothing in the existing tables, so there is no migration: npx convex deploy is enough, and 0.3 clients (the app, hlm 0.3, agents) keep working. Calls are now limited per key (120 reads and 60 changes a minute), and /mcp refuses requests with a browser Origin that is not in HELM_MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS; MCP clients outside a browser send no Origin. Rollback: deploy 0.3.0 again. Its schema does not define callLimits; if Convex refuses the push because that table holds rows, empty it first (npx convex import --table callLimits --replace --yes empty.jsonl with an empty file). The rows only count calls, so nothing is lost."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Upgrading to 0.3.0","u":"deploy.html#upgrading-to-030","x":"0.3.0 adds task numbers (tasks.ref, new table taskCounters) and key kinds (apiKeys.kind, taskEvents.keyId and actorName). All new fields are optional in the schema, so the deploy accepts existing data, and the app, the agent and old API keys keep working before the migration runs. Until then, tasks created before the upgrade have no number (#n does not find them) and old keys count as agent keys. Take a snapshot to roll back to: npx convex export --path helm-before-0.3.0.zip (it holds the user's tasks; keep it out of the repository). Deploy: npx convex deploy. Right after, number the existing tasks (oldest first, per user; runs in batches of 200 and schedules itself until done) and mark the existing keys as agent keys: npx convex run migrations:backfillTaskRefs npx convex run migrations:backfillKeyKinds Run it right away: tasks added between the deploy and the migration get numbers first, so older tasks get the numbers after them. Both migrations are idempotent and can be run again. Check that nothing is left: npx convex run migrations:status must print { \"keysWithoutKind\": 0, \"tasksWithoutRef\": 0 }. If tasksWithoutRef is not 0 yet, the scheduled batches are still running; check again after a few seconds. If the key the user types commands with is a CLI key, make a new one with \"kind\": \"cli\" (see mcp.md), switch the CLI to it and revoke the old one, so the history shows cli instead of agent: <name>. Kinds cannot be changed on an existing key. Rollback: deploying 0.2.0 again over migrated data fails schema validation (ref, kind, actor: \"cli\", keyId and actorName are unknown to it). Roll forward with a fix instead, or restore the snapshot from step 0 (npx convex import --replace-all helm-before-0.3.0.zip, which drops changes made since) and deploy 0.2.0."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Moving an existing key from disk into the deployment","u":"deploy.html#moving-an-existing-key-from-disk-into-the-deployment","x":"For a deployment that already trusts a key pair kept on a computer (the private half was used to sign app tokens locally): Check that the deployment publishes the key you hold: npx convex env get DAYHAND_JWKS | node scripts/signing-key.mjs check /path/to/key.json It must print OK: DAYHAND_JWKS publishes this key (kid …). The file may hold the private JWK itself or { \"privateJwk\": … }, as written by npm run app-token -- --generate and scripts/signing-key.mjs new. Set the private key, then deploy the code with pairing: node scripts/signing-key.mjs private /path/to/key.json | npx convex env set DAYHAND_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK npx convex deploy Check the configuration without a real device: a bogus device token must get 401 (configured), not 503 (not configured): curl -s -X POST \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"deviceToken\":\"helmdev_x.y\"}' # {\"error\":\"invalid_grant\",…} On 503, npx convex logs shows the reason (missing variable, or a key that does not match DAYHAND_JWKS). Check that the CLI's API key belongs to the app user: npx convex data apiKeys must show userId = <DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER>|owner. Then dayhand pair works and the phone sees the same tasks. Paste-in tokens issued earlier with npm run app-token keep working until they expire (same key). Pair the phone instead, then stop issuing them. Move the key file off the disk into an offline backup (a password manager), or delete it. The deployment environment is now the only place that needs it. Anyone with admin access to the deployment can read its environment variables."},{"p":"Deploying Dayhand","t":"Rotating the signing key","u":"deploy.html#rotating-the-signing-key","x":"node scripts/signing-key.mjs new new-key.json. Publish both public keys for an hour: set DAYHAND_JWKS to a data URI whose keys array holds the old and the new key, and deploy (auth.config.ts is read at deploy time). Pipe the new private key into DAYHAND_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK; new tokens are signed with it. After an hour (the token lifetime), set DAYHAND_JWKS to the new key only and deploy again. Paired devices keep working through a rotation: their device tokens do not depend on the key."},{"p":"Phone pairing and app sign-in","t":"Phone pairing and app sign-in","u":"pairing.html","x":"The phone app never holds a signing key. It is paired once with a one-time code and then asks the backend for short-lived app tokens. dayhand pair (API key) ──> code XXXX-XXXX-XXXX, valid 10 min, single use phone: POST /auth/pair {code} ──> deviceToken (long-lived, revocable) phone: POST /auth/token {deviceToken} ──> app JWT (ES256, 1 hour) phone: Convex client with that JWT ──> app.* queries and mutations as the owner All endpoints are HTTP actions on the deployment's HTTP actions URL ( locally Requests and responses are JSON (Content-Type: application/json); token responses carry Cache-Control: no-store."},{"p":"Phone pairing and app sign-in","t":"Pairing link","u":"pairing.html#pairing-link","x":"dayhand pair prints the code, a deep link and the same link as a QR code: HTTP actions URL>&code=<XXXX-XXXX-XXXX> The app reads url (the HTTP actions URL, where /auth/pair and /auth/token live) and code from it. Typed codes may be lower case and may omit the dashes; O is read as 0, I and L as 1."},{"p":"Phone pairing and app sign-in","t":"POST /auth/pair","u":"pairing.html#post-authpair","x":"Request: { \"code\": \"E6M8-FE02-XSNT\", \"deviceName\": \"Pixel 9\" } deviceName is optional (1 to 100 characters, default \"Phone\"); it is shown by dayhand devices. Status Body Meaning 200 { \"deviceId\": \"jd7…\", \"deviceName\": \"Pixel 9\", \"deviceToken\": \"helmdev_…\", \"convexUrl\": \" } Paired. Store deviceToken in encrypted storage; it is shown only now. convexUrl is the URL for the Convex client. 400 { \"error\": \"invalid_request\", \"error_description\": \"…\" } Body is not JSON, code is not a string, or deviceName is invalid. 401 { \"error\": \"invalid_grant\", \"error_description\": \"The code or token is not valid\" } Wrong, unknown, malformed, expired, already used or burned code. The answer is the same for all of them. 429 { \"error\": \"rate_limited\", … }, header Retry-After: <seconds> Too many failed attempts on this deployment; codes are not checked until the window ends."},{"p":"Phone pairing and app sign-in","t":"POST /auth/token","u":"pairing.html#post-authtoken","x":"Request: { \"deviceToken\": \"helmdev_<22 chars>.<43 chars>\" } Status Body Meaning 200 { \"token\": \"<JWT>\", \"tokenType\": \"Bearer\", \"expiresIn\": 3600, \"expiresAt\": 1792691766000 } App JWT; expiresAt is Unix ms. 400 { \"error\": \"invalid_request\", … } Body is not JSON or deviceToken is not a string. 401 { \"error\": \"invalid_grant\", \"error_description\": \"The code or token is not valid\" } Unknown, malformed or revoked device token. Drop the token and pair again. 503 { \"error\": \"not_configured\", … } The deployment cannot sign tokens (configuration problem on the server). Retry later; do not drop the device token. The JWT has header { \"alg\": \"ES256\", \"typ\": \"JWT\", \"kid\": … } and claims iss (DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER), sub (the owner, e.g. owner), aud: \"helm\", iat, exp (iat + 3600)."},{"p":"Phone pairing and app sign-in","t":"Using the token in the Android app","u":"pairing.html#using-the-token-in-the-android-app","x":"Give the Convex client an auth callback (ConvexClientWithAuth / setAuth with a token fetcher) that calls POST /auth/token and returns token. Fetch a new token before expiresAt (for example when less than 5 minutes are left) and whenever the client asks for a refresh. convexUrl is the deployment's own view of its address. On a local backend it is which an emulator cannot reach: there, keep the port and use the host of the pairing link's url (e.g. 10.0.2.2). Network errors and 503 are temporary: keep the device token and retry with backoff. 401 from /auth/token means the device was revoked (or the token is corrupt): delete the device token and show the pairing screen. Revoking a device stops new tokens at once; a JWT it already holds stays valid until its exp (at most one hour). The app does all of the above from version 0.3.0: the Connect screen, the link, the encrypted device token and the refresh are described in android/README.md. A device cannot revoke itself (the /devices/* routes need an API key), so the app's Disconnect only forgets the sign-in on the phone and names dayhand revoke <deviceId> for the server side."},{"p":"Phone pairing and app sign-in","t":"Managing devices (CLI)","u":"pairing.html#managing-devices-cli","x":"dayhand pair # code + link + QR; --no-qr for text only, --json for scripts dayhand devices # name, paired when and by which key, last used, revoked dayhand revoke <deviceId> They call POST /devices/pairing-code, GET /devices and POST /devices/revoke ({ \"deviceId\": … }) with Authorization: Bearer <personal API key> (scope full, since 0.8.0). Pairing codes and revocations are recorded in the history under the key's kind and name (cli, or agent: <name>; see mcp.md). The deployment admin can do the same without an API key: npx convex run devices:createPairingCode '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\"}' npx convex run devices:list '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\"}' npx convex run devices:revoke '{\"userId\":\"<issuer>|owner\",\"actor\":\"app\",\"deviceId\":\"<id>\"}' npx convex run devices:resetPairLimit # after someone exhausted the failed-attempt limit Only an app user can pair: the API key's user id must be <DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER>|<subject>, because the phone's JWT gives exactly that identity. A key of another user gets an error from dayhand pair."},{"p":"Phone pairing and app sign-in","t":"Security notes","u":"pairing.html#security-notes","x":"Codes: 12 Crockford base32 characters (60 random bits). The first 4 select the code, the other 8 are stored as SHA-256 only. A code dies after one use, after 10 minutes, or after 5 wrong secrets for its selector. /auth/pair allows 10 failed attempts per 10 minutes for the whole deployment (the endpoint is unauthenticated, so there is no user to count per). Over the limit it answers 429 without checking the code. Someone can therefore block pairing for 10 minutes, but cannot guess a code; the admin can clear the counter with devices:resetPairLimit. Device tokens: a 16-byte selector and a 32-byte secret; the secret is stored as SHA-256 only and compared in constant time. /auth/token has no attempt limit on purpose: 256-bit secrets cannot be guessed, and a limit would let anyone lock the real phone out. Error answers never tell whether a code or token existed, was used, expired or revoked. The signing key (DAYHAND_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK) lives only in the deployment environment and is never returned. See deploy.md. A personal API key can pair a device, and that device keeps working after the key is revoked. If a key may have leaked, revoke it and check dayhand devices as well."},{"p":"REST API","t":"REST API","u":"api.html","x":"Since backend 0.8.0, Dayhand has a REST API for integrations that do not speak MCP: Shortcuts, n8n, Home Assistant, a cron script. It lives under <deployment HTTP actions URL>/api/v1 (e.g. and is described by an OpenAPI 3.1 document at /api/v1/openapi.json, which needs no key. The REST API is a thin layer: every task route runs the same domain operation as one MCP tool (mcp.md), with the same arguments, the same results and the same error codes. The route table is backend/convex/lib/restRoutes.ts; the dispatcher (convex/rest.ts) only checks the key, counts the call and translates HTTP to the tool's arguments and back. A contract test (backend/tests/rest-contract.test.ts) runs the same steps through MCP and REST and compares the results."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Authentication and scopes","u":"api.html#authentication-and-scopes","x":"Send a personal API key as a Bearer token: curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $DAYHAND_API_KEY\" OAuth access tokens of agent connections are bound to /mcp (RFC 8707) and get 401 here. Each route needs a key scope (mcp.md): read for GET routes, write for changes to tasks and settings, full for the account routes (API keys, webhooks, import). The OpenAPI document gives it per operation as x-dayhand-scope; a key with less gets 403 insufficient_scope. Give an integration the smallest scope it needs and an expiry date: dayhand key create n8n --scope read --expires 90d Changes are recorded in the task history like changes made through MCP: agent: <key name> for an agent key, cli for a CLI key."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Routes","u":"api.html#routes","x":"Method and path MCP tool Scope GET /tasks list_all (query parameters query, status, labels, dueBefore, dueAfter, overdue, ready, proposed, includeDone, includeArchived, limit, cursor, at) read POST /tasks add_task (201, Location: /api/v1/tasks/<number>) write GET /tasks/{taskId} get_task: { task, history } read PATCH /tasks/{taskId} update_task write DELETE /tasks/{taskId} delete_task (to the trash) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/complete complete_task ({ \"proof\": { \"summary\": \"…\", \"checks\": [ … ] } } optional) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/status set_status ({ \"status\": \"doing\" }) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/reschedule reschedule_task ({ \"dueDate\": \"2026-11-09\", \"dueTime\": \"07:00\" }) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/now set_now ({ \"resumeNote\": \"…\" } optional) write DELETE /now set_now with taskId: null write POST /tasks/{taskId}/archive archive_task write POST /tasks/{taskId}/restore restore_task write POST /tasks/{taskId}/claim claim_task ({ \"minutes\": 30 } optional; 409 while another agent holds it) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/claim/renew renew_claim ({ \"minutes\": 30 } optional) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/release release_task ({ \"note\": \"…\" } optional) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/accept accept_proposal: the proposal joins the plan (the owner: a cli key) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/merge merge_proposal ({ \"intoTaskId\": \"#12\" }; the owner) write POST /tasks/{taskId}/reject reject_proposal ({ \"reason\": \"…\" } optional; the owner) write GET /approvals list_approvals (query parameters status, taskId, limit) read POST /approvals request_approval (201; { \"action\": \"…\", \"options\": [ … ], \"reversible\": false }) write GET /approvals/{approvalId} get_approval read POST /approvals/{approvalId}/decide decide_approval ({ \"optionId\": \"o1\", \"note\": \"…\" }; the owner) write GET /agents get_agent_controls read POST /agents/pause pause_agents ({ \"paused\": true, \"reason\": \"…\" }; the owner) write POST /agents/policies set_tool_policy ({ \"apiKeyId\": \"…\", \"toolName\": \"delete_task\", \"rule\": \"deny\" }; the owner) write GET /health plan_health (query parameter at) read GET /events list_events (query parameters since, by, taskId, limit): the activity log (agent-work.md) read GET /today list_today read GET /board board read GET /trash list_trash read GET /export?format=markdown|json|ics export_plan read GET /settings, PATCH /settings get_settings, update_settings read, write GET /keys, POST /keys, DELETE /keys/{keyId} none (API keys) full GET/POST /webhooks, GET/DELETE /webhooks/{webhookId}, POST /webhooks/{webhookId}/test, POST /webhooks/{webhookId}/enable, GET /webhooks/{webhookId}/deliveries none (webhooks.md) full POST /import none (Import) full GET /openapi.json none; no key needed {taskId} is the task number (/tasks/42) or its id. Bodies are JSON (Content-Type: application/json; PATCH also takes application/merge-patch+json); a route whose tool takes no further arguments needs no body. Query parameters are typed as the tool's arguments: lists as repeated parameters or comma-separated (status=todo,doing), booleans as true/false. An unknown query parameter or body field is refused (422), like an unknown tool argument. Routes marked \"the owner\" run the owner's tools (mcp.md): a cli key may call them, an agent key gets 403 forbidden. Since 0.11.0 an agent key's changes also pass the owner's controls (agent-work.md): while agents are paused every route that changes something (keys and webhooks included) answers 403 agents_paused; a tool policy deny answers 403 forbidden, and ask answers 403 approval_required with approvalId: once the owner allows that request, send the same request again with \"approvalId\": \"…\" in the body. Results are the tool's structuredContent: an object as it is, a list or null as { \"result\": … } (GET /trash gives { \"result\": [ … ] }). The schemas are the ones in docs/schemas/, included in the OpenAPI document."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Changing a task: PATCH","u":"api.html#changing-a-task-patch","x":"PATCH /tasks/{taskId} is a JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386) of the task's fields: a field left out keeps its value, null clears it. curl -s -X PATCH \\ -H \"Authorization: Bearer $DAYHAND_API_KEY\" -H 'Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json' \\ -H 'If-Match: \"1793011200000\"' \\ -d '{\"note\": null, \"labels\": [\"work\"], \"dueDate\": \"tomorrow\"}' One difference from RFC 7386: an object or list value (repeat, labels, links, checklist, reminders) replaces the old one as a whole, as update_task does; it is not merged key by key. Status, now, archive and trash have their own routes."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Pages","u":"api.html#pages","x":"GET /tasks without limit returns every match, as list_all does. With limit (1 to 200) it returns a page, total (all matches) and nextCursor, and a Link: <…&cursor=…>; rel=\"next\" header while there is a next page."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Idempotency, versions and dry runs","u":"api.html#idempotency-versions-and-dry-runs","x":"Idempotency-Key: a unique id per change (e.g. a UUID), the operation's clientId. Sending the same change again with the same key does not apply it twice; POST /tasks returns the task created the first time. A body clientId that differs from the header is refused. ETag and If-Match: every answer about one task carries ETag, the task's version (it changes with every change to the task). Send it back in If-Match on a change and the change applies only while the task is still at that version; otherwise 412 precondition_failed and nothing changes, so a client never overwrites a change it has not seen. If-Match: * matches any version. A retry of an applied change with the same Idempotency-Key still succeeds, even though the version has moved on. GET /tasks/{taskId} with If-None-Match answers 304 while the task is unchanged. The version covers the stored task; derived fields such as overdue (which changes at midnight) are not part of it. ?dryRun=true on a change checks it and returns what it would produce, marked \"dryRun\": true, without saving anything (200, also for POST /tasks)."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Errors","u":"api.html#errors","x":"Errors are problem details (RFC 9457) with Content-Type: application/problem+json. code is the same code an MCP tool error has (mcp.md), and detail the same message: { \"type\": \" \"title\": \"Invalid argument\", \"status\": 422, \"detail\": \"dueDate must be YYYY-MM-DD, \\\"today\\\" or \\\"tomorrow\\\", got \\\"someday\\\"\", \"code\": \"invalid_argument\" } type names the code; it is an identifier, not a page to fetch. Status code When 400 invalid_argument The body is not JSON, or not a JSON object 401 unauthenticated No key, or an invalid, expired or revoked key, or an OAuth access token (WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm=\"dayhand\") 403 insufficient_scope The key's scope does not cover the route 403 agents_paused The owner paused every agent; an agent key's changes wait until they resume agents 403 forbidden Only the owner may do this, or the owner's policy denies the tool to this key, or the owner denied the approval 403 approval_required The owner's policy asks first; approvalId names the request to wait for 404 not_found No such task, key or webhook, or no such route 405 method_not_allowed The path exists with other methods (Allow lists them) 409 conflict Another agent holds the task's claim (claim_task) 412 precondition_failed If-Match does not match the task's version, or the task's state does not allow the change yet (an agent completing a task with unmet acceptance criteria, renewing a claim it does not hold) 413 payload_too_large A body over 256 KB (2 MB for POST /import) 415 unsupported_media_type A body that is not JSON 422 invalid_argument Wrong or missing arguments, an unknown field or query parameter, or a change the task's state does not allow 429 rate_limited Too many calls with this key (Retry-After, retryAfterMs) 500 internal Anything unexpected; the detail says only Internal error New codes may be added in a minor version; treat an unknown code like internal."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Limits","u":"api.html#limits","x":"The REST API shares the limits of the key on /mcp (mcp.md): 120 reads (GET) and 60 changes a minute per key, counted together over both surfaces. Every answer to an authenticated request carries: Header Meaning X-RateLimit-Limit Calls of this kind (reads or changes) a minute X-RateLimit-Remaining Calls of this kind left in the current minute X-RateLimit-Reset When the current minute ends, Unix seconds A call over the limit gets 429 with Retry-After (seconds) and is not applied."},{"p":"REST API","t":"API keys","u":"api.html#api-keys","x":"A full key manages keys for integrations: Route What it does GET /keys Every key (id, name, kind, scope, createdAt, expiresAt, revokedAt, createdWith); never a key or its hash POST /keys { \"name\": \"n8n\", \"scope\": \"read\", \"expiresAt\": 1798761600000, \"kind\": \"agent\" }: a new key, shown once in key (201) DELETE /keys/{keyId} Revokes a key; the keys it created stop with it A key created this way has scope read (the default) or write, never full; it expires no later than the key that created it, and it works only while that key works. So a key can never outlive its parent's revocation, and an integration can never mint keys of its own. dayhand key list, dayhand key create and dayhand key revoke use these routes."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Import","u":"api.html#import","x":"POST /import brings a plan into Dayhand from a file: format File What becomes of it dayhand-json Dayhand's own JSON export (dayhand export --format json, export_plan format json, version 1); helm-json, its name before the rename, is still accepted Every task outside the trash with all its fields: status, dates, repeat rule, labels, links, checklist, reminders, done and archived times. Task numbers are kept where this account never gave them, so an export into an empty account keeps its numbers. History and settings are not imported. ics iCalendar: VTODO and VEVENT SUMMARY → title, DESCRIPTION → note, DUE (or DTSTART) → day and time in your time zone (UTC and TZID times are converted), RRULE → repeat rule, CATEGORIES → labels, URL → link, STATUS:COMPLETED/IN-PROCESS → done/doing, VALARM before the start → reminders. Cancelled entries and changed occurrences of a series (RECURRENCE-ID) are skipped. Dayhand's own calendar file (dayhand calendar export) reads back without its ✓ and Dayhand #n marks. todoist-csv Todoist's CSV export of a project Rows of type task; @label words in the content → labels; the section → a label; note rows → the task's note; sub-tasks (INDENT 2 or more) → checklist items of their task; DATE in English (2026-11-03, today, tomorrow at 9am, Nov 20 15:30, a weekday, every day, every 2 weeks, every monday, friday, every weekday) → day, time and repeat rule. A date it does not understand stays in the note, with a warning. Priorities are not imported. Todoist's CSV format is not documented by Todoist; this reader follows its current export. curl -s -X POST \" \\ -H \"Authorization: Bearer $DAYHAND_API_KEY\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data \"$(jq -Rs '{content: .}' todoist.csv)\" The body is { \"content\": \"<the file's text>\", \"format\": \"auto\" }; auto (the default) recognises the format from the content. The answer (docs/schemas/import-result.schema.json) says how many tasks the file had (total), how many were imported (created, and the tasks in tasks), which were skipped and why, which failed add_task's checks (with the message; the others are imported all the same) and warnings about what could not be carried over. Every task is checked by the same rules as add_task, gets an import history entry with the key's actor, and sends task.created to webhooks. Importing the same file again imports nothing twice: each task's clientId is a hash of the format and its place in the file. A task from a Dayhand export whose id is still in this account is skipped too, so importing an export into the account it came from changes nothing. ?dryRun=true reads the file and checks every task without storing anything (dryRun: true). At most 2000 tasks per import; the server stores them in batches of 200, so a failure halfway leaves the first batches imported, and running the same import again completes it. dayhand import <file> [--format …] [--dry-run] sends a file this way."},{"p":"REST API","t":"OpenAPI","u":"api.html#openapi","x":"GET /api/v1/openapi.json serves the OpenAPI 3.1 document of this deployment (servers is its URL). It is generated from the same sources as the API: the route table, the tools' argument validators (the MCP inputSchema) and the result schemas of docs/schemas, so it cannot drift from what the server does. Each operation names its MCP tool (x-mcp-tool) and the key scope it needs (x-dayhand-scope). Load it into Swagger UI, Postman, n8n's HTTP Request node or a client generator. The tests validate it against the official OpenAPI 3.1 schema and check every answer they get against the response schema it gives. The document is public on purpose: it describes the API, not anyone's data. It is the same for every deployment of a version, apart from servers."},{"p":"REST API","t":"Not offered","u":"api.html#not-offered","x":"CORS: browsers cannot call the API from another site (keys do not belong in web pages). OAuth access tokens: they are for /mcp only. Server-sent events or long polling: use webhooks to hear about changes."},{"p":"Webhooks","t":"Webhooks","u":"webhooks.html","x":"Since backend 0.8.0, Dayhand can tell other systems about changes as they happen: a webhook is an HTTPS address that gets a signed POST for every change of a task. Messages follow Standard Webhooks, so any of its verification libraries (JavaScript, Python, Go, …) checks them. Webhooks are managed with a full API key through the REST API (api.md) or dayhand: dayhand webhook add --event task.completed --event task.created dayhand webhook list dayhand webhook test <id> dayhand webhook deliveries <id> dayhand webhook rm <id> --yes Route What it does GET /api/v1/webhooks Every webhook (never its secret) POST /api/v1/webhooks { \"url\": \" \"events\": [\"task.completed\"], \"description\": \"n8n\" } → { webhook, secret } (201); the secret is shown only here GET /api/v1/webhooks/{id} One webhook DELETE /api/v1/webhooks/{id} Removes it and its delivery log; messages still waiting are dropped POST /api/v1/webhooks/{id}/test Sends a webhook.test message now (202), also to a webhook that is turned off POST /api/v1/webhooks/{id}/enable Turns it back on after it was turned off for failing GET /api/v1/webhooks/{id}/deliveries?limit=50 The delivery log: newest first, each attempt with its time, duration, HTTP status or error, and the body sent A user may have at most 10 webhooks."},{"p":"Webhooks","t":"Events","u":"webhooks.html#events","x":"Type When task.created A task is added (also the next occurrence of a repeating task, and imported tasks) task.updated A task's fields, status or now flag change, it is archived, or it is claimed or released (claim_task, release_task; renewing a claim sends nothing) task.completed A task is marked done task.deleted A task goes to the trash task.restored A task comes back from the trash or the archive now.changed A task becomes the now task or stops being it (one message per task whose flag changed) approval.decided The owner decided an approval request (request_approval, since 0.11.0); data.approval is the request with its decision (schema) task.returned Only to the webhooks a task names in returnTo, whatever they subscribe to: the task was completed, rejected or merged into another task (since 0.11.0) webhook.test Only from POST …/test Leaving out events subscribes to the task events and now.changed; approval.decided has to be listed, because its data holds an approval, not a task. Changes from every surface count: the app, MCP, the REST API and dayhand. A dry run (dryRun) sends nothing, and neither does a change that fails: the message is queued in the same transaction as the change. The body (docs/schemas/webhook-event.schema.json): { \"type\": \"task.updated\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-03T09:12:44.120Z\", \"data\": { \"task\": { \"id\": \"…\", \"ref\": 42, \"title\": \"Dentist at 15:30\", \"status\": \"todo\", \"…\": \"…\" } }, \"previous\": { \"title\": \"Dentist\" }, \"actor\": { \"type\": \"agent\", \"name\": \"n8n\" } } data.task is the task after the change, in the same shape as everywhere else (schemas/task.schema.json). previous holds only the fields that changed, with their values before ({} for a new task). actor is who made the change: app, cli or agent and the key's or client's name. now.changed adds data.isNow. Fields may be added in a minor version. task.returned is how an agent or an integration that asked for a task hears back (agent-work.md). Its data holds outcome (completed, rejected or merged), task, proof (the task's proof or null), source, requestedBy, and reason (rejected) or mergedInto (merged: the task it went into, whose own completion sends completed later). A turned-off webhook gets nothing."},{"p":"Webhooks","t":"Verifying a message","u":"webhooks.html#verifying-a-message","x":"Every message carries three headers: Header Value webhook-id The message id, e.g. msg_kn74…. The same on every retry: store it and drop a message you have already handled webhook-timestamp When this attempt was sent, Unix seconds. Reject messages older than a few minutes (replays) webhook-signature v1,<base64>: HMAC-SHA256 of <webhook-id>.<webhook-timestamp>.<body> with the secret's bytes (the base64 after whsec_) With the reference library: import { Webhook } from \"standardwebhooks\"; const payload = new Webhook(process.env.DAYHAND_WEBHOOK_SECRET).verify(rawBody, headers); // throws when not genuine Verify the raw body exactly as received, before parsing it. To change a secret, add a new webhook, move the receiver to its secret, then remove the old one."},{"p":"Webhooks","t":"Delivery, retries and turning off","u":"webhooks.html#delivery-retries-and-turning-off","x":"A delivery succeeds on any 2xx answer within 10 seconds. Anything else (another status, a timeout, a refused connection, a redirect, which is never followed) is retried with the same webhook-id and body after 5 s, 5 min, 30 min, 2 h, 5 h, 10 h and 10 h: eight attempts in about 27 hours. Messages can arrive more than once and out of order; use webhook-id and the timestamp in the body. When 5 deliveries in a row fail on every attempt, the webhook is turned off: enabled: false, disabledAt and disabledReason in dayhand webhook list and GET /webhooks, and a webhook.disable entry in the history. Changes then send nothing to it. Fix the receiver, check it with dayhand webhook test <id>, and turn it on with dayhand webhook enable <id>. A successful delivery sets the count back to 0; test messages do not count. The delivery log keeps 30 days (a daily cron removes older entries)."},{"p":"Webhooks","t":"Security","u":"webhooks.html#security","x":"Only HTTPS on public hosts. Dayhand refuses (when the webhook is added, and again before each attempt) user names or passwords in the URL, localhost and names under .local, .internal, .localhost, .home.arpa, .lan, .corp, .intranet, single-label names, and IP addresses that are loopback, private, link-local (cloud metadata such as 169.254.169.254), carrier-grade NAT, multicast or reserved, in IPv4 or IPv6 (also IPv4-mapped). Redirects are not followed. What remains: a public name whose DNS points at a private address (DNS rebinding). Convex runs the requests from its own network, not the user's, which limits what such a name could reach; on a self-hosted deployment, keep the backend's outbound traffic away from internal services. DAYHAND_WEBHOOK_ALLOWED_ORIGINS (comma-separated origins, e.g. exempts those exact origins from the rules, for tests or a receiver on a self-hosted deployment's own network. Production leaves it unset. The secret is stored as it is, because every message is signed with it, and is shown only once. Anyone with admin access to the deployment can read it. A webhook sends the user's own tasks to an address the user chose, so managing webhooks needs a full key: a read or write key for an integration cannot add one. The message body has the task's title, note and other fields. Treat the receiver like any place that stores the plan."},{"p":"Webhooks","t":"Configuration","u":"webhooks.html#configuration","x":"Variable Default Meaning DAYHAND_WEBHOOK_ALLOWED_ORIGINS unset Origins exempt from the address rules (above) DAYHAND_WEBHOOK_RETRY_DELAYS 5,300,1800,7200,18000,36000,36000 Seconds before each retry; the number of values is the number of retries"},{"p":"Calendar feed and .ics export","t":"Calendar feed and .ics export","u":"calendar.html","x":"Dayhand can show your dated tasks in Google Calendar, Apple Calendar or any other calendar app that subscribes to a calendar by URL. The feed is read-only: the calendar shows the plan, and changes are made in Dayhand (the app, dayhand, the agent). Two-way sync (CalDAV) is not offered: Convex's HTTP router serves only GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS and PATCH, not CalDAV's PROPFIND and REPORT, and edits from two sides would need conflict handling that a read-only view does not."},{"p":"Calendar feed and .ics export","t":"What the calendar shows","u":"calendar.html#what-the-calendar-shows","x":"The same iCalendar text (RFC 5545, backend/convex/lib/ical.ts) serves the feed and the one-time .ics export: Task In the calendar Day and time An event of 30 minutes at that time, in the time zone from the settings (default Europe/London), with its VTIMEZONE Day only An all-day event No day, archived, in the trash Left out Done Kept for 90 days as a single event marked ✓, without alarms or repeat Repeating One event with the rule as RRULE (the same rule dayhand and MCP show as rrule) Reminders VALARMs relative to the start: the task's own reminders, else the default minutes from the settings (those need a time) SUMMARY is the title, DESCRIPTION is Dayhand #<number> and the note, CATEGORIES the labels. Events are TRANSP:TRANSPARENT: a task does not make you busy. UID is <task id>@helm and DTSTAMP the task's last change, so an unchanged plan gives the same text and calendar apps update events in place. Completing a repeating task creates the next task of the series (a new id), so the calendar gets the done occurrence and a new series. A repeating task's due date that is not itself a date of the rule (a Mon/Thu task due on a Wednesday) is its own event, and the series starts at the next date with its own UID (<task id>-next@helm): RFC 5545 leaves such a series undefined. UNTIL of a timed series is the end of that day in UTC, as RFC 5545 requires next to a TZID. A monthly rule on a 29th to 31st falls on the last day of shorter months in Dayhand. Its RRULE form needs BYMONTHDAY with BYSETPOS, which calendar apps do not all expand (ical.js ignores BYSETPOS there), so such a series is written as single events for two years ahead (UIDs <task id>-<YYYYMMDD>@helm); the feed moves that window along. A rule counted from completion (from: \"completion\") shows the dates it would have if each occurrence were done on its day; its real dates are not known in advance. The tests parse every output with ical.js (a separate RFC 5545 parser) and check that each repeat rule expands to exactly the dates of Dayhand's own lib/repeat.ts, in the time zone, across clock changes (backend/tests/ical.test.ts, calendar.test.ts)."},{"p":"Calendar feed and .ics export","t":"Turning it on","u":"calendar.html#turning-it-on","x":"Android: Settings → Calendar shows the address with Copy, Add to Google Calendar, Add to Apple Calendar, Create a new address and Turn off, and Export .ics, which shares a one-time file through the system share sheet. CLI: dayhand calendar on (or dayhand calendar to see it), dayhand calendar rotate, dayhand calendar off, dayhand calendar export -o dayhand.ics (cli.md). Then: Apple Calendar (iPhone, iPad, Mac): open the webcal:// address, or on a Mac File → New Calendar Subscription and paste the address. The subscription has a setting to remove alerts; leave it off to keep the reminders. Google Calendar: on the web (the phone app cannot add calendars by URL), Settings → Add calendar → From URL, and paste the https:// address; or open the \"Add to Google Calendar\" link. Google then shows it on the phone too. How fresh it is. The feed asks clients to look again every hour (REFRESH-INTERVAL, X-PUBLISHED-TTL) and lets them cache it for 5 minutes. Apple Calendar lets you pick the interval. Google Calendar refreshes subscribed calendars on its own schedule, usually every several hours, and ignores these hints; a change in Dayhand can take that long to show there. Google also does not take reminders from subscribed calendars. Neither could be checked from this repository (it needs a real Google account and time); treat both as reported behaviour."},{"p":"Calendar feed and .ics export","t":"HTTP endpoints","u":"calendar.html#http-endpoints","x":"Method and path Key Answer GET /cal/<token>.ics the token in the path 200 text/calendar; charset=utf-8; 404 for any wrong, old, malformed or revoked token GET /calendar/feed personal API key, scope full { \"feed\": { url, webcalUrl, googleCalendarUrl, createdAt } }, or { \"feed\": null } when off POST /calendar/feed personal API key, scope full turns it on (keeps an existing address) and returns it POST /calendar/feed/rotate personal API key, scope full a new address; the old one answers 404 at once POST /calendar/feed/revoke personal API key, scope full { \"revoked\": true } (or false when it was off) The app uses app:getCalendarFeed, app:createCalendarFeed ({ replace: true } for a new address) and app:revokeCalendarFeed. The export is export_plan with format: \"ics\" (MCP, dayhand export --format ics, app:exportPlan); its text is the calendar itself. Changes to the feed are recorded in the history (calendarFeed.create, .rotate, .revoke) without the address."},{"p":"Calendar feed and .ics export","t":"Threat model","u":"calendar.html#threat-model","x":"The address is a password to read the plan. Whoever has it reads every dated task: titles, notes, labels, times and reminders, until the address is replaced. It grants nothing else: no changes, no undated or archived tasks, no history, settings or keys. Threat Control What is left Guessing the address 44 base64url characters: a 72-bit selector and a 192-bit secret from crypto.getRandomValues, compared in constant time; any miss is the same plain 404 Nothing practical; there is no rate limit on misses The address leaks (shared calendar, screenshot, a calendar app's sync, a forwarded link) Create a new address (app) or dayhand calendar rotate: the old one answers 404 at once; Turn off / dayhand calendar off stops the feed. The app and dayhand say that the address is secret Whoever had it keeps what they already downloaded. Subscriptions must be added again with the new address Logs Dayhand's code never logs the address or writes it to the history; the feed sends Referrer-Policy: no-referrer and X-Robots-Tag: noindex The hosting platform logs request paths, the token included: the Convex local backend writes GET /cal/<token>.ics to its access log (seen in testing), and Convex Cloud and any proxy in between can see it too. Calendar providers (Google, Apple) store the address to poll it. This is inherent to a secret URL, which is what calendar apps support; Google's own \"secret address in iCal format\" has the same property Caches Cache-Control: private, max-age=300; no-store on the management endpoints A client may keep a copy for 5 minutes Stored secret The server keeps the secret itself (not a hash) so the address can be shown again on the phone and in dayhand. Whoever can read the database already reads the plan the address opens Database backups contain the address too Someone else's key manages your feed /calendar/feed* need a personal API key with scope full and act only on its owner's feed; OAuth connections (agents) cannot reach them An API key can show the address, as it can read the plan"},{"p":"Calendar feed and .ics export","t":"Out of scope","u":"calendar.html#out-of-scope","x":"CalDAV and two-way sync, per-calendar filters (one feed with everything dated), a REST API and webhooks, OAuth for calendars."},{"p":"Architecture","t":"Architecture","u":"architecture.html","x":""},{"p":"Architecture","t":"One operations layer, thin identity layers","u":"architecture.html#one-operations-layer-thin-identity-layers","x":"apps (Android, macOS, Linux) ──(JWT)──> convex/app.ts ┐ ├─> domain operations (convex/lib/*.ts) AI agent ──(MCP, API key)──> /mcp (convex/mcp.ts) ───┤ registered as internal functions Claude app ──(MCP, OAuth access token)──> /mcp ──────┤ in convex/ops.ts dayhand CLI ──(MCP tools/call, API key)──────────────┤ integration ──(REST /api/v1, API key)──> convex/rest.ts ┘ (same tools, lib/restRoutes.ts) apps ──(device token)──> /auth/token ─┐ ──(pairing code)──> /auth/pair ─┼─> convex/pairing.ts ──> convex/devices.ts dayhand CLI ──(API key)──> /devices/* ┘ Claude app ──> /.well-known/*, /oauth/* (convex/oauthHttp.ts) ──> convex/oauth.ts Android app ──(JWT)──> app.lookup/decideConnectionRequest ─┐ dayhand CLI ──(API key)──> /connections* ──────────────────┴──> convex/oauthOwner.ts calendar app ──(secret address)──> GET /cal/<token>.ics ─┐ Android app ──(JWT)──> app.*CalendarFeed ────────────────┼─> convex/calendarFeeds.ts ──> lib/ical.ts dayhand CLI ─(API key)─> /calendar/feed* (calendar.ts) ──┘ Domain operations take the acting identity explicitly: userId and actor (app, cli or agent), plus keyId and actorName for changes made with an API key. They are exposed as Convex internal functions (convex/ops.ts), so no client can call them directly. convex/app.ts takes userId from the signed-in user (ctx.auth) and sets actor: \"app\". convex/mcp.ts takes userId from the personal API key and sets actor to the key's kind (cli or agent), actorName to its name and keyId to its id; for an OAuth access token it sets actor: \"agent\", actorName to the client's name and connectionId to the connection, and allows only the tools of the token's scopes (oauth.md). Arguments that try to set any of these are rejected. The /devices/* endpoints do the same. Around it: lib/mcpTools.ts (tool catalog, input schemas from the validators, output schemas from docs/schemas via the generated lib/outputSchemas.ts, behaviour hints), lib/mcpContent.ts (resources and prompts, read through the same internal queries) and lib/mcpProtocol.ts (the 2025 and 2026-07-28 protocol eras on one endpoint). dryRun exists only on the MCP layer: the internal mutation runs the operation, every check included, then throws its result, so Convex rolls the transaction back (convex/ops.ts). The CLI is an MCP client too, so app, agent and CLI run exactly the same code. convex/rest.ts is the REST API (api.md): each route of lib/restRoutes.ts names the MCP tool it runs, so it calls the same internal function with the same arguments. It adds only HTTP: problem details (lib/problem.ts), Idempotency-Key as clientId, ETag and If-Match (checked inside the mutation, ops.ts), X-RateLimit-* from the same per-key counters as /mcp. lib/openapi.ts builds the OpenAPI 3.1 document from the same table, validators and docs/schemas; lib/toolResults.ts holds the result and error shapes both surfaces share. Webhooks: recordEvent (lib/core.ts), which every change of a task goes through, also queues a message for each live webhook of the user that wants its type (lib/webhookEvents.ts), in the same transaction, so a rolled-back change (a dry run, an error) sends nothing. convex/webhooks.ts delivers it from the scheduler, signed per Standard Webhooks (lib/webhookSign.ts), to an address checked against SSRF (lib/webhookTarget.ts), and retries it with the same webhook-id."},{"p":"Architecture","t":"Data model (convex/schema.ts)","u":"architecture.html#data-model-convexschemats","x":"Table Purpose tasks ref (the user's task number), title, note, dueDate (YYYY-MM-DD), optional dueTime (HH:MM), status (todo/doing/done), isNow, resumeNote, repeat, labels, links, checklist (items id, title, done), reminders (own reminders; absent = the defaults), agent work since 0.10.0 (agent-work.md): acceptance (criteria id, text, met), plan, proof, dependsOn, blockedReason, claimedBy, claimExpiresAt; proposals since 0.11.0: proposedAt (waiting in the inbox), source (kind, ref, url), requestedBy, returnTo (webhook ids), mergedInto; completedAt, archivedAt, deletedAt, createdAt, updatedAt, clientId taskEvents History: actor, keyId or connectionId and actorName (the API key or agent connection behind a cli or agent change), action, changed fields before and after, at, clientId, and for task changes seq, the position in the activity log (list_events) eventCounters Last activity log position given per user (lastSeq); every task change reads and writes it, so positions follow the commit order taskCounters Last task number given per user (lastRef) taskSources Since 0.11.0: which task each outside source (kind, ref) made, so one source makes one task; re-pointed when a proposal is merged, removed when the task is purged approvals Since 0.11.0: approval requests: taskId, title (what the agent wants to do), detail, options (id, label, consequence), reversible, expiresAt, requestedBy, source (agent or policy; a policy request also has tool, argsHash, arguments, usedAt), the decision (decidedAt, optionId, note, decidedBy), clientId agentControls Since 0.11.0: the owner's switch, one row per user: pausedAt, pausedBy, reason toolPolicies Since 0.11.0: one agent key's rule for one write tool or *: keyId, tool, rule (allow, ask, deny) apiKeys name, kind (cli or agent), scope (read, write or full; absent = full), expiresAt, parentKeyId (the key that created it through /api/v1/keys), SHA-256 hash of the key, createdAt, revokedAt pairingCodes One-time pairing codes: public selector, SHA-256 secretHash of the secret part, createdBy, expiresAt (10 minutes), usedAt, failedAttempts devices Paired phones: name, token selector, SHA-256 secretHash, pairedBy, createdAt, lastUsedAt, revokedAt authLimits Fixed-window counters, one row per bucket: failed POST /auth/pair attempts, and the OAuth limits (oauth:*: requests per client, registrations, wrong codes typed on the phone) callLimits MCP calls per API key or agent connection in the current one-minute window: keyId or connectionId, kind (read or write), windowStart, calls oauthClients Clients from Dynamic Client Registration: clientId (helmc_…), name, redirectUris, createdAt, lastUsedAt (clients with a metadata document URL are not stored) oauthRequests Authorization requests waiting for the phone: browser handle selector + secretHash, SHA-256 of the code shown (userCodeHash), client, redirectUri, state, PKCE codeChallenge, scopes, resource, then the decision (userId, grantedScopes, approvedAt/deniedAt) and the single-use authorization code (codeHash, codeExpiresAt, codeUsedAt) oauthGrants Agent connections: userId, client (clientId, clientType, clientName), redirectUri, scopes, resource, createdAt, lastUsedAt, refreshExpiresAt, revokedAt oauthTokens Access and refresh tokens of a connection: kind, selector, SHA-256 secretHash, expiresAt, rotatedAt (refresh), scopes (after a narrowing refresh) calendarFeeds The read-only calendar feed, at most one per user: selector and secret of the address /cal/<selector><secret>.ics (the secret is kept so the address can be shown again, calendar.md), createdAt webhooks HTTPS endpoints for task events (webhooks.md): url, events, description, signing secret (whsec_…, kept to sign), failureStreak, lastSuccessAt, lastFailureAt, disabledAt, disabledReason webhookDeliveries The delivery log: one message to one webhook, eventId (the webhook-id, the same on every retry), type, the exact payload, status (pending, succeeded, failed), attempts (time, duration, HTTP status o"},{"p":"Architecture","t":"Rules","u":"architecture.html#rules","x":"Today and overdue. \"Today\" is the calendar date in the user's time zone. A task is overdue when it is not done, not archived, not in the trash and its dueDate is before today. This is decided in one place, convex/lib/dates.ts (localDate, isOverdue). Views accept an optional at (Unix ms) so clients can re-evaluate after midnight; Convex caches queries, so a subscribed client should pass the current time. One now task. setNow clears isNow on every other task of the user and moves the chosen task to doing. Leaving doing, completing, archiving or deleting clears it. Repeat. Completing a repeating task creates the next occurrence: the first date of the series after the due date that is also after today (a late completion does not create an overdue task), or, for a rule counted from completion, the first date after today. A series ends with until or count. The logic is one pure module, convex/lib/repeat.ts: advance for completing and occurrences for listing a series ahead; convex/lib/rrule.ts maps rules to and from RFC 5545 RRULE (mcp.md). The Android app has the same logic in domain/Recurrence.kt. The owner's controls. Every change an agent makes (an agent key or an agent connection) first passes gateAgentWrite (convex/lib/agentControls.ts) inside the same mutation: paused agents are refused, a key's tool policy deny is refused, and ask needs an approval request decided \"allow\", used up by that change. The app and cli keys are never refused. Proposals (proposedAt) are left out of Today, the board, list_all, ready and the calendar until accepted (agent-work.md). Trash. softDelete sets deletedAt; restore clears it. A daily cron (convex/crons.ts, 03:30 UTC) permanently removes tasks deleted more than 30 days ago, with their history. Task numbers. Adding a task (and a repeat's next occurrence) takes the next number from the user's taskCounters row. Numbers only grow, so a purged task's number is never given again. Every operation that takes a taskId also accepts \"#<number>\". History. Every change writes a taskEvents row with the actor and only the changed fields. History is shown as app, cli or agent: <key or client name>. Settings, API key and device changes are recorded without a task id. Idempotency. Every mutation accepts an optional clientId. Adding a task with a clientId that already exists returns the existing task; any other operation whose clientId is already in the history is not applied again. The Android outbox relies on this."},{"p":"Architecture","t":"Authentication","u":"architecture.html#authentication","x":"Agent and CLI: personal API keys (dayhand_ + 32 random bytes, base64url), each with a name and a kind (cli or agent, default agent) that becomes the actor of its changes. Keys are generated in an action (mutations have a deterministic random source), shown once, and stored as SHA-256 only. A missing, wrong or revoked key gets HTTP 401. Each key may make 120 reads and 60 changes a minute over MCP (convex/callLimits.ts); a call over the limit is refused before it runs. Agent connections (OAuth 2.1): the Claude apps and other MCP clients get an access token (helmat_…, one hour) and a rotating refresh token (helmrt_…, 60 days) through an authorization code with PKCE, after the owner approves the request on the paired phone by the code its sign-in page shows. Tokens are selector + secret, stored as SHA-256, bound to <issuer>/mcp, and stop at once when the connection is revoked. Protocol and limits: oauth.md; threats: oauth-threat-model.md. App: a custom ES256 JWT. The deployment trusts the public key given in DAYHAND_JWKS (a JWKS data: URI) for issuer DAYHAND_JWT_ISSUER, audience helm; userId is the token identifier <issuer>|<subject>. The deployment itself signs these tokens with DAYHAND_JWT_PRIVATE_JWK, an environment variable read only by POST /auth/token (convex/lib/appToken.ts); it checks that the key matches DAYHAND_JWKS and answers 503 when it does not. Pairing: dayhand pair (or the admin) creates a one-time code, valid 10 minutes. The phone exchanges it at POST /auth/pair for a long-lived, revocable device token, and the device token at POST /auth/token for an app JWT valid one hour. Codes and device tokens are selector + secret; only SHA-256 of the secret is stored and compared in constant time. Pairing failures are limited per deployment. dayhand devices and dayhand revoke manage devices; a revoked device gets no new JWTs. Protocol: pairing.md; deployment and key migration: deploy.md. Tokens by hand: scripts/jwt.mjs creates a key pair and tokens for tests; scripts/app-token.mjs (npm run app-token) signs a long-lived token from a key file for scripts and local development. The Android app pairs instead (version 0.2 took such a token pasted, and keeps one stored until the phone is paired)."},{"p":"Architecture","t":"Reminders","u":"architecture.html#reminders","x":"Reminders and the morning summary are computed on the devices, not on the server: the server stores only the settings (summaryTime, defaultReminderMinutes) and each task's own reminders (which replace the defaults for that task). Each app plans them from its local copy of the tasks, in the time zone from these settings, with the same rules: the Android app arms AlarmManager (android/README.md), the macOS app leaves notifications with the system (macos/README.md) and the Linux app rings them while it runs (linux.md)."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"Security and privacy","u":"security.html","x":"Dayhand has no hosted service and no accounts of its own. Your plan lives on your devices and on a server you run, and nothing in Dayhand sends it anywhere else. This page says what is stored where, which secrets exist and how they are protected, what the agent controls can and cannot do, and how to report a problem. The detailed threat models are linked at the end."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"Where your plan is","u":"security.html#where-your-plan-is","x":"Place What it holds Who can read it Your server (a Convex deployment) the whole plan, its history, settings, hashes of keys and tokens, webhook secrets, the calendar address you, and anyone with admin access to that deployment: your Convex account, or your computer for the local server The Android app its copy of the plan and the waiting changes; the sign-in, encrypted with a key in the Android Keystore the phone; Android's cloud backup and device transfer are off for Dayhand Dayhand for macOS its copy of the plan in ~/Library/Application Support/Dayhand; the sign-in in the login Keychain your Mac user Dayhand for Linux its copy in ~/.local/share/dayhand; the sign-in in the Secret Service keyring your Linux user dayhand and dayhand ui no offline plan or outbox; shell completion caches task numbers, titles and labels; the key is in the system keychain, or a file only you can read your user on that computer Backups and exports whatever you export: dayhand export, the phone's backup file, npx convex export wherever you keep them Shell completion writes tasks-<hash>.json in the CLI cache folder: ~/.cache/dayhand (or $XDG_CACHE_HOME/dayhand) on Linux, ~/Library/Caches/dayhand on macOS, or %LOCALAPPDATA%\\dayhand\\Cache on Windows; DAYHAND_CACHE_DIR overrides it. The folder is created with mode 700 and files with mode 600 where POSIX permissions apply. The 60-second lifetime controls freshness, not deletion: cached titles and labels remain on disk until you remove the cache files. The local server (dayhand server start) keeps its data and keys in a private folder (mode 700, files 600) and listens on 127.0.0.1 only. A server on your Convex account runs on Convex's infrastructure, in your account; Convex can technically reach it, as any host can reach what it hosts."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"No tracking","u":"security.html#no-tracking","x":"The Android app has no analytics, no crash reporting and no other outside services. It talks only to your server. Dayhand for macOS and Linux talk only to your server. A macOS build made with the release key also checks the update feed (Sparkle), with Sparkle's system profile off; builds you make yourself never do. dayhand talks only to the server you signed it in to. dayhand server start downloads the Convex local backend from Convex's GitHub releases once, at a pinned version whose checksum it checks. This website sets no cookies and loads nothing from other servers."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"Secrets and how they are kept","u":"security.html#secrets-and-how-they-are-kept","x":"Secret Made by Kept as Ends API key (dayhand_…) dayhand server start or deploy, dayhand key create, the deployment admin shown once; the server stores only its SHA-256 when revoked or at its expiry; keys made with a key stop with it Pairing code dayhand pair 12 characters, the secret part stored as SHA-256 after one use, 10 minutes, or 5 wrong tries Device token (a paired phone or computer) POST /auth/pair on the device, encrypted (Keystore, Keychain, Secret Service); SHA-256 on the server when the device is revoked (dayhand revoke) App token (JWT) the server, from a device token in memory on the device after one hour OAuth tokens (the Claude apps) /oauth/token, after you approve on the phone SHA-256 on the server access one hour; refresh 60 days, rotated on every use; at once when the connection is revoked App-signing key dayhand server start or deploy in the deployment's environment; a copy in the server folder when you rotate it (rotating) Calendar address turning the feed on the secret part on the server, so it can be shown again when you make a new address or turn the feed off Webhook secret (whsec_…) dayhand webhook add on the server as it is, since every message is signed with it when the webhook is removed Keys and tokens are long random values compared in constant time; error answers never say whether a code or token existed, expired or was revoked. A key never goes into config.toml: dayhand login keeps it in the macOS Keychain, the Windows Credential Manager or the Secret Service, or in a file readable only by you when there is none. The keychain keeps the key out of dotfiles and shell history; it does not hide it from programs running as you."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"Who may do what","u":"security.html#who-may-do-what","x":"Scopes. An API key reads (read), also changes tasks and settings (write), or also manages keys, webhooks, devices, connections and the calendar feed (full). A key made with another key is read or write and never outlives its parent. Owner tools. Deciding approval requests, pausing agents, tool policies and accepting, merging or rejecting proposals are for you only: the apps and cli keys. Agents do not see these tools and are refused when they call them. Agent controls are checked inside the same transaction as the change, for MCP and the REST API alike, so there is no way around them through another surface (agents). Limits. Each key and each OAuth connection may make 120 reads and 60 changes a minute. What Dayhand cannot do: stop an agent from acting outside Dayhand. An approval is a record the agent is expected to respect; enforce it in the agent's own tooling as well."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"The server's doors","u":"security.html#the-servers-doors","x":"/mcp refuses requests from a browser page (an Origin header) unless you allow that origin, and answers a missing or wrong credential with 401. The REST API has no CORS: other websites cannot call it from a browser, and OAuth tokens do not work there. Webhooks are sent only to public HTTPS addresses: local, private, link-local and reserved addresses are refused when the webhook is added and again before each delivery, and redirects are not followed (webhooks). OAuth sign-ins return only to the Claude apps' documented addresses, with PKCE, and every connection is approved on your phone (OAuth threat model)."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"Good habits","u":"security.html#good-habits","x":"Give every agent and integration its own key, with the smallest scope and an expiry. Revoke it when it is no longer used (dayhand key list, dayhand key revoke). A key can pair a phone, and the phone keeps working after the key is revoked: when a key may have leaked, revoke it and also check dayhand devices. Lost a phone or a laptop: dayhand revoke <deviceId>. Its app token still works for up to an hour. Treat the calendar address like a password. If it was shared, make a new one (dayhand calendar rotate). Keep the copy of the app-signing key in a password manager, or delete it; the deployment holds the one in use. Keep backups and exports out of shared folders: they contain your plan. Keep the backend up to date: git pull and dayhand server deploy (or start) again (updating)."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"Reporting a problem","u":"security.html#reporting-a-problem","x":"Report security problems privately, never in a public issue, pull request or discussion. See SECURITY.md for reporting steps, what to include and response times."},{"p":"Security and privacy","t":"Threat models and details","u":"security.html#threat-models-and-details","x":"OAuth threat model: the Claude apps' sign-in, with each threat, its control and its test. Calendar feed: the secret calendar address. Phone pairing: codes, device tokens and app tokens. Architecture: how each kind of client proves who it is."},{"p":"OAuth threat model","t":"OAuth threat model","u":"oauth-threat-model.html","x":"What the OAuth server (oauth.md) protects, from whom, how, and what is left. It covers /.well-known/*, /oauth/*, /connections*, the OAuth path of /mcp and the approval in the Android app. API keys, pairing and app JWTs are described in architecture.md and pairing.md."},{"p":"OAuth threat model","t":"Assets","u":"oauth-threat-model.html#assets","x":"The owner's plan: tasks, history and settings, read and changed through /mcp. Access and refresh tokens of agent connections, authorization codes, and the codes shown on the sign-in page. The owner's decision: only the owner, on the paired phone, may let an agent in, and only with the scopes the owner chose."},{"p":"OAuth threat model","t":"Actors and trust boundaries","u":"oauth-threat-model.html#actors-and-trust-boundaries","x":"Actor Trusted with Owner with the paired phone Every decision; the app talks to app.* with its JWT Owner with an API key (dayhand) Listing and revoking connections (/connections*) Claude app, Claude Code Its own tokens after approval; nothing more. Its name comes from its metadata document on Any other MCP client Same as Claude once approved, but its name is self-asserted (Dynamic Client Registration) Anyone on the internet Nothing: every /oauth/* endpoint is open to them by design The browser that opens the sign-in page is not trusted: it proves nothing about who uses it. The phone is what makes a request legitimate."},{"p":"OAuth threat model","t":"Threats and controls","u":"oauth-threat-model.html#threats-and-controls","x":"# Threat Control Test T1 Code interception: an attacker gets an authorization code (a log, a redirect) and exchanges it PKCE S256 required (no plain, no request without it); the code is bound to client, redirect URI and resource, lives 2 minutes and dies on its first presentation, right or wrong oauth.test.ts: wrong verifier burns the code; code bound to client, redirect URI and resource T2 Code replay: the same code is exchanged twice A second presentation fails and revokes the connection the first one created \"a code used twice fails and revokes the connection\" T3 Open redirect / code theft by redirect URI: a client registers and gets the owner's code Only Claude's documented callbacks are accepted, at registration and in metadata documents: exactly, and / with path /callback on any port; no other host, scheme, path, query, fragment, credentials or IP spelling. Before the redirect URI is checked, errors are an error page, never a redirect oauthRules.test.ts (17 refused spellings), \"a wrong redirect URI gets an error page\" T4 Impersonation of Claude: a client calls itself \"Claude\" The phone says Identified by claude.ai only for a metadata document fetched from an allowed origin (default only); a self-registered client is shown as Registered itself (its name is not verified). Names are cleaned of control and bidi characters and cut to 60 Instrumented ConnectionsScreenTest, cleanName tests T5 Consent phishing: an attacker opens the sign-in page with their own client and sends the owner the code (\"type this in Dayhand\") The code works only on the owner's phone and shows who asks and where the answer goes; a request older than 3 minutes gets a red warning; an answer to a program on the computer (loopback) gets a warning; the page and the app tell the owner to approve only a page they opened themselves. The approved code still goes to the page that asked, so the attacker must also hold the redirect URI: Claude's own callback, or a loopback on the attacker's machine ConnectionsViewModelTest (\"a request minutes old is flagged\"), instrumented consent tests T6 Guessing the code typed on the phone 8 Crockford base32 characters (40 bits), valid 10 minutes, at most 5 waiting per client; 10 wrong codes per user per 10 minutes, then the app refuses \"wrong codes are limited per user\" T7 Token theft or guessing Tokens are a selector plus 256 random bits, stored as SHA-256 only, compared in constant time, never logged or shown. Access tokens last one hour — (format and storage by construction) T8 Refresh token theft Rotation on every refresh; a rotated token used again more than 30 seconds later revokes the connection (both parties lose it, the owner sees it on the phone) \"reusing a refresh token rotated long ago revokes the connection\" T9 Token for another server (confused deputy) Every token is bound to <issuer>/mcp (RFC 8707); /mcp refuses a token bound to anything else; resource must name /mcp when sent \"a token issued for another resource (audience) gets 401\" T10 More access than granted Scopes tasks:read and tasks:write only; no broad default (a request without scope gets reading only); the phone may grant a part, never more; tools/list is filtered and other tools, user resources and prompts get 403 insufficient_scope; a refresh can only narrow \"a read-only connection…\", \"a write-only connection…\", \"a refresh may narrow the scopes\" T11 Escalation from a connection: an agent mints API keys, pairs phones or approves itself OAuth tokens work only on /mcp; /devices* and /connections* take API keys only; the approval needs the app's JWT; identity fields in tool arguments are refused \"OAuth tokens cannot manage devices or connections\" T12 Revocation that does not bite A revoked connection's access and refresh tokens are refused on their next use, not when they expire \"revoking a connection on the phone or with dayhand stops its tokens at once\", RFC 7009 test T13 SSRF through metadata URLs Only URLs on DAYHAND_OAUTH_CIMD_ORIGINS (default are f"},{"p":"OAuth threat model","t":"Residual risks","u":"oauth-threat-model.html#residual-risks","x":"Blocking Claude's published identity. The client_id of the Claude apps is public, so anyone can start 20 requests with it every 10 minutes and keep 5 waiting, which delays new Claude connections (not existing ones) for up to 10 minutes. The workaround is \"Register automatically\" (Dynamic Client Registration), whose client_id is private to that registration. The global flood guards (300 request starts per 10 minutes, 100 registrations per hour) can be filled the same way; npx convex run oauth:resetLimits clears them. Nobody gets access this way. Consent phishing with a loopback client. An attacker who persuades the owner to approve a code, despite the warnings, gets a connection through a loopback redirect on the attacker's own machine. The warnings and the request's age are the only defence; the owner sees the connection under Agent connections and can disconnect it. A stolen refresh token used within 30 seconds of the legitimate refresh is taken for a retry. The legitimate client's next refresh then fails, and a later use of an old token revokes the connection. Metadata documents are fetched on every authorization request (no cache): at most 20 per client per 10 minutes, to claude.ai only. Self-asserted names of registered clients can be anything printable; the app marks them as unverified. Revoking an app JWT (a lost phone) takes up to an hour (pairing.md); during that hour the phone could still approve requests. Revoke the device with dayhand revoke. Not tested against the real Claude apps before a production deployment: Claude reaches the server from Anthropic's network over public HTTPS. The flows are tested with a simulator (the MCP SDK's OAuth client and tests/oauthClient.ts) and on an emulator (android/scripts/e2e-connections.py)."},{"p":"OAuth threat model","t":"Out of scope","u":"oauth-threat-model.html#out-of-scope","x":"Client secrets, confidential clients, the JWT bearer and client credentials grants, token introspection, OpenID Connect and sign-in on the web: Dayhand has no web accounts, and the paired phone is the only way in."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"FAQ and troubleshooting","u":"faq.html","x":""},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Questions","u":"faq.html#questions","x":""},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Is there a hosted Dayhand, or an account to create?","u":"faq.html#is-there-a-hosted-dayhand-or-an-account-to-create","x":"No. You run the server yourself, on your computer or on your own Convex account, or use the apps with no server at all. Dayhand is a personal open source project with no hosted service."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"What does it cost?","u":"faq.html#what-does-it-cost","x":"Nothing: Dayhand is free and open source under the Apache License 2.0. A server on your computer costs nothing, and so does Convex's Free plan, whose limits are far above one person's plan (what it costs)."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Can several people share one plan?","u":"faq.html#can-several-people-share-one-plan","x":"Not as separate people. A Dayhand server has one owner, and the phone, the computers and every agent work on that owner's plan. Agents and integrations get keys of their own, so their changes are told apart. Another person runs a server of their own."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Which agents can use it?","u":"faq.html#which-agents-can-use-it","x":"Any MCP client that can send an Authorization header (Claude Code is one command), the Claude apps as a custom connector with OAuth, and any agent that runs shell commands through dayhand. See Agents and you."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Can an agent do something I did not allow?","u":"faq.html#can-an-agent-do-something-i-did-not-allow","x":"Inside Dayhand, no: a policy per agent key allows, asks for or denies each tool, risky steps wait for your answer when the agent asks, and one switch pauses every agent. Dayhand cannot stop what an agent does outside Dayhand, such as running commands on your computer."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Does it work offline?","u":"faq.html#does-it-work-offline","x":"Yes. The Android, macOS and Linux apps keep the plan on the device, and reminders are worked out there. Changes wait in an outbox and go to the server in order when it can be reached. dayhand and dayhand ui need the server."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Can I see my tasks in Google Calendar or Apple Calendar?","u":"faq.html#can-i-see-my-tasks-in-google-calendar-or-apple-calendar","x":"Yes, through a read-only feed that calendar apps subscribe to by address: Settings → Data → Calendar on the phone, or dayhand calendar on (calendar feed). Changes are made in Dayhand."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Can I bring my tasks from another app, and take them out again?","u":"faq.html#can-i-bring-my-tasks-from-another-app-and-take-them-out-again","x":"dayhand import reads Dayhand's own JSON export, iCalendar files (tasks and events) and Todoist's CSV export (import). dayhand export writes the whole plan as Markdown, JSON (with the history) or iCalendar, and the phone saves a backup file. Backlog.md import and export are planned."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Is there an app for iPhone or Windows?","u":"faq.html#is-there-an-app-for-iphone-or-windows","x":"Not yet. The iPhone and iPad app is in development, with nothing to install yet. On Windows, dayhand works with a server on your own Convex account (dayhand server deploy); there is no desktop app."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Which languages does Dayhand speak?","u":"faq.html#which-languages-does-dayhand-speak","x":"The Android, macOS and Linux apps and dayhand ui speak English, Polish, German, Spanish, French, Brazilian Portuguese, Japanese and Simplified Chinese. Date phrases (fri 2pm) are English everywhere, and so are the other dayhand commands, --json and the MCP tools."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Why do some names still say helm?","u":"faq.html#why-do-some-names-still-say-helm","x":"Dayhand was called Helm until backend 0.9.0 and CLI 0.10.0. The old hlm command and HELM_* names still work with a warning, and a few stored names stay as they were (renamed from Helm). The repository moved to maiarowsky/dayhand; links to maiarowsky/helm redirect."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Troubleshooting","u":"faq.html#troubleshooting","x":""},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"dayhand: command not found","u":"faq.html#dayhand-command-not-found","x":"npm link puts the command in npm's global folder. Check that npm prefix -g followed by /bin is on your PATH, or run node cli/dist/bin/dayhand.js from the checkout. Where npm link needs administrator rights, use that path instead of sudo."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"dayhand says there is no key (exit 3)","u":"faq.html#dayhand-says-there-is-no-key-exit-3","x":"dayhand whoami shows which server, profile and key source it uses. dayhand server start and dayhand server deploy sign in by themselves; for a server that already runs, run dayhand login --url <the server's address> with a key of kind cli. A key in DAYHAND_API_KEY wins over the stored one, so an old value there can hide a good login. On Linux without a keyring (a server, a minimal desktop), dayhand login --store file keeps the key in a file only you can read (login)."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"dayhand version says the server is not compatible","u":"faq.html#dayhand-version-says-the-server-is-not-compatible","x":"The CLI needs a server with the same major version that is not older than the one it was built for. Update the server: git pull, then dayhand server deploy or dayhand server start again (updating)."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"dayhand server start does not start","u":"faq.html#dayhand-server-start-does-not-start","x":"It runs on macOS and Linux (x64 or arm64), not on Windows: use dayhand server deploy there. When ports 3210 or 3211 are taken, choose others with --port and --site-port. dayhand server start --dry-run lists every step without changing anything. Run it from inside the checkout, or pass --backend <path to backend/>."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"The phone cannot connect","u":"faq.html#the-phone-cannot-connect","x":"The server is on your computer. It listens on 127.0.0.1, which the phone cannot reach, and release builds of the app need HTTPS. Use tailscale serve and dayhand server start --public-url … (Phone and computer), or a server on your Convex account. To check the address from the phone, open in its browser: it should show a JSON document. The code is refused. A code works once, for 10 minutes; make a new one with dayhand pair. After 10 wrong codes in 10 minutes the server stops checking codes for the rest of that window; wait, or clear it from backend/ with npx convex run devices:resetPairLimit and the deployment's --env-file. \"Replace the plan on this phone?\" The code is for another server than the one whose plan is on the phone. Cancel keeps everything; save a backup file first if you want to keep those tasks. \"The server signed this phone out.\" The device was revoked. Make a new code with dayhand pair; the tasks and waiting changes are still on the phone."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Reminders do not ring on Android","u":"faq.html#reminders-do-not-ring-on-android","x":"Settings → Notifications says whether notifications and exact alarms are allowed, with a button for each. Without exact alarms, reminders can come a few minutes late. Overdue tasks never ring, and a reminder more than 15 minutes late (the phone was off or saving battery) is dropped. Minutes before the due time need a task with a time; use a time of day for tasks without one. After a force stop, Android drops the alarms until you open Dayhand again."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"The days are wrong","u":"faq.html#the-days-are-wrong","x":"\"Today\", reminders and date phrases follow the time zone in the server's settings, Europe/London until you change it: dayhand settings shows it and dayhand settings set timezone=Europe/Warsaw changes it. On the phone, Settings → Server offers Use this phone's time zone when the two differ."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"A change was refused","u":"faq.html#a-change-was-refused","x":"Message or exit code Meaning What to do exit 5, rate_limited the key made more than 120 reads or 60 changes this minute wait as long as the message says, then run it again exit 5, network or server error the change may or may not have been applied run the same command with the --client-id from the error; it is never applied twice exit 6, conflict another agent holds the task's claim pick another task, or wait until the time named exit 6, precondition_failed an agent completed a task whose criteria are not all met, or without a summary tick the criteria (dayhand criteria) and add --summary exit 7, agents_paused you paused every agent dayhand agents resume, from a cli key or the phone exit 7, forbidden only the owner may do this, or a policy denies the tool to this key use a cli key, or change the policy exit 7, approval_required a policy asks first decide the request on the phone, then rerun with --approval <id>"},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"The Claude app shows \"Unknown client\" when connecting","u":"faq.html#the-claude-app-shows-unknown-client-when-connecting","x":"Remove the connector and add it again choosing Register automatically for the OAuth client. The steps and what else to check: Claude apps."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Google Calendar does not show a change","u":"faq.html#google-calendar-does-not-show-a-change","x":"Google refreshes subscribed calendars on its own schedule, often every several hours, and ignores the feed's hint to look again every hour; it also ignores the reminders of a subscribed calendar. Apple Calendar lets you choose how often it refreshes (calendar feed)."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"A webhook stopped receiving messages","u":"faq.html#a-webhook-stopped-receiving-messages","x":"After 5 deliveries in a row that failed every attempt, a webhook is turned off. dayhand webhook list says so and why, and dayhand webhook deliveries <id> shows each attempt. Fix the receiver, check it with dayhand webhook test <id>, then dayhand webhook enable <id> (webhooks)."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"macOS asks whether Dayhand may use its Keychain item","u":"faq.html#macos-asks-whether-dayhand-may-use-its-keychain-item","x":"Every build you make yourself is a new, unsigned app to macOS, so after each rebuild it may ask once. Allow it; the item holds the Mac's sign-in to your server."},{"p":"FAQ and troubleshooting","t":"Something else","u":"faq.html#something-else","x":"Run the failing command with --json to see the error code, check dayhand version and dayhand whoami, and look at the server's logs (npx convex logs in backend/ with the deployment's --env-file). A bug you can reproduce is welcome as an issue; a security problem goes privately (security)."},{"p":"Contributing","t":"Contributing","u":"contributing.html","x":"Dayhand is open source under the Apache License 2.0, and contributions are welcome. The rules for a change, signing off your commits, the commit format and the tests each part needs are in CONTRIBUTING.md; this page is a map of the repository and of these docs."},{"p":"Contributing","t":"The repository","u":"contributing.html#the-repository","x":"Folder What it is Start with backend/ the Convex backend: data model, operations, MCP, REST API, webhooks, OAuth architecture cli/ dayhand, the command line and dayhand ui cli/README.md android/ the Android app (Kotlin, Compose) android/README.md macos/ Dayhand for macOS (Swift, SwiftUI) macos/README.md linux/ Dayhand for Linux (Qt 6, QML, C++) linux/README.md packaging/ the Arch Linux package and the Homebrew formula template docs/ these pages, the JSON schemas of --json and the MCP results, the Agent Skill below man/ the manual pages, generated from the CLI site/ dayhand.dev: the landing page and the built docs site/STYLE.md scripts/ checks and builders for the README and the site Test setup and the checks each change needs: backend, CLI."},{"p":"Contributing","t":"Writing these docs","u":"contributing.html#writing-these-docs","x":"Edit the Markdown in docs/, not the generated pages in site/docs/. Rebuild and check commands are in CONTRIBUTING.md; how the generated site works is in site/STYLE.md. Which files become pages, their titles in the menu and their order are in scripts/docs/pages.mjs. A new page goes there. Link to other pages by their file name ([webhooks](webhooks.md#events)). Links to any other file in the repository become links to it on GitHub. A link to a missing file or heading stops the build. For generated command references and the Agent Skill, follow the CLI contribution instructions instead of editing the output. Write only what is true in main. Something that is not released or not built yet is marked as planned or coming soon, with no command for it. Check a command before you document it. English, short sentences, plain words."},{"p":"Contributing","t":"Translations","u":"contributing.html#translations","x":"The apps and dayhand ui speak eight languages. Each part keeps its texts in one place and has a check (a test or lint) that fails when a text is missing in a language: Android: android/app/src/main/res/values*/strings.xml macOS: macos/Sources/DayhandLocalization/Localizable.xcstrings Linux: linux/translations/dayhand_<language>.ts dayhand ui: cli/src/i18n/ Corrections from native speakers are welcome."},{"p":"Contributing","t":"Releases","u":"contributing.html#releases","x":"The CLI is released on its own with release-please, and nothing is published yet. How a release will work, for maintainers: release.md."},{"p":"Renamed from Helm","t":"Renamed from Helm","u":"rename.html","x":"Helm became Dayhand in backend 0.9.0, CLI 0.10.0 and Android app 0.9.0. Nothing has to change at once; the old names keep working with a deprecation warning: Before Now Old name hlm command dayhand, short dh hlm still runs until CLI 1.0 and warns on every run CLI variables HLM_*, HELM_URL, HELM_API_KEY, HELM_BACKEND_DIR, HELM_SERVER_CONFIG_DIR DAYHAND_* read while the DAYHAND_* name is unset, with a warning CLI config folder hlm (e.g. ~/.config/hlm) dayhand used while there is no dayhand folder, with a warning saying where to move it keychain entry hlm dayhand read when there is no dayhand entry; dayhand logout removes both server folder helm-server dayhand-server used while there is no dayhand-server folder deployment variables HELM_* DAYHAND_* read while DAYHAND_* is unset (deploy.md) Android build: -Phelm.serverUrl, HELM_RELEASE_* -Pdayhand.serverUrl, DAYHAND_RELEASE_* read when the new one is unset (Gradle warns) API keys helm_… new keys start with dayhand_ old keys work until revoked MCP resources pairing links still accepted import format helm-json dayhand-json still accepted backend/cli/helm.mjs (npx helm) dayhand removed These stay as they were, because they are stored in data or in tokens already given out: the issuer of the local backend and the user ids made from it, the JWT audience helm, the device and OAuth token prefixes (helmdev_, helmat_, helmrt_…), the calendar UIDs …@helm (a new suffix would duplicate every event in Google and Apple Calendar), the Android app's storage names and Kotlin package dev.helm.planner. The GitHub repository is now maiarowsky/dayhand; GitHub redirects the old maiarowsky/helm addresses, including git clone and existing checkouts."},{"p":"Renamed from Helm","t":"Known risk: another DayHand","u":"rename.html#known-risk-another-dayhand","x":"There is an unrelated to-do app called DayHand for iPhone, iPad and Mac (amouraux/DayHand, in public TestFlight beta in October 2026). App Store names are unique, so if it gets there first, this project's Apple apps would be listed as \"Dayhand Planner\". The CLI, npm, Homebrew and Android are not affected."}];
